      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS.</head>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS.</p>
        <p>THE EDITOR of" The Examiner" invites corre spondence on all subjects of public interest.</p>
        <p>Lrrnas should be brief and pointed. Their object must be to impart or seek information. not abuse.</p>
        <p>LETTERS must in all cases be accompanied by the</p>
        <p>name and address of the writer. COMMUNICATIONS received early in the week— except urgent matters—can always be given the best attention.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>MR. C. E. PARKER AND WESLEYAN METHODISM.</head>
        <p>MR. C. E. PARKER AND WESLEYAN METHODISM.</p>
        <p>Sir,—The highly interesting speech of Mr. Parker at the recent Synod meetings, and re- ported in your last issue, calls for some observa- tions.</p>
        <p>Is it a fact that Methodists to-day are impres- sing nobody except themselves? Are we sqiiply lulling ourselves to sleep, careless of progress, and untouched by the movements which are re- ceiving sympathetic attention from all serious of our time—movements calculated to _ate the obvious disabilities under which so many suffer? We trust this is not entirely true. Surely Methodists have had some small share in .the battle for public control of monopolies, for instance, now happily fought and won—to I say nothing of the Free Trade issue, likewise happily fought and won; and also other triumphs now pending—not officially, of course, but we have been sound on these questions. Over the trend of future legislation some of us, perhaps, are not quite so enthusiastic, believing that the suggested remedies for social ills may gravely interfere with personal liberty, and are of doubtful moral value. It must be remembered, however, that we are Bible men, first and last. We cannot set our- selves up as counsellors or dictators, as we have nothing to do with a State religion. We are free. We simply exist as a small body of men and womin in touch with life, yet out of touch with life, and so long as the nation decline to consider the claims of God and the soul we shall always occupy this peculiar position. LATCHFORD WESLEYAN.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>CHILDREN AND KINEMATOGRAPHS.</head>
        <p>CHILDREN AND KINEMATOGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>Sir,—With reference to the debate on Monday night about healthy children attending kine- matograph exhibitions, Mr. Upson was perfectly right in saying that even if Warrington adopted the proposals advocated by the Medical Inspec- tion Sub-committee the town would not be able to take credit to itself for being the first to move in this matter.</p>
        <p>Liverpool, Manchester and Blackburn have already taken steps in this direction, some of them considerably more drastic than those we now advocate, but I carefully refrained from mentioning what was being done in other parts of this country, because I was desirous that the Warrington Education Committee should settle this matter on its own merits, and not be in- fluenced by what had been done by other authorities.</p>
        <p>Personally, I should like to see Warrington follow the example of a large town in another country, where no child is allowed to go to picture shows at all whilst attending what cor- responds to our elementary schools; but in return I would have special exhibitions, for the children, of films of real educational value, of which there are hundreds in existence to-day. As regards unhealthy children attending these picture palaces there is surely here no case to answer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>HOLMAN KINGDON, Chairman Medical Inspection Sub-committee. Bank Quay, Warrington, May 21st, 1914.</head>
        <p>HOLMAN KINGDON, Chairman Medical Inspection Sub-committee. Bank Quay, Warrington, May 21st, 1914.</p>
        <p>LETTERS BY AN IRlSHMAN—continued.</p>
        <p>The nation was as passive as clay in the hands of the potter, and it is a circuifistance of</p>
        <p>peculiar aggravaticn that a large part of the legislation I have recounted was a distinct vio- lation of a solemn treaty. he commercial legislation which ruined Irish industry, the con- fiscation of Irish land which demoralised and</p>
        <p>impoverished the nation, were all directly due to the English Government and the English Parliament."</p>
        <p>Mr. Fronde says: "England governed for what she deemed her own interest, making her calculation on the gross balance of her trade ledgers, and leaving her moral obligations to accumulate, as if right or wrong had been blotted out of the Statute Book of the universe. The English deliberately determined to keep Ireland poor and miserable as the readiest means to prevent it being troublesome. They destroyed Irish trade and shipping by naviga- tion laws. They extinguished Irish manufac- tures by differential duties. They laid disabili- ties even on its wretched agriculture, for fear that Irish importations might injure the Eng- lish trade." Thus the story of English government of Ire- land in the 18th century was the suppression of indugtries, the hampering of agriculture, the fostering of religious differences, and the denial of education to the inferior caste. A GLEAM OF SUNSHINE. It seemed for a while as though the period of penal laws had been only that darkest hour before the dawn—since, out of oppression, freedom was born. When an Irish Parliament was given full powers, its use of them exceeded all anticipation. Lord Clare made this admis- sion in 1798, concerning the system which he was fated to destroy "There is not a nation on the face of the habitable globe which has advanced in cultiva- tion, in manufactures, with the same rapidity in the same period as Ireland." Judge Jebb, another distinguished man, wrote In the course of 15 years our commerce, our agriculture and our manufactures have swelled to an amount that the most sanguine friends of Ireland could not have dared to prognosticate." Lord Plunkett in 1799 said: "Ireland's revenues, her trade, her manufactures, had thriven beyond the home or example of any other country of her extent within these few years before the Union, advancing with a rapidity astonishing even to herself." The bankers of Dublin held a meeting on at which they passed the Great B fpoellcoevtnegrrleBstoillU 79tlion8; "Resolved, that since the renunciation of the power of ritain in eminently I- -1782 to legislate this f kingdom o rlre l Ireland, h , theee commerce prosperity .</p>
        <p>_ creased." Lecky, the Unionist historian, writes: "From the concession of Free Trade in 1779 to the Re- bellion in 1798, the national progress of Ire- land was rapid and uninterrupted. In ten years from 1782 the exports more than trebled." All this might have extended and augmented under the natural development of freedom. All this was destroyed because England would not allow Irishmen to trust their own fellow citi- zens with equal rights. The recall of Fitz- william was followed by measures of most brutal coercion; the country was kicked into rebel- lion, and the advocates of Union got the excuse they wanted to suppress the liberties of Ire- land.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>J. S. GARLAND,</head>
        <p>J. S. GARLAND,</p>
        <p>51, 53 &amp; 55, Bridge Street,</p>
        <p>Warrington.</p>
        <p>'Phone No. 79.</p>
        <p>Lawn Mowers Ground by special machinery.</p>
        <p>A Few Second-hand Mowers for Sale.</p>
        <p>LEITERS BY AN IRISHMAN.</p>
        <p>Survey of the Facts Governing the Demand for Home Rule.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>IV.-OPINIONS ON THE PERIOD OF PENAL LAWS AND GRATTAN'S PARLIAMENT.</head>
        <p>IV.-OPINIONS ON THE PERIOD OF PENAL LAWS AND GRATTAN'S PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>A marked feature of English government in Ireland has been the constant recurrence of famine. It was artificially produced by mili- tary means in the reign of Elizabeth, but in the 18th century it resulted quite naturally from the conditions that had been established. Again I take some illustrative quotations, brought to- gether by Mr. J. A. Fox in his book "Why Ire- land wants Home Rule."</p>
        <p>"The Protestant Bishop Nicholson, an Eng- lishman, in 1718 writes: Never did I behold even in Picady, Westphalia, or Scotland, such dismal marks of hunger and want as appeared in the countenances of most of the poor creatures I met on the road.' One of his horses, accidentally killed during his travels on an occasion, was surrounded by fifty or sixty famished cottagers, struggling desperately to obtain a morsel of flesh for themselves and their children.' " " In 1727, Boulter, Protestant Archbishop of Armagh and Primate of Ireland, wrote to the Duke of Newcastle that since his arrival in that country the famine had not ceased among the poor people. The dearness of corn in the pre- ceding year was such that thousands of families were obliged to quit their dwellings to seek means of life elsewhere, and many hundreds perished." "Dr. Sheridan, in 1728, declares that the poor are sunk to the lowest degree of misery and poverty—their houses their dung- hills, their food the blood of their cattle or the herbs of the fields '—' Kerry cows, in fact, know I no Sundays.'" In 1734 the Protestant Bishop of Cloyne, Rev. Berkeley, asked these questions in the " Querient " (Q. 132.) "Is there on the face of the earth any Christian and civilised people so destitute of everything as the mass of the people in Ire- land?"</p>
        <p>(Q. 172.) "Can it be said that the quantities of beef, of butter, of wool, and of leather, ex- ported from this island are the superfluity of the country, when so great a number of in- habitants are naked and starving?" The year 1741 was called;' the year of slaugh- ter, of death and desolathin." The cemeteries were too small for the burial of those who died on the roadside, or those whose bodies had to be sought for in their abandoned homes. Bishop Berkeley says: "I have seen the labourer en- deavouring to work at his spade, but fainting from want of food and forced to quit it. I have seen the aged father eating grass like a beast, and in the anguish of his soul wishing for his dissolution. I have seen the helpless orphan exposed on the dunghill and none to take him in for fear of infection, and I have seen the hungry infant sucking at the breast of the already expired parent." On the 16th December, 1778, in the House of Commons, Lord Nugent described the Irish people "as suffering all the destitution and dis- tress which it was possible for human nature to endure. Nine-tenths of the people earned no more than 4d. a day. In summer their whole food consisted only of potatoes and water." Speaking in the course of a debate in the House of Commons in 1787, the Attorney- General, Mr. Fitzgibbon, said "I am well acquainted with the province of Munster, and I know that it is impossible for human wretchedness to exceed that of the miserable peasantry of that province. I know that the unhappy tenantry are g:•ound to powder by relentless landlords. I know that far from being able to give the clergy their just dues (Protestant tithes), they have not food or raiment for themselves; the landlord giasps the whole. The poor people of Munster live in a more abject state of poverty than human nature can be supposed able to bear; their miseries sire intolerable."</p>
        <p>In short, the majority of the population lived always on the verge of famine, and yet the country was as fertile as it is to-day. Arthur Young, who travelled through it in 1768, says of Limerick and Tipperary: "It is the richest soil I ever saw, and Meath and West Meath are not less rich."</p>
        <p>As for the people, Lord Sheffield wrote:— "The Irish people are not naturally lazy; they are, on the contrary, of an active nature, capable of the greatest exertions, and of as good a disposition as any nation in the same state of improvement; but that men who have very little to do, should appear to do little, is not strange."</p>
        <p>DEPRESSED BELOW THE SAVAGE STATE. Yet the condition of this race was. thus described by Benjamin Franklin. in 1772:—. " The bulk of the people are tenants, 'ex- tremely poor, living in the most sordid wretchedness, in dirty hovels of mud and straw, and clothed only in rags. • . . Had I never been in the American colonies, but were to form my judgment of civil society by what I have lately seen I should never _advise a nation of savages to admit of civilisation, for I assure you that in the possession and enjoyment of various comforts of life, compared to these people, every Indian is a gentleman, and the effect of this kind of civil society seems to be the depressing multitudes below the savage state that a few may be raised above it." . .</p>
        <p>This state of things was inevitable where law, custom and public opinion all allowed the owner of the land to raise the rent to the utter- most which he could extort, and where no other means of livelihood but the land offered itself. The reason why there was no alternative is to be found in the Government of England. Every possible Irish industry which could compete with the smallest English interest was not merely discouraged, but actually prohibited by law. Stafford, in the reign of Charles 1., first laid down the policy of retaining Ireland in dependence. Being then Lord-Lieutenant, he wrote to the King in 1634 "I am of opinion that all wisdom advises to keep this kingdom as much subordinate and dependent upon England as is possible, and holding them from the manufacture of wool, and then enforcing them to fetch theil• cloth- ing from thence, and to take their salt from the King, how can they depart from us without nakedness and beggary?" been k ol killed dlr or el It was only, however, after the Williamite abnadd--tcahkieenf s e ariVi wars, when the main part of the fighting popu- lationlike—hadcemaebil alike—had in Con- tinental armies, that this policy was given full effect. Mr.•Lecky writes : " No country ever exercised a more complete control over the destinies of another than did England over those of Ireland, for three-quar- ters of a century after the Revolution. No serious resistance of any kind was attempted.</p>
        <p>Continued in preceding column.</p>
      </div>
