      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY 23, 1914. THE LITTLE MOUSE WITH THE POOR CHANCE.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY 23, 1914. THE LITTLE MOUSE WITH THE POOR CHANCE.</p>
        <p>. AITING. This has _le first two of the three Parliament Bills—the Welsh Church Bill and the Irish Home Rule Bill. Monday-and Tu. lay were given up to the third reading debate on the Welsh Church Bill, which was carried by a majority of seventy-seven, and .is now, consequently, assured of a, safe Passage to the Statute-book, whatever the House of Lords may do. On Wednesday the 'financial resolutions to the Home Rule Bill were taken, and on Thursday began the third reading debate, which will be continued on Monday and Tuesday of next week.</p>
        <p>1,245 members; life, 22; honorary, 125; ordinary, 1,098; total, 1,245. The membership in 1912-13 wal3 1,201, giving an 'increase of 44.</p>
        <p>To Help the Injured. "The members of the Ambulance Corps are in attendance each week, and their valuable services (which are rendered voluntary) are ap- preciated by the players. The first aid ren- dered by them is of the utmost importance, and their work has on many occasions been highly spoken of by the doctors called in for assist- ance.</p>
        <p>Northern Union Retrospect. " Generally, the season just closed has been the most successful on record. From all clubs in membership with the Northern Rugby Foot- ball Union come optimistic reports of increased interest and larger 'gates,' and never in the history of the Union were the prospects brighter.</p>
        <p>'Efforts are being made by every club to im- prove the efficiency of the players, so that the finer points of the game may be better achieved. That, from the spectators' point of view, is essential; and that it is of considerable im- portance is shown by the attendance at the matches where the ball is thrown about for some useful purpose.</p>
