      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>WARRINGTON CLUB'S PROFITS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON CLUB'S PROFITS.</p>
        <p>A Satisfactory Account of the Year's Working.</p>
        <p>BALANCE OF £477.</p>
        <p>A satisfactory report has been prepared for presentation to the annual meeting of the members of the Warrington N.U. Football Club, to be held on Thursday. The report cer- tainly reflects the greatest credit upon the com- mittee and officials, and from the financial standpoint it is excellent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>TREASURER'S REPORT.</head>
        <p>TREASURER'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>The Treasurer, Mr. T. Pemberton, in his report states :</p>
        <p>"I am pleased to report that the past season has been a financial success. After allowing ample depreciation and providing a sum of over £BO for the renewal of part of the fencing on the westerly and southerly sides of the ground, there remains a profit of £447 15s. 2d. "The number of persons paying for admis- sion to the ground, inclusive of the benefit match to George Dickenson, was 144,177, the net gate money being ,R 2,527 19s. 11d., as against .t 2,269 18s. Bd. in season 1912-13. Subscriptions show an increase of £33 7s.</p>
        <p>_ "On the expenditure side repairs are more, owing to the cost of the fencing already men- tioned. Pares, refreshments, instrance, and medical charges are less. The amount spent on transfer fees and expenses searching for new Players was £509 7s. id.; last year, £445 15s. lid. "Considerable interest was taken in the Works Competition, the money received amounting to .t 123 2s. 2d., as against £7l Bs. 7d. in respect of the previous competition. It is, however, impossible at the present moment, owing to the large number of accidents, to state the exact amount which can be devoted to charities."</p>
        <p>SECRETARY'S REPORT. The 111-effects of the Strike. Mr. T. P. Bradshaw, the hon. secretary, gives a lucid account of the year's doings in his report. Dealing with the strike in January, he Says:</p>
        <p>"It is patent to everyone that the strike had a material effect on the players during the rest of the season, and the unanimity necessary for the well-being and success of the club was not as one would desire, with the result that there was a considerable falling off in the Play gener- ally, and the position in the League table suffered in consequence. It may be interesting to note that this is the second strike in the his- tory of the club, the previous one being in the season 1895-6.</p>
        <p>" The play of the back division has been very unsatisfactory, especially at centre, and every effort has been made by the comMittee to strengthen that part of the team, but without success. Visits have been made' to many Rugby Union games, but the right material for North- ern Union football has not been forthcoming. The forwards, the mainstay of the team, have played remarkably well during the whole of the season, but it could not be expected of them to win every match. It is evident, even to the most inexperienced mind, that the team needs strengthening, and further efforts are being made to get together a side worthy of the name of the club.</p>
        <p>School Boys' Competition. "The above League has again had a very •successful season, seven teams from schools in the town competing, the winners being St. James'. The attendance at the semi-final and final ties of the South-West Lancashire Cup Competition exceeded all expectations, and the Warrington Boys are to be congratulated upon being the first to win the trophy. They gave a really bright display, and the way they opened out the game showed that they knew what to do with the ball. It was quite evident that they had been well coached. As most of the be debarred from taking further part acompetition, the committee think that in boys the h mey the present is an opportune time to urge for- warl the claims of an Intermediate League. It would be a pity to neglect such promising talent, and they cordially invite the co-opera- tion of the School Boys' Committee in the formation of a suitable scheme. The committee wish to take this opportunity of thanking the secretary, Mr. S. J. Price, and the other officers and committee of the 'League for their services during the past few years. " During the season four players were sent off the field for misconduct, three being suspended and one cautioned. No complaints have been made, or fines imposed during the season for late starts. Three players received county honours during the season. " Owing to the resignation of Mr. Ashton as the club's representative on the Committee of. the Northern Rugby Union, the Chairman of the club (Councillor Osborne) was nominated and elected in his place, and the thanks of the members are due to him for the zeal he has shown on that body, and also on the Committee of the Lancashire County Union:</p>
        <p>'The membership of the club is composed of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>ASSOCIMOti.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIMOti.</p>
        <p>Looking Back on an Encouraging</p>
        <p>Season,</p>
        <p>LEAGUE CHAMPIONS,</p>
        <p>The Warrington and District Association League, a body that is making a meritorious effort to place the Association game on. a better basis in Warrington, wound up a very success- ful season on Saturday by means of a hotpot supper at the Lion Hotel, to which over 50 sat down, and at which the Mayor (Councillor Peter Peacock) presented the Dodds Challenge Shield and medals to the Great Sankey team, who were declared champions of the League, First Division, having fulfilled their fixtures without suffering defeat and having headed their division with 25 points. They scored 62 , goals against six goals scored by their oppo- nents. The following players received their medals: E. Glover (captain), R. Stephens, H. Ditchfield, A. Gardner, H. Payne, E. Darling- ton, T. Illidge, J. Ditchfield, S. Poulter, A. Houghton and J. Bond. The members of the team bought a medal by subscription for their hon. secretary, Mr. E. T. Clarke, in recognition of his valuable services.</p>
        <p>The Warrington EXce-lsior team were declared winners of the Second Division, having fulfilled their fixture list without suffering defeat, and obtaining 25 points. They scored 59 goals, against nine goals scored by their opponents. The following players received their medals: J. Rigby (captain), T. O'Neil, W. Leah, R. Spencer, P. Boyd, S. Lloyd, H. Walker, P. Pritchard, G. H. Cox, J. Morris and A. Welsby. The hon. secretary, Mr. R. Lawton, also received a medal for his services rendered to the club. Mr. Glover and Mr. Rigby, the captains of the respective winning clubs, responded in suit- able terms, and declared, on behalf of the players, their appreciation of the medals which they had received.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Smith, secretary of the Orford St. John's Club, received the medals of the War- rington and District Junior Football Medal Competition on behalf of the Orford St. John's team, who proved successful in defeating the St. Barnabas' team in the final. The St. John's team went through the competition and scored 12 goals against two.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. B. Starkie presided over an attend- ance which included Mr. Lowe, vice-president of the Great Sankey P.C.; Mr. J. Wright, vice- chairman of the League; Mr. R. G. Brandreth, secretary; Mr. S. Essenhigh, assistant secre- tary; and Mr. Garner, treasurer; Councillor George Archer and others. After the presentation a vote of thanks was heartily accorded the Mayor, on the motion gf Councillor Archer, who said he would like to see the progress of the Association game in Warrington, seconded by Mr. Lowe.</p>
        <p>"ONLY A MAYOR'S. DUTY." The Mayor, in reply, said it was only a Mayor's duty to encourage everything which was for the benefit of the people of the town. He thought an Association such as that, which had been lying dormant for some considerable time, and was again becoming active, deserved encouragement; that was why he was present. From inquiries he had made lie found that the success of the League this year had primarily rested with the Secretary, Treasurer, and other officials, and when he looked at their Chairman and found he gave the cup, the impression was forced upon him that the League had some splendid men around it. (Hear, hear.) Warrington was noted for sport in all direc- tions. He did not know how many clubs— bowling, cricket and football, and other asso- ciations—he was connected with, and by whom he was reminded once a year that he was a vice- president. (Laughter.) He should like to have been present at the annual meeting of the War- rington Northern Union Club, but he would be amongst the Mayors of England who, at the time of the annual meeting, would, be on an international visit to Cologne and Brussels. He hoped their Association would prosper year by year, and that they would not allow it to die off again. (Applause.) •</p>
        <p>A BRIGHT PROSPECT'. Mr. Starkie, in proposing the health of the Warrington and District Association Football League, said the officials deserved every credit for getting it into working order. (Applause.) When anything had lain dormant for some time it was very hard' to get it to and he trusted the League's future would be better than the past. He would like to see a big addition to their Association, because, as they knew, pro- fessionalism was growing, whilst they wanted more amateurism. They did not want 26 play- ing and seven or eight thousand watching—they wanted everybody playing. If they desired that the nation of the future should not decline they must go in more for sport. (Hear, hear.) Mr. Brandreth, in reply, said nobody could look back on the season without feeling encour- aged in many ways. The League was run on proper business lines.</p>
        <p>Mr. Garner proposed, and Mr. Clarke sec- onded, a vote qf thanks to the artistes, Mr. Wrench replying. The Captain of the Sankey team (Mr. E. Glover) received the shield from the Mayor on behalf of the team, whilst Mr. J. R. Rigby re- ceived the cup on behalf of the Warrington Excelsior team. The members of each team re- ceived their medals, whilst honorary medals were given by the Warrington Excelsior Club to their team.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>School Boys to the Rescue.</head>
        <p>School Boys to the Rescue.</p>
        <p>" There is, however, a dearth of young players with sufficient experience and ability for first- class football, and those in close touch with the game have begun to realise the fact that it is necessary to cultivate 'the young idea.' With that object in view, the Committee of the Lan- cashire Football Union offered the South-West Lancashire League Cup for competition amongst the school boys in the district. It is desirable that Intermediate Leagues should be formed, so that when the promising boys leave school they may not be lost sight of, but may be en- abled to continue playing until they become old enough, and of sufficient experience and stamina, to participate in first-class football. From the boys' point of view, too, it is of con- siderable importance. It is hoped that some- thi4jg will be accomplished during the off season so that a League may be formed, and lir-work- ing order, by' September next.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>Teams' Performances.</head>
        <p>Teams' Performances.</p>
        <p>"The first team had played 34 League matches,, half of which were won, and occupy the 11th place in the N.U. League table, as compared with 18th last season. There were 280 points scored for, and 332 against them. The team finished the first half of the season with 137 points for, and 144 against them. "The 'A' team finished sixth in the Combina- tion table, as compared with 20th last season. Of 30 matches played, 19 were won, and alto- gether 319 points were scored for, and 200 against them. During the first half of the season three matches only were lost."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>ATTENDANCE OF COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>ATTENDANCE OF COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>_....__. _ There were 55 meetings held, and the com- mittee made the followfng attendances:— Messrs. T. P. Bradshaw (hon. secretary), 55; J. J. T. Sampson, ST; T. Barnes, 53; W. Hill, 53; J. H. Whitham, 51; T. Pem- berton (hon. treasurer), 50; T. Bunch, 49; F. Barber, 46; E. Osborne, 44.</p>
        <p>Presi- dent, Edmund Osborne; ELECTION OF OFFICERS. The following have been nominated: Pemberton; hon. auditors, W. Webster and G. Giffin. hon. treasurer, Thos.</p>
        <p>The following have been nominatea for the office of hon. secretary: T. P. Bradshaw and W. Mason.</p>
        <p>The following have been nominated for the committee : F. Barber, T. Barnes, jun., T. Bunch, W. Hill, J. T. Sampson, J. H. Whitriam, J. Winstonley (who retire but are eligible for election), J. F. Foster, W. Littlef air, W. Mason, J. Sudlow and A. Wright.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>PLAYERS' BENEFIT.</head>
        <p>PLAYERS' BENEFIT.</p>
        <p>The following special resolution will be pro- posed by C. F. Trow- and seconded by J. W. Plinston : "That a first team match be granted for the joint benefit of A. Naylor and E. Jor- dan."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>MEDICINE FOR TIIE MILLION. 175,000 bottles of Dr. Cassell's Tablets for one firm of Chemists.</head>
        <p>MEDICINE FOR TIIE MILLION. 175,000 bottles of Dr. Cassell's Tablets for one firm of Chemists.</p>
        <p>LARGEST ORDER EVER PLACED.</p>
        <p>- The proprietors of Dr. Cassell's Tablets have just received from Messrs. Boots, cash chemists, Nottingham, what is easily the largest single order ever placed for a proprietary medicine. The consignment will total no less than 175,000 bottles for distribution over Messrs. Boots' 550 branches. Large orders are also being con- stantly received from chemists in all parts of the country. Nothing but an assured demand would induce them to lay in such enormous stocks, and nothing but real merit could account for that demand. Dr. Cassell's Tablets are made from the formula of a noted specialist. They are widely advertised all over the world for all forms of Nerve Troubles, and functional derangements of the stomach and kidneys in either children or adults; and wonderful as the published testimony may seem it is true beyond any shadow of doubt. Any chemist in the country- can supply Dr. Cassell's Tablets.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>IN THE COURTS.</head>
        <p>IN THE COURTS.</p>
        <p>BOROUGH POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY. Before Messrs. R. T. Fairclough (in the chair), A. J. Jolley and T. Pemberton.</p>
        <p>Electrician in Trouble.—Walter Raymond, electrician, Toll House, Grappenhall, was charged with stealing from the bedroom at the Talbot Hotel two gold guards and other smaller articles, and also with 'stealing tools, valued 405., whilst ke was employed at the Empire Hall. —lt was stated that prisoner was employed at the Talbot Hotel doing repairs, and after he left the articles were missed. One of the chains was found in a tool box at his house, and he had given the other one to his sister.—Tools were missed from the Empire Hall whilst prisoner was working there, and these were found at his home.—A fine of 405., with an alternative of one month's imprisonment, was imposed in each case. ' 4</p>
        <p>I ngratitude.—Bertha Beesley, wife of a Pres- ton cab driver, was charged with stealing bed- clothes and other articles, valued 205., the pro- perty of Annie Oultram, King-street, Warring- ton.—Early in April prisoner left her husband and arsived in Warrington with another man. They took apartments at the house of Mrs. Oultram, .prisoner saying the man was her hus- band. The man left the woman stranded after a few days, but she remained at the house wad agreed to work for Mrs. Oultram in return for boaid and lodgings until the man should re- turn. On April 26th prisoner disappeared and the articles were misSed. She was traced to a Wigan factory, w'here, on being arrested, she endeavoured to hide pawn tickets relating to the missing property in a tea can.—Prisoner said she lad arranged to return the articles.— She was fined 205., or in default 14 days.</p>
        <p>A Sordid Story.—Agnes Noon, a single woman, lodging in Winwick-road, was sent to prison for three months for having neglected her two chil- dren, and also for having been drunk whilst in charge of a child under the age of seven.s—Mr. Browne prosecuted on behalf of the N.S.P.C.C., and told a sordid story of neglect. The woman had refused to go in the Workhouse and had taken one of her children who had been suffer- ing from pneumonia from that institution, against the directions of Inspector Wainwright, who said the boy should not be taken away until there was a proper home for him. She was seen in a drunken state with her two chil- dren by the Lord Rodney, and taken into cus- tody.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>MONDAY.</head>
        <p>MONDAY.</p>
        <p>Before Dr. Joseph (in the chair), Aldermen Smethurst, Bolton, and James Evans, and Messrs. S. Wright, W. Roberts, W. Furness, and G. Pilling.'</p>
        <p>Fought Like Lions.—Joseph Hopwood, Pig- hill, and John Rankin, 3, Cloth Hall-yard, were charged with fighting in Crown-street on Satur- day afternoon.—Detective Clough said he separ- ated them, but they began to fight again like lions. The trouble was caused through a penny bet.—Prisoners were fined 10s. and costs.</p>
        <p>An Unprofitable Customer.—Edward Foster, 2, Fothergill-street, was charged with on May 16, .stealing from Messrs. Woolworth's shop, 21, Sankey-street, four knives, three combs and a wash leather, value 2s. 6d.—The Chief Con- stable related that on Saturday night prisoner was seen to take the wash leather and put it into his pocket. The manager *as informed, and as the other articles were found in pris- oner's possession the police were called. Pris- -oner offered to pay for' the articles. If every- body who entered the shop did the same thing no firm Could stand up against such a practice. —Prisoner was fined 10s. and costs.</p>
        <p>" Cowardly Theft."—John Robert Entwistle, a labourer, giving an address at a lodging-house, Cloth Hall-yurd, was charged with stealing from a man's vest is. sd. in cash, the property of William Jones.—The Chief Constable character- ised the offence as a " cowardly theft." It ap- peared that the prosecutor, who is deaf and dumb, was on Saturday night sleeping in a room at the lodging-house, which was occupied by two other men, one of whom was the pris- oner. About midnight prosecutor had occa- sion to leave the room, and whilst he was away prisoner took Jones' Waistcoat from underneath the pillow and after taking the money from the pockets threw the Waistcoat through the win- dow, later suggesting to the other occupant of the room that prosecutor should also be thrown through the window. Early the following morn- ing Jones, meeting a policeman, made signs to the effect that money had been taken from him, and after inquiries prisoner was arrested, when amongst other miiney found in his possession was a shilling in the lining of his cap.—Pris- oner was sent to gaol for a month with hard labour.</p>
        <p>In a Railway Carriage.—Five men from the Manchester district were charged with playing a game of chance in a railway carriage on the Cheshire Lines Railway between Liverpool and Warrington. on Saturday. They were John Hammond, bookmaker, The Fox, ,Bury-street, Salford; Alfred Mason, bookmaker's clerk, 30, Bury-street, Salford; Joseph Lever, hawker, 76, Crown-street„, Hulme, Manchester; John Bloor, auctioneer's clerk, 30, River-street, Hulme; and James Sutcliffe, The Fox, Bury-street, Salford. The men pleaded guilty.—The Chief Constable stated that the party boarded the 6.30 train at Liverpool on Saturday evening, and in the same compartment were some young men from War- rington, who were returning from a bowling match. Accused commenced playing the three- card trick, and as it appeared easy the War- rington men tried their luck, with the result that they lost .27. The Warrington men could smell a rat, and when they intended to get out of the train at Warrington to inform the police, one of the prisoners tried to prevent them, and .22 was given to each of two local men, the pris- oners saying they had only won .24. Several of the prisoners had cards. He asked for the full penalty for first offenders to be imposed, as he had not then got a record of the prisoners be- yond the fact that some had been convicted pre- viously, and this they admitted.—Accused were each fined 40s. and costs or one month's hard labour.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY. Before Mr. J. W. Hayward (in the chair), Alderman Smethurst, and Messrs. W. T. Williams and C. Jackson Holmes.</p>
        <p>Driving Dangerously.—John James Long- shaw Lowe, greengrocer, 69, Winwick-road, was summoned for driving a horse and cart to the danger of the public in Winwick-road.—lt was stated that he was driving at the rate of 10 miles per hour, and by keeping in the middle of the street prevented a char-a-bane from pro- ceeding. There was a great scene of excite- ment.—Defendant : The horse wai- wicked, and I could not use the whip.—A fine of 10s. and costs was imposed.</p>
        <p>Theft of Rings._ A domestic servant, aged 15, was charged on remand with the theft from her mistress of iwo gold rings. The girl took the rings from a dressing table, and then asked permission to go to the doctor for some medicine for her father. She, however, proceeded to the pawnbroker's, where she disposed of the stolen property. She had previously appeared before the Court, and she was sent to the Exeter Re- formatory School until she attains the age of 19 years, the father being ordered to pay is. per week towards her maintenance.</p>
      </div>
