        <p>and rendered unconscious. She was now recovering.—Prisoner was remanded to the ton Petty Sessions.</p>
        <p>Temper and Jealousy.—Ernest Green, 61, thorne Villas, Station-road, Great Sanke,V,„ summoned for neglecting his wife and chiln and Mrs. Green asked for a separation era,er The complainant said they had been nia—i twelve years, and there were three children. ' had not received any money from her husb; for three weeks, and she left him en the May. Her husband worked for the Inte' tional Tract Society, and earned about 305' week. She asked for an allowance of 155,4. week.—Defendant said he wanted a separ", order.—Mr. Laws, police court missioner. the case had been going on for a long tirneo a.. mutual agreement had been arrived at sj time ago. The parties had come together.le incompatibility of temper and jealousy, with other things, were responsible for s parties living a cat and dog life. There %die chance of them coming together.—lnsPecp Wainwright said that on an average defell had only given his wife Is. per day from 20th to May 6th. He had said he would C"oi housekeeping, and since May 6th lie beef&amp; Spent threepence in food in The house.— ant said that when he bought things bigli expected the same amount of money fronor, as if he had not bought anything.—A3 with an allowance of 15s. per week was 01°</p>
