      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>Boy's Fatal Ascension. Day Expedition.</head>
        <p>Boy's Fatal Ascension. Day Expedition.</p>
        <p>Mr. P. A. Jones, deputy coroner, coll,scis.uv7af an inquest on Thursday on Austin Conr°er. son of Martin Conroy, commercial trace.'elell' Allen-street, who was drowned in the St. e and Sankey Canal about noon the sago Emma Conroy, mother of deceased, 53'„ittl;' last saw her son alive prior to his de,P:we.„' for church shortly before 10 o'clock. 11`„for alone from the house, but had done After leaving church he went fishing 'nlo canal. He had said earlier in the mor1h1ns1,01; be would do so, but she had forbiddellidoj Soon after 12 o'clock two little boys carae neighbour and told her he was drowned etperj been conveyed to the Infirmary. She well:d and identified some of his clothing, since seen the body. bemll William Bate, of Hulme Lock, sado working about 300 yards from Bewseo 0!;0, Bridge, when some boys came and told 11./dge a boy was.drowned. He went to the VI f mediately, but could not see any sign%o Sr.(ll body. Presently a woman brought salt ho piing irons, and he then recovered t"' from the canal. _</p>
        <p>A verdict of "Accidentally drowned turned, the jury passing a vote of with the parents.</p>
        <p>COSTLY CHESHIRE ROADS'</p>
