      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <p>p</p>
        <p>be pure, for, immediately it is allowed to be- come at all tainted, ill-health and disease ensue. It is, unfortunately, impossible to state with any degree of exactitude the number of fowls that a certain plot of land will accommodate— there are so many factors ,that must be taken into consideration. The number depends, for one thing, upon the varieties kept, some being so much more active- than others. It depends, too, upon the time of year. During the spring and early summer, when there is plenty of growth in the soil, the number may be consider- ably greater than during the dead time of year —the autumn and winter, Then, again, the number depends upon the nature of the land, a light, porous soil being able to support many more birds than one consisting of clay. For the guidance, however, of the man with limited space at his command, it may be stated that for small laying varieties the space per bird should not be less than three square yards of gravel run, and, say, thirty square yards should be the plot of grass. If the breed or breeds kept be of the larger type, not more than ten birds should be maintained on a piece of ground capable of safely carrying twenty non-sitters. On the whole, the margin of safety in the stock- ing of land can only be satisfactorily determined by the poultry-keeper's own observations as to the state of health of his fowls and the condi- tion of the soil.</p>
        <p>Eggs, Poultry, etc.</p>
        <p>BOY'S FATAL FLOWER HUNT.</p>
        <p>Brave Labourer's Attempt at Rescue.</p>
        <p>A fatality marred a pleasant afternoon in the Bank Quay district on Sunday, when little John Thornley, son of Alfred and Mary Jane Thorn- ley, 8, Lloyd-street, was drowned in the canal. Full of life, the little fellow left home with his brother Alfred, aged ten, about 3.45 p.m., and at 4.30 the parents received news that the boy had been drowned. At the inquest on Monday Clement Griffiths, forge labourer, 29, Leicester-street, said that he was walking along the banks of the canal, and when about 20 yards from Buttermilk Bridge he heard a boy shout "My little brother has fallen into the water." He ran towards the spot, and at the same time he saw the boy struggling in the water, almost in the centre of the canal. He (witness) took off his coat and vest and dived in. He got hold of the boy, who, however, got hold of his shirt sleeve and so impeded him. He (wit- ness) then, seemed to get into difficulties, and owing to the weight of water in his trousers he had to release his hold of the boy to save him- self as he was not a good swimmer. By this time another man had dived in, but before he could get hold of the bpy he had disappeared. BODY RECOVERED. Frederick William Dutton, labourer, 35, Hes- keth-street, said he obtained grappling irons. and after ten minutes recovered the body ten yards from the bridge, where the water was six or seven feet deep. Sydney Packer, 46, Plumpton-street, said he assisted in dragging the canal, and also, along with A. S. Whitby, tried artificial respiration for 45 minutes. The brother of deceased said they were pick- ing flowers when his brother slipped in the water. A verdict of "Accidental death" was returned and the jury commented upon the bravery of -Griffiths. ' The Coroner said he would bring the facts of the case before the Royal Humane Society.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY 23, 1914. FARM AND GARDEN.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY 23, 1914. FARM AND GARDEN.</p>
        <p>Cheshire County Council for about .02,000) and greatly extended.</p>
        <p>Government Assistance.</p>
        <p>The Board of Agriculture, it is stated, will give a capital grant of 75 per cent, for the pur- chase of the site on which the dairy block, educational buildings, and hostel are placed, and for the erection of the necessary buildings. To- wards the cost of purchasing the Farm Institute working it, and to the maintenance of the.lnsti- Plc Insti- tute assistance w ill be given annually, and will consist of payments at the rate of from 71 to 75 per cent. on the loss of each year's working of the farm, the payment and repayment of any loans borrowed for this purpose being included in the farm accounts.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>SWINE FEVER.</head>
        <p>SWINE FEVER.</p>
        <p>Swine fever is still prevalent in many parts of the country, thirty counties having contri- buted to the total of 180 outbreaks in the week ended May 9, which involved the slaughter of 1,240 pigs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>CROP PROSPECTS IN THE BALANCE.</head>
        <p>CROP PROSPECTS IN THE BALANCE.</p>
        <p>The condition of the crops reflects the severe strain to which farmers and gardeners have been subjected by the long spell of easterly winds, says " The Times," but the position .15 not without hope, except in those fertile dis- tricts in the Midlands and the basin of .the Humber, where many of the crops of intensive culture have suffered irretrievable injury. Early potatoes in many other parts have re- ceived a bad check, but the result Ts not likely to be more than delay in digging. This, how- ever, is 'serious, as after the first few weeks prices tumble down rapidly to the normal level. Wheat again promises to be the best of the corn crops. There are blemishes, such as thin- neSs of plant and inequality of growth, that are difficult to reconcile with the fine seeding season and the open winter, but on the whole there should be a very fair harvest. The prospect for the clovers and grasses is scarcely more cheerful than was the case last year. Most of the mange's are in, and in the North, where they can be sown several weeks earlier than in the warmer South, the sowing of swedes is well forward.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>SCARCITY OF PIGS.</head>
        <p>THE SCARCITY OF PIGS.</p>
        <p>It is not .easy to reconcile- the ;;;;te of the markets with the outcry of scarcity of pigs, says "The Times." It might be expected that high prices would be an accompaniment or a result of deficiency in the supply, but although trade has been brisk at times, the general experience of sellers has not been indicative of anything abnormal in• the number of animals available. The official reports upon trade at the metropoli- tan and provincial markets describe the demand for fat pigs as slow, with a downward tendency in prices. It is significant that the trade for breeding sows and young pigs is decidedly better, a fact which suggests that responsibility for the stagnant condition of the pig-raising industry does not lie with the producer. The farmer as a rule is eager to turn to account any source of revenue, and the breeding and feed- ing of pigs would appeal to him if he could feel that a moderate profit could be obtained.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>ADVANCE IN AGRICULTURE STUDY.</head>
        <p>ADVANCE IN AGRICULTURE STUDY.</p>
        <p>New laboratories to cost £12,000 are to be erected at the Rothamstead Agricultural Ex- perimental Station, Harpenden, in celebration of the Lawes and Gilbert centenary. They have become necessary through the general advance of agricultural education in this country, and the demand for exact knowledge of soil and plant problems. There will be provided special rooms for acid digestions, distillations, drying, photographing, the preparation of soil for pot experiments and investigations dealing with crops; a library dealing with agriculture, chemical laboratory for special investigations and another laboratory, probably the first of its kind in England, for the study of soil pro- tozoa. The committee, who are asking for the .C 750 still required, state that Rothamstead, which is the oldest agricultural station in the world, will also become one of the best equipped.</p>
        <p>THE NEW MILK BILL. Registration and Inspection Regulations.</p>
        <p>The new Milk Bill backed by Mr. Herbert Samuel, Mr. Burns, Mr. Runciman, and Mr. Herbert Lewis, just issued, has the following provisions :</p>
        <p>The sale of milk from any cow which has given tuberculous milk to the knowledge of the indivi- dual selling, or which is suffering from emacia- tion due to tuberculosis or from tuberculous udder or acute inflammation of the udder, or any disease liable to infect or contaminate the milk, is an offence.</p>
        <p>The Local Government Board may make general and special orders for the registration of dairies; the inspection of dairies and persons employed "in or about" them; the prevention of the sale of dirty or contaminated milk; the prohibition of the addition of colouring matter, or of adulteration with water or skimmed or separated milk to milk for sale for human con- sumption; the regulation of the cooling and conveyance of milk; the labelling of churns, &amp;C. These orders " shall " be made by the Local Government Board with the concurrence of the Board of Agriculture, and shall have effect as if enacted in this Act.</p>
        <p>On the report of the medical. officer of health the " responsible authority" shall have power to stop the supplies of milk from a suspected dairy, but in such case the dairyman is to be relieved of responsibility for breach of contract, if the breach is due to the Order.</p>
        <p>_ Cattle in suspected dairies may be inspected, and it shall be within the power of the Local Government Board and the medical officer of health, or any person authorised in writing by them, to take samples of milk at any time before it is delivered to the consumer. A sanitary authority may establish and main- tain depots for the sale of milk specially pre- pared for consumption by infants under two years of age, and provide such laboratories, &amp;c., as may be necessary. In case a local authority neglect to fulfil any of their duties under this Act they may be compelled by mandamus to act, and a district council may be superseded by the county council.</p>
        <p>THE NEW CHESHIRE FARM INSTITUTE.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Cheshire Education com- mittee at Crewe on Monday, Dr. Hodgson presid- ing, the scheme of agricultural education for the year was considered. The Dairy Institu- tion at Worleston is to be given up, and the work which has for many years been carried on there will be transferred to the new Form Institute at Henhull (recently purchased by the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>CHESHIRE SCHOOL HOLIDAYS.</head>
        <p>CHESHIRE SCHOOL HOLIDAYS.</p>
        <p>The Cheshire Education Committee on Mon- day decided to abolish holidays for school chil- dren, as a reward for good attendance, on the ground that they have in the main ceased to achieve the object for which they were estab- lished—namely, the improvement of attendance in elementary schools. The abolition takes effect from August next.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>MEANING OF "NERVES."</head>
        <p>THE MEANING OF "NERVES."</p>
        <p>Nerve are fibres running from the brain to all parts of the body, like the wires of a tele- phone exchange. Some of them give us the sense of touch and feeling; othereconvey rnes- sages to the brain and the will to our muscles and enable us to move. When we are "nervous" or have "an attack of nerves," it means that the brain and spine are in some way out of order. Either the nerves are too sensitive, so that we get neuralgic pains, neurasthenia, head- ache, sciatica, and so on, or else they do not do the will of the brain, and we get trembling hands or limbs and perhaps St. Vitus' Dance. The nerves and spine are fed and kept in health by the blood. If the blood is thin and poor, nervous weaknesses, diseases and pains follow. We need a nerve-tonic to feed up the blood so that it, in turn, may feed nerve, spine and brain. The medicine which goes direct to the blood, enriching and purifying it, is Dr. William's' Pink Pills. You cannot take them without feel- ing the uplift which they give. Your appetite improves, your brain is clearer, you feel full of new life. All dealers sell Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People or you can send postal order 2s. 9d. for one box (13s. 9d. for six boxes) to Dr. Williams' Medicine Co., 46, Holborn-viaduct, London, no charge for postage. In any case write to Post Dept., 46, Holborn-viaduct, Lon- don, for a book on Home Treatment of the Nerves—it is free. A post-card will do.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>IN THE GARDEN.</head>
        <p>IN THE GARDEN.</p>
        <p>Sow Beet in drills 11 inches deep and 12 inches apart..</p>
        <p>Plant out sprouted tubers of early Potatoes six inches deep and two feet apart in the rows. Be careful to cover the tubers with fine soil, and them for protection. as soon as they push through draw soil over</p>
        <p>. Sow dwarf Beans in double rows, when the foliage holds up better. A good way of getting i a catch crop of these Beans is to sow them in between Parsnips now growing, the foliage of h Bnnieollitsnbsheoi lnl, (1 i e open, ing a good and clean crop. Ite Pi iea may s gP advisablethu just yet to sow Scarlet ltu but an advance in time may be made by sowing in boxes for trans- planting. Sow Broccoli, Lettuce, Dwarf Peas, Radish; i t ii rr Spinach, Turnip, Cabbages, Brussels Sprouts, and Onions.</p>
        <p>ATTENTION TO WINDOW BOXES. The filling of a window bo4 with suitable occupants does not mean that the plants are going to make a good show without constant attention. Neglect in watering for even one day may do a lot of mischief. The boxes, it must be remembered, a most exposed position, catclhiingem.edU alchl7- in and sun, and consequently dry much more quickly than plants standing on the ground. An occasional stimulant will have beneficial results, but should be given after a good watering, when the manurial feed will be effective.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>SUMMER FLOWERS.</head>
        <p>THE SUMMER FLOWERS.</p>
        <p>In a few weeks gardens- should be really at their best, when it is a pleasure to view the effect of the hard work entailed in the making of a decent-sized garden. Summer-flowering plants, such as Pentstemons, Snaps; Chrysan- themums, Violas, and Pansies should now be got into position. Stocks, Asters, Salpiglossis, Nemesias, and other half-hardy subjects may be put out as soon as they are big enough. Hardy annuals can also be sown in the open, the rising temperature will make them germinate quickly and grow fast.. The following are suitable for this late sowing: The Nigellas (Love-in-a-Mist), Clarkias, Annual Chrysanthemums, Godetias, Nemophilas, Lavate a Malope, Linum, Nastur- tiums, Rhodanthea, Shirley Poppies. Primroses and Polyanthuses may be divided and planted in a shady place,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>STRAWBERRY HINTS.</head>
        <p>STRAWBERRY HINTS.</p>
        <p>1 The first flowers to open on strawberries are always the strongest, and if they escape the frost 1 are invariably followed by the largest and earliest fruit. Unfortunately, strawberry flowers are very easily injured, there being nothing to shield them from cold rain, sleet and frost, and very often many of the earliest blooms are irreparably injured. It therefore pays to pro- tect them, especially on early borders. Once the flowers have set—unless extra severe weather follows—the danger is past. Any kind of light shading would protect the blooms if fixed well above the strawberry plants in flower every night, and removed in the daytime. When light canvas was not to be had, we have frequently used long litter scattered loosely over the plants and found this answer the purpose.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>KEEP THE HOE GOING.</head>
        <p>KEEP THE HOE GOING.</p>
        <p>The various vegetable crops will now be com- ing on apace, so every means must be taken to keep them growing, and for this purpose timely hoeings and frequent stirring of the surface soil are the best. It is astonishing how quickly crops respond to such treatment. Hoeing not only assists growth, but also disturbs the various insect pests which attack the young vegetable crops. Weeds also must be kept down, as, be- sides choking up the crops, they so impoverish the soil that in many cases they fail to do any good. As soon as the lines of the various crops can be discerned and the ground is in a fit state for hoeing, the work should be gone into with a will, as, if this is done, the seedling weeds hardly visible to the naked eye will be destroyed. Any perennial rooting weeds should be removed and burned. , Groundsel is a terrible pest in the kitchen garden --hen allowed to gain a footing. Although only annual, the seeds will remain in the ground L years and a crop of seedlings will appear as s-on as the seeds are turned to the surface. Groundsel should never be allowed to remain on the ground after it has been hoed ' up, but should at once be removed and burned. Select, if possible, a dry day, and, if possible, a hot sunny day on which to go through the vari- ous crops. When hoeing do not merely loosen wmihr ndainl‘g..,when the surface, but use the draw hoe, working deeply into the soil, leavingi the weeds on the destroyed as str good ed surface. The Dutch hoe s a useful implement when properly handled for the destructionto nyl Fresh aekal surface rksflei onoaf ts ihe se ogniSor ground unign n stdho:iesuuewsaatler tlalsoparts be oy t s ar as anything for their destruction.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>PEACE SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>PEACE SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>A Year of Vigorous and Useful Work</p>
        <p>The report of the Peace Society, which was read and adopted at the annual meeting, refers to the aftermath of wars and the direful results of the perpetual preparation for war, and em- " the facts side, the events idde, it points aPo bfh oat uhs ites eymse tt. reTi sut m, as t7lni gaasbt oo illustratedut th ew oatrh'e' ra to progress in Peace circles in the Peace move- ment, and, in society generally, making special reference to various facts relating to the pro- gress of arbitration. The work of the society, it records, has been carried on during the year with the usual vigour and usefulness, and with somewhat greater variety. The last annual meeting at the fansion House was a record one, and encouraging. At the opening of the Peace Palace at The Hague, in August, a marble bust of King Edward VII., Presented by the society, was unveiled, the society being represented at the 20th Universal Peace Congress previously held in that city.</p>
        <p>_ The remainder of the year was spent by the Secretary, Dr. Darby, in a Peace propaganda tour across Canada, occupying four months, and involving 80 addresses and at least 14,000 miles of travel. This time at home was occupied with a vigorous Anti-Conscription Campaign, the observance of Peace Sunday on December 21st, and in general activity. A total of nearly 700 addresses and meetings stand to the account of the Secretary and agents for the year, while the Auxiliaries and Affiliated Societies report continued activity. The publications issued during the year amount to 56,800, to which have to be added 42,400 copies of the " Herald of Peace" and "Olive Leaf" in four quarterly issues, or a total of 100,000 copies. The co- operation of the Press continues to a greater extent than before, the list of papers admitting notices and paragraptai bolsg longer than ever.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>CYCLING AND MOTORING':</head>
        <p>CYCLING AND MOTORING':</p>
        <p>Successful British Makes in Yorkshire Light Car Trials.</p>
        <p>CLEAN CHAINS.</p>
        <p>Many cyclists appear to imagine that if they oil their machine in the usual places that is sufficient to ensure the cycle running in good order. This, however, is not the case. The chain, which is not covered in a gear case, must receive careful attention if the full power of the machine is to be obtained with the least expenditure of pedalling. Periodically I make a point of thoroughly cleaning the chain of my bicycle, and, although it takes up a certain amount of time, I consider myself amply repaid. I proceed as follows: After taking the chain off and brushing it well, I leave it to soak for some hours in paraffin oil, then rinse it in the same liquid. It is then allowed to drip until the paraffin is all away, when I let it soak again over night in sperm oil. By so doing the oil penetrates between the links and into all thc. joints. After draining I wipe it well with a soft cloth, then brush it over with blackhead. I , use the same material on the cogs of the wheel and hub. When the chain is put on the machine a remarkable difference is noticeable in the ease with which it runs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>Best Gear.</head>
        <p>The Best Gear.</p>
        <p>One thing the bicycle and the motor car cer- tainly have done is to draw attention to the important part played by the principle of gearing in almost every branch of mechanical activity. Strangely enough, there is no subject about which the average novice is more ignorant. But he certainly has good reason for this unfortunate state. One of his friends swears by a high gear, another hastens to assure him of the many advantages of the low-geared machine, while a third says: " Stick to a medium gear, old man, and you won't be far wrong." Fortunately the cycle maker can be trusted nowadays to avoid extremes, but perhaps my own experience over a number of years may not be uninteresting to prospective riders. I commenced my cycling career on a rather heavy machine, geared at 64, distinctly low as gears go, but then quite high enough for me. How- ever, after considerable pcactice, this seemed too low, except when riding uphill or against a strong head wind. My present cycle, new last season, is fitted with a three-speed hub, having a medium gear of 70, with direct drive, which to me is perfection for all ordinary riding. The low gear of 55 makes hills little more trouble than the level, while the high gear of 85 gives an excellent turn of speed when circumstances are favourable. Mud-guards. . . _</p>
        <p>Practically every roadster is fitted with mud- guards at the present day, but not all are of such a form as will keep the rider clean from top to toe. In windy weather one frequently finds that the mud is blown on to the body from the top of the front wheel; and, again, when traversing any ill-conditioned roads, a certain amount of watery mud is thrown on to one's boots. Two simple devices will overcome these difficulties. For the former an extension of the guard for some eight or nine inches from the fork crown over the top of the front wheel will keep all blown mud off the rider. For the latter a pear-shaped piece of leather reaching to within a few inches of the ground, fitted to the bottom of the front guard, will hinder the feet from becoming bespatted. These are simple precautions, but they add considerably to the comfort of the all-weather cyclist. I have personally found these two additions of the greatest service, and I have no hesitation in riding to the office each day of the year through the worst weather.</p>
        <p>The Light Car Trial. The trial for light cars, which was held in Yorkshire during the first week in May, under the auspices of the Royal Automobile Club, was brought to a close on Saturday, 9th May, at Harrogate. The whole distance was 1,010 miles, including twelve hills, two of which were timed each day, and only eight out of the 32 starters were able to complete the full course. It is gratifying to know that all of the successful cars were of British manufacture and design, since it indicates that our makers are able to turn out a machine that is the equal to those produced in other countrids.</p>
        <p>The Quality of a Cheap Machine. It is an interesting fact to note that seven of the successful cars were drawn from Classes A and C, ,namely, four from the former and three froth the latter. Class A was for cars priced at 150 guineas and under, and Class C for cars valued at between 175 and 200 guineas. I do not want readers to infer from this that the cheapest car is necessarily the best, for that is not always the case, but I think it does indicate that our manufacturers, given a good design, can turn out a trustworthY light car of a quality and at a price that will compare favourably with the; cheap American product. A word must be added with reference to the cars that failed to pass the ordeal. The regulations did not dis- criminate between an engine that is accidentally stopped and a car that is held up owing to a broken part; therefore this fact must be taken into consideration. For this reason it does not mean that all of the machines that were un- successful must be placed in a lower category than those that were able to pass the test. In many cases the failure was purely of a minor character and this point should be remembered.</p>
        <p>Motor Cycles for the G.P.O. It is a surprising fact that the G.P.O. has not introduced the motor cycle and side-car before this for the collection and delivery of letters in the rural districts of the United Kingdom, but I hear that a beginning is to be made in the near future. Orders have been placed with Bir- mingham, Bristol and Coventry firms for twenty machines, and these are to be delivered at an early date. It is impossible to over-estimate the value of mot* cycles for this work, and there is but little doubt that in a short while the 0.P.0. will not be making use of a single horse for any purpose wbatsoevet.. This innovation will affect almost everyone living in the less accessible parts of the country, since by their use it will be possible to arrange for at least two deliveries a day instead of the one which is common at the present time. Not only will the service be accelerated, but the additional mails can be run at a lower cost than is now the case. Govern- ment departments are slow at introducing now ideas, but when once a beginning is made they certainly forge ahead.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>NEW RALEIGH MODELS.</head>
        <p>THE NEW RALEIGH MODELS.</p>
        <p>The latest models of the new Raleigh bicycles for 1914 are now being shown by the Raleigh agents. The Raleigh, known all over the world as "the all-steel bicycle," is always worth the special study of every cyclist, because of its many unique features. Its all-steel construction, for instance, gives it unique strength because of the absence of malleable iron castings in the head and seat-bracket joints and in the crank bracket. These, which are usually employed in other makes, are heavy and liable to hidden flaws. The Raleigh Company use in their stead light, strong loops, pressed from the finest homo- geneous, cold-rolled steel. Raleigh frames are built by most scientific methods; tubes and joints are united by a process vastly superior to that in general use. In place of the old-fashioned blow-pipe and brazing hearth, where the sound- ness of the joint depends entirely on the skill and care of the workman, and under which method imperfect brazing is of frequent occur- rence, Raleigh cycle frames, after being fitted together, are immersed in a crucible of molten brass, which is kept at a uniform temperature, so that there is no possibility of a burnt tube,</p>
        <p>THE CYCLISTS' NOTE BOOK.</p>
        <p>Importance of Learning to Pedal Correctly.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>CHESHIRE REST HOUSES.-12,</head>
        <p>CHESHIRE REST HOUSES.-12,</p>
        <p>Raven Inn, Ashbrook.—lt is 37 Miles there and back to this pretty little rest house on the road to Church Minshull and Crewe. Eight members, including two ladies, of a Warrington Cycling Club, drew up there for tea a week or two ago, and I was one of the other six. Strange, I had not discovered it before, for it is plainly marked on the sheet 44 of the Ordnance Survey that I reviewed in this column on the 2iid of May. We had left the major section of the club at Whitegate, having made a quick jour- ney with the wind behind us, by the direct road through Sandiway. The running through Over is splendid, and down Swanlow-lane it is nearly all a gentle slope, downwards to the Raven Inn. The whole of this fine road lies along a ridge of high land separating the great Cheshire plain from the valleys of the Weaver, the Dane and the Wheelock, and magnificent stretches of country are visible on both hands all the way. On the west side are Beeston Castle and the Peckforton Hills, with Moel Famma in Denbighshire in the far distance; on the east, the dim outlines of the Derbyshire Hills back the splendid view.</p>
        <p>After tea, the landlord—l quite forget his name—took us to the back of the farm, and pointed out many interesting bits in the view— Nantwich Church, about nine miles away, and just off the right of the church, the Wrekin in Shropshire, no less than 35 miles away in a straight line. Coming back we turned off at Marton Cross, and enjoyed a fine 'spin through Delamere and Norley.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>RIDING EASILY AND CORRECTLY.</head>
        <p>RIDING EASILY AND CORRECTLY.</p>
        <p>With so many miles of dead level country on all sides of us, the greatest mistake is made by many cyclists in not learning accurate pedal- ling. The beginner keeps the foot horizontal all the way round and so only drives with one foot and that only during less than half the stroke. A great deal is sometimes said about the "dead centres," as if our driving power was a piston and connecting rod, and needed a fly wheel like an engine. There ought to be no dead centre with a pliable motor such as the human ankle. The proper position for the sole of the foot is to get the widest part over the pin of the pedal. Then before the pedal passes the top of the stroke the heel is lowered quickly, and so the foot pushes forward. After the easy downward thrust the heel is raised in order to claw the pedal backwards till the other foot is able to exert its full power. Thus for two- thirds of the circuit both feet are helping the machine forward, and the best work is being got out of the limbs. It is much easier to learn these actions when the gear is large and the feet are not working too quickly to think of what we are doing, and then comes in the ad- vantage of a long 8-inch crank for men, or 7- inch for women, with a big gear to correspond. When choosing a machine, sit comfortably on the saddle and with one pedal at its lowest point and the leg outstretched see if you can place the toes under the lowered pedal. If you can easily, that is the proper height of frame and saddle for you, and you will get most work out of your efforts.</p>
        <p>CLUB RUNS TO-DAY. Warrington Cycling Club—Acton Bridge. Warrington Wesleyan—Runcorn Pier Head. Sankey Bridges Wesley Guild—Pickering Bridge.</p>
        <p>Lamp Time: To-day 9.10; next Saturday, 9.20. SILVER-STREET.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>ST. lIEI,ENS GAMBLERS AND POLICE.</head>
        <p>ST. lIEI,ENS GAMBLERS AND POLICE.</p>
        <p>At St. Helens on Tuesday Peter Ross, 115, Central-street, was charged with gambling, ob- structing the police, and assaulting Constables Ramsden and Dorrell, on Sunday. Mr. Haslam Fox defended. Chief Inspector Dunn said the two officers went into Messrs. Pilkington's glass works and saw about 20 men assemble and play pitch and toss on a large scale. Two men with their jackets off and sleeves rolled up were pitching the coins, and the others were betting on the pitch. After watching from a position about 30 feet from the gang, the officers went out of the works into tlid street to tackle them. A man standing at the corner of Victoria-street gave the alarm, and when the officers arrived there was, of course, no sign of gambling. He told Derrell to arrest Holding and Jones, and Rams- den arrested Litherland and Moses Middlehurst, who had been convicted on his own admission of gambling on this occasion. Constable Ramsden said when they had the four men in custody they were followed by a rapidly-swelling ciowd. At the corner of Central-street and Standish-street, Ross and an- other man assumed the leadership and appealed to the crowd not to allow the officers .to take the men. He rushed at him and liberated his two prisoners, and knocked him about. Dorrell and witness then succeeded in locking up Afiddlehurst in spite of the crowd of 500 or GOO. They immediately went back to arrest Ross, and found him on the waste land near Cowley. street. They told him that he was to be arrested, and he at once rushed at them with his head down, pulled their legs from under them, and threw them, and after a struggle w n uta bleed- ing away. Witness next saw Dorrell on the ground being kickeddbat, a largeynae as 31 r .F fromo Fox, for rmdo defendant, asa dt to eplrdo prove atth if ft thatprisoner was e sol not gambling, e wasot of tnes se sptrison for two months j uTstiiifieedßeionehresesinsttißngosasrtreo with hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>CYCLING AND MOTORING—continued.</head>
        <p>CYCLING AND MOTORING—continued.</p>
        <p>as in hand brazing, and the molten brass run- ning into every crevice always ensures a perfect joint. This is most essential in the production of a high-cla§s cycle. The Raleigli bearings are hardened so that they will last a lifetime, while every bp is gauged to the three-ten-thousandth part of an inch, the result being that beautiful silky run- ning and "life" which is always so apparent in a Raleigh.</p>
        <p>THE RUNCORN DONKEY. -</p>
        <p>Appeal Case in High Court.</p>
        <p>The case known as the "Runcorn Donkey Case" came up in the King's Bench on Mon- day, when Justices Ridley, Rowlett and Shear- man had before them the case of Dee v. Yorke, which came before them on a case stated by the Runcorn magistrates. The Court upheld the decision of the justices. Mr. S. Bevan said this was a case stated by the Runcorn justices, who refused to convict Dr. Warrington Yorke, who was charged before them with an offence under the Protection of Animals Act, 1911. Dr. Yorke was director of the Runcorn Re- search Laboratories, which were maintained in connection with the Liverpool School of Tropical Medicine, and the offence with which he was charged was that, having experimented as he was entitled to do, on a donkey, in connection with the research work in which he was en- gaged, he exposed it to unnecessary suffering by having it in a field where it was subjected to the . glare of the sun and the attacks of flies. The justices refused to convict. JUSTICES' FINDING.</p>
        <p>Counsel said his contention was that it was apparent that the justices refused to find un- necessary suffering on the ground that Dr. Yorke, being engaged as he was in this research work, and being a person of eminence in that direc- tion, could not be convicted for causing unneces- • sary suffering, and that he was protected by the position he occupied and the experiment. Counsel theft read the case stated by the jus- tices. It said that the doctor was conducting experiments with a view to finding a cure for sleeping sickness.</p>
        <p>On June 20th, 1913, he administered a drug to a donkey, which brought on gradual paralysis without pain. n was then taken out into a field kept for that purpose, and the respondent often went to see the animal. It ultimately got into a dry ditch, where it was fed and watered, a ditch about 100 yards away from a public foot- path. The complaint was made by the police. On July sth the donkey was painlessly destroyed, the experiment being complete. NOT UNNECESSARY SUFFERING. The Bench stated that they were of opinion Ithat the animal did not suffer unnecessary pain in the field, that the respondent knew of the state of the ass, and as the respondent was a great medical authority engaged in experiments for the benefit of mankind, and was licensed by the Secretary of State, he could not be said to be causing intentional and unnecessary suffering and could not be convicted. They, therefore, dismissed the information. The decision, they said, was solely on question of facts. Counsel said appellants did not complain of Ithe experiments, but of exposure of the animal, land on the facts he submitted that the justices had misdirected themselves. Counsel submitted that the animal should hare been put under shelter and properly looked after. \ The Court, without calling upon Mr. Neilson for the other side, dismissed the appeal with costs.</p>
        <p>Mr. Justice Ridley said the Court were of opinion that this decision of the justices must be affirmed. The Court could not interfere here as the matter was one entirely for the justices. Justices Rowlett and Shearman agreed. Mr. Neilson stated that Dr. Yorke had laid all the facts of the case before the Home Office, and they had expressed themselves as entirely satisfied with the conduct of Dr. Yorke in the matter.</p>
        <p>Manliness and Power. Manliness and power are the two things to be admired, to be sought after, and are to be ob- tained by studying the needs of the body, and serving it in proper manner. Man is consti- tuted in a way that calls. for a mixed diet, being only partly carnivorous. Nature has pro- vided him with one of the very best foods— grape sugar. This is a substance which so builds up the body that it becomes powerful and manly, and gives one that incomparable health that makes life worth living. Grape sugar is so called because it is found in the grape, esp - cially in those ripened naturally in the sun as in Greece. The little Grecian grape which we receive in the form of the Currant is full of pure grape sugar. Currants, by their inviting flavour and compelling attractiveness, are always delicious to the taste, as well as highly nutritious. Have you tried this, the latest way of cooking them? „ . STEWED CURRANTS. lb. fine Currants, pint water, lb. sugar, 2 cloves, 1 pinch 'Cinnamon, strip lemon rind, Cochineal Method: Put cloves, lemon, and about 15 dropscinnamon, cochineal into a saucepan and boil 10 minutes. Thoroughly • wash the Currants, and stew them very gently for half an hour. Stewed Currants are very nice served with milk puddings,gs, blancmange. custard, or whipped cream.</p>
        <p>Eyeglasses for Dog. The curious case of the New Jersey boy who sees upside down has been paralleled by a freak of Nature in a Dachshund puppy owned in San I Diego. The dog's sense of distance is reversed. When called he will back away from his master, although obviously desirous of coming to him. A scientist became interested, says the "New York' American," and discovered that the canine was suffering from astigmatic myopia. A special pair of spectacles was made for it. After becoming accustomed to the glasses the dog became nor- ' mai. though its unusual appearance causes much</p>
