      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>EXAMINER; :SATURDAY, MAY 23, 1914.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER; :SATURDAY, MAY 23, 1914.</p>
        <p>CHILDREJI AND CINEMATOGRAPHS.IWIIITECROSS SHAFT FATALITY.</p>
        <p>Education Committee Suggest Some Stringent</p>
        <p>Restrictions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>DISEASE AND DEFECTIVE VISION.</head>
        <p>DISEASE AND DEFECTIVE VISION.</p>
        <p>Warrington Education Committee on Monday approved of stringent regulations to govern scholars' attendance at picture shows. These are the outcome of the detailed particu- lars prepared by Dr. Joseph, the school medical officer, and presented in his report. Briefly, children under 14 years are not to be allowed in such places after 8.15 p.m. unless accompanied by a parent or a guardian, or in the hours when the schools are open. In times of an epidemic of infectious disease it is suggested that all children under the age of 14 years be excluded from the shows. Seats for the use of the audience not to be placed nearer the screen than 20 feet. Although approved by the Education Com- mittee, that committee have no power to en- force these regulations. They, however, ask the Health Committee to agree with them, and then recommend the Watch Committee to make the necessary stipulations when issuing or renewing licences to picture houses.</p>
        <p>Mr. Howitim KINGDON, as chairman of the Medical Inspection Sub-committee, introduced the proposals. The minutes stated that the committee, having proceeded to the further con- sideration of the report of the School Medical Officer with special reference to cinematograph exhibitions and their influence upon the health of public elementary school children, it was resolved:</p>
        <p>"That the following recommendations be for- warded for the consideration of the Health Com- mittee with a view to their final zubmission to the Watch Committee for embodiment An the regulations governing the issue of lice ices to cinematograph exhibitions, viz : "1. Children under fourteen years of age shall not be allowed to enter into or be in the licensed premises after the hour of 8.15 p.m., unaccompanied by their parent or guardian. "2. Children under fourteen years of age shall not be allowed to enter into or be in the licensed premises during the hours when the public elementary schools are open. "3. In the event of an epidemic of infectious disease arising and the Medical Officer of Health advising that it is in the interests of public health, the Watch Committee may, for such period as they think fit, direct that all children under the age of fourteen shall be exclud'ed, and after such direction has been notified to the licensees, no child under fourteen years shall be permitted to enter into or be in the licensed premises at any time during the period named. "4. Seats for the use of the audience shall not be allowed to be placed nearer than twenty feet from the screen on which the pictures are shown."</p>
        <p>He first of all paid a tribute to the excellent way in which the report had been prepared and presented by the School Medical Officer, his assistant, and the Director of Education. It was the first report they had received from Dr. Joseph, and it was a most excellent one. (Hear, hear.) He did not propose to deal with the clinics, as that work had met with the satisfac- tion of the committee.</p>
        <p>" Keen-ama-tograph." Another point had arisen in the report upon which it was desirous that the committee should take steps to remedy what was an unsatisfac- tory state of affairs. That was in regard to children attending the cinematograph—Mr. Kingdon pronounced it " Keen-ama-tograph "] —shows.</p>
        <p>MEMBER: The what? (Laughter.) Mr.,KINGDON said he understood that was the enact pronunciation of the word—(laughter)— though he did not know whether members would follow him in it. (Laughter.) Councillor ROBERTS (the chairman) : I would like to hear it again. (Laughter.) Mr. KINGDON : I am afraid you will hear it many times • before I am finished. (Laughter). The speaker then dealt with each clause. The first, he said, was purely a health matter. The second excluded children during school hours, and the third led to certain limitations in the case of epidemics. They wished to stop chil- dren who might step from the clinic round the corner to a picture show, from attending such places. That was a weak link in their chain which should be strengthened. Children shut out on account of epidemics should certainly not be allowed to go to such exhibitions, where they would sit close to other children, and thus spread the disease. (Hear, hear.) That was undoing all the good work the committee were trying to carry out. Considered Broadly. He wished to consider the question broadly. In Warrington they had a large number of chil- dren attending elementary schools, and they, as an Education Committee, had to make up their minds whether they were to exercise a certain amount of control over these children, and it was for them to say when the children should go to the shows. There was a tendency to-day to be afraid of ridicule, and there might be a fear that because nobody else had done what was now proposed, somebody might laugh at the committee. If it was right then they should go in for it. It was a great mistake that they, as an Education Com- mittee, did not try to take the lead a little more than they did. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>Lead in School Clinics.</p>
        <p>--..--. _ ..... They were amongst the first to adopt school clinics, and he was anxious that they should follow it up to safeguard the health of the chil- dren. The newspapers were giving a good deal of support, and he saw from one report that the Lancashire teachers were taking a stronger view of the subject than he did. It was found that 50 per cent. of the poorest children went to the picture shows, many going every even- ing each Iveek. No one could persuade him, said one teacher, but that many films were most unsuitable for children. (Hear, hear.) If he had the power he would not allow a child to go to picture palaces until it was 14 years of age. Person- ally, he (Mr. Kingdon) was not prepared to go quite as far, but he thought they should ap- prove of the proposals laid before them. "THAT BEAST."</p>
        <p>_ Mr. C. E. PARKER said he wished to refer to another matter in the report, but thought it would not complicate matters if he refrained until they had dealt with—(turning to Mr. Kingdon): What do you call the beast? (Loud laughter.) Mr. H. KINGDON (jocularly) : Are you refer- ring to me, sir? (Laughter.) Mr. FAIRCLOUGH wanted to know what steps were taken to exclude children already suffering from disease. He thought they should take that idto consideration with the other matters.</p>
        <p>CHILDREN OF THE POOR. Councillor PEMBERTON pointed out that it was intended to exclude children at 8.15 p.m., whereas the first house at the picture shows did not end until 8.40 p.m. He could not see why the children of the poor were singled out and not the children of the rich. These were unfortunately the only places where the children of the poor coulthgo. No doubt they would like to go to the Hippodrome or the theatre, but they have to go to the pic- tures because they are much cheaper. He had seen many 'instructive pictures, but there were some le thought the children should not see. He thought it would be far better for the Watch Committee to censor the proprietors of places where there were such films, rather than to debar the children altogether from the pleasure of attending such places.</p>
        <p>With a- Cool Mind. They must look at the Matter with a cool mind. The committee °nett to be very careful what they were doing. He rather resented the idea that the children of the poor should be interfered with. A man who was getting 18s. per week had as much affection for his children as a man who was getting 418 a week. lie did not like the second clause. Suppose a school was open and a child was away with a cut fioger or a sprained wrist, that child could not go to the pictures. The third clause was somewhat arbitrary. When schools were closed it prevented children who had not the disease from going to the pictures, yet thee,e, were allowed to phiy in the parks and the streets. They cm:11 go to the theatre, but :act to the pictiitee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>TO ADD THE THEATRES.</head>
        <p>TO ADD THE THEATRES.</p>
        <p>_ Mr. KINGDOM asked if Mr. Pemberton would be satisfied if the Hippodrome and theatres were added to the resolution. Councillor PEMBERTON said he would not. He thought the child suffering from n infectious disease should be isolated, and it was not right to punish all the children who were not ill. He objected to the children of the poor being selected for such treatment, while the children of the millionaire were left alone. Unfortu- nately, some people thought poverty made all the difference.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN: I think it is the poverty which is really, the foundation of the scheme. Those who are better off can take sore of their chil- dren.</p>
        <p>COMING TO THAT. Councillor PEMBERTON : I am coming to that. Their poverty is so appalling that they want somebody to take care of them. That seems to be the plea of everybody here. I think, how- ever, it is a very serious thing to interfere too much with the liberty of the subject. Father WILSON said he could not vote for the resolution unless the other licensed places were added. It seemed to him it was an attempt to put restrictions on the poor and not upon those who were in a better position. He agreed with Councillor Pemberton that the restrictions were too whole Sale in their character. *There was one thing which would have to be done. The municipality must find cheap amusement under healthy conditions for these children.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>"TOUCHED THE SPOT."</head>
        <p>"TOUCHED THE SPOT."</p>
        <p>Alderman BENNETT thought Father Wilson had touched the spot. The poor little children of the town craved for pleasure and excitement, and that they could only find at the picture shows. That such was possible was a serious reflection upon society. He could not think of a more pathetic spec- tacle than that of the past two weeks, when crowds of poor children were going about back streets with miniature Maypoles trying to get a little artificial pleasure. (A Member: And pennies.) That might be 80. He would not talk further about the spectacle, as he felt bitter against it. It should be possible to give the children a real Maypole amiast the trees and in surroundings that were healthy and wholesome.</p>
        <p>They as a corporation had not done one tithe of their duty to the children, otherwise every part of the town would have its place where every child could live a natural, healthy life. Legitimate Pleasures. Why should any poor child be deprived of the legitimate pleasures which the members allowed to their own children? (Councillor PEMBERTON : Hear, hear.) Every child should have the same opportunity and until that was so they, Cor- Poration members, had not done their duty as civilised, Christian citizens. The CHAIRMAN: You are going a little outside the subject on the agenda. (Laughter.) Alderman BENNETT said he knew that, but he felt strongly on the subject. He knew, un- fortunately, from experience, that many of the films were morbid and unwholesome. He saw one which depicted the Roman arena put in the crudest possible way, and that picture nearly made his blood run cold. It was not good for children to see such scenes. SCHOOLMASTER'S VIEW. Mr. J. Ilesobi said from a great deal of the discussion one would think that the committee proposed to exclude children entirely from such shows. (A Member: "I wish we could do !") The first clause said _children should not be in these places after 8.15 p.m. They could go before that time. There was Saturday after- noon, when they did not propose to exclude the children. The Chairman of the sub-committee had given the idea that Warrington were pioneers in the matter. They were not pioneers in Warrington, but were lagging lamentably in the rear of Liverpool, where they had ex- cluded children after 4.30. Then Councillor Pemberton had said that when the schools were closed, it was proposed to exclude the poor children from the pictures, and allow the others to go. If Councillor Pem- berton could tell them how to get at the other children they would soon shut them out.</p>
        <p>Parental Control. Ae question of interference with parental control had been raised. He did not know if Councillor Pemberton, knew he could not take children into places licensed for the sale of drink; the State interfered there. He did not think anyone would suggest that such a law should be altered, although it might interfere with the "liberty of the subject." What the committee proposed was to help the parents in the interest of the children. It was unfortun- ate that the children had not votes, for he thought the outlook would be modified if they were voters. (Laughter.) A great many parents did not see what was for the good of their children, and the com- mittee had to help them, and if they could not persuade them they must legislate so as to remedy the parental control, and exclude chil- dren from places they knew was detrimental to the welfare of the children.</p>
        <p>" Place of Worship." In regard to clause three, the committee would be prepared to add to that resolutiom the words "all places of entertainment." He could corrobo- rate what Mr. Bingdon had said from personal experience as to the detrimental effect upon children attending " these places of worship." (,Loud laughter.) His slip had been rather un- fortunate. (Laughter.) The children suffered from eye strain. Some- time ago he went into a picture palace and was within 25 feet of the screen and he suffered from a splitting headache through being so near the screen. (A Member: "Question.") The chil- dren went to the cheapest parts of the house, which were close to the screen, and the eye- strain was most severe. Canon STEVENSON suggested that children suffering from skin diseases as were not " epi- demic," such as ringworm, should be excluded. Mr. LoNGLAND thought that clause four would be hard on children who visited Warrington, or were home from other schools for holidays. He did not see anything in the point about legislat- ing against the "children of the poor," for if children under fourteen were excluded when the elementary schools were open, "children " would be excluded whether they were poor or well-to-do.</p>
        <p>• Mr. Ptissrox said when children were in a picture palace from 6.15 to 8.15 p.m., they had seen the whole of the show. He did not think they would do much for the 'children of the future if they did not now act. THE PRACTICAL ISSUE. Mr. C. E. PARKER, said they must get to the practical issue. Ile thought medical men were agreed it was useless to close the school in epi- demics when children could go to other places and mass together in an atmosphere probably worsts than that of the schools. There was a great deal to be said on both sides, but it was not unreasonable to ask those children who had only cut fingers to sacrifice a little for the :eneral good. Ever;body had to sacrifice some- thing for others. Though the recommendations might appear to be illogical in not including all places of amusement, it had been found in several places that picture palaces were the cause of the spread of infection. In the cir- cumstances he thought the recommendations to the Watch Committee were justified and toler- ably moderate. Councillor CHRIMES, while not opposed to the scheme, moved it back for further considera- tion. Councillor PEMBERTON seconded. PROBABLE MODIFICATIONS.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. J. JOLLY said the scheme was not final, and it would probably be modified by the Health and Watch Committees, BO there was no harm done in approving of it.</p>
        <p>_ Mr. KINGDOM appealed to Councillor Chrimes to withdraw his amendment. The CHAIRMAN supported that appeal and thought it was only dealing partially with the question to select one class of entertainment and not all.</p>
        <p>Finally the amendment whs defeated, three voting for it—Father Wilson and Councillors Pemberton and Chrimes.</p>
        <p>_ The recommendation was then agreed to unanimously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>Whitewashing with Machinery id</head>
        <p>Whitewashing with Machinery id</p>
        <p>lotion.</p>
        <p>JURY'S CONSENT.</p>
        <p>There was a sequel to a shocking fatality at Whitecross Works, when, on Monday, Coroner Brighouse conducted an inquest on the victim. Albert Jackson (33), lodging at 29, Plumpton- street, who was whirled round a shafting at the works on the previous Thursday, whilst white- washing in one of the shops. Mr. A. Browne appeared for the relatives, and Mr. T. S. Steel for the firm. Mr. H. E. Brothers, H.M. Inspector of Factories, r.:so watched the proceedings.</p>
        <p>A "HURRY UP " .1011. William Herbert Jack on labourer at the works, residing at 54, Plutupton-street, said de- 'ceased, who was his brother, was a married man with three children, whose wife left him seven years ago and had not been seen since. He spoke to the deceased at 6.30 a.m. on the morning; of the accident, and at 7.30 he heard of the accident. He found his brother on the scaffold, where he had been whitewashing, in the Old Roll Turning Shop. 'Mr. Browne: What was to prevent this white- washing being done at the end of the week when the shaft was not running?—Nothing, sir. Had you orders to get this job done as quickly as possible?—That job was a "hurry up" job from the time we started it up to the accident, The place was wanted for a purpose. Through not being left over a risk is run P—lt seems so.</p>
        <p>With the result that your brother was killed? —Yes.</p>
        <p>How far would your brother be from the shaft when the accident happened?—l should think about two feet. Was the shaft on a level with your brother? No, sir, a bit above. Have you dose this whitewashing before?— Only once last year. What part of the week?—ln the week. The Coroner: Was there anything to Prevent him having a lower scaffold and a longer brush? Could you then have done the work just as well? —Yes, sir, I could.</p>
        <p>Croft-examined. Mr. Steel: Who told you it was a "hurry Up" job?—l have been on the job and know what it was. Do you know it would not have mattered if it had been a month P—They told us it was wanted for a certain time.</p>
        <p>Who told you—Ayres, the foreman. Did you notice how many long brushes there were F—One. Were there not three?—No. One seven feet long, and the others four to five feet. They would have enabled anyone to reach the parts away from the shafting?—The four or five feet brushes would have been no use. The Foreman: We have seen three or four there this morning. Was the scaffolding safe? —Yes. Mr. Steel: The room is exactly like it was on the day of the accident. Photographs of the room and the shafting were put in.</p>
        <p>A MATE'S VERSION. John Aspinall, labourer, 48, Mill-lane, said he was employed along with Jackson in white- washing the shop. A painter named Edward Chew was also at work in the same shop. Jack- son was working on the top of the scaffold, which was seven feet from the floor. They were wear- ing sacks to prevent their clothes getting splashed with whitewash. Mr. Ayres must have seen where the scaffolding was when he en- tered the shop in the morning. Whilst work- ing on the floor he heard a shout, and on look- ing up saw deceased being drawn up and whirled round by the shafting. He had the engine stopped, and Jackson was taken to the Infirmary, after his clothes had been cut from the shafting where they were held tightly, by Arthur Cotterill, a blacksmith, ,of 5, Froghall- lane.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>Coroner's Questions.</head>
        <p>Coroner's Questions.</p>
        <p>The Coroner. Was there anything to prevent a lower scaffolding and a longer brush being used?—No.</p>
        <p>The planks could not be placed on the sup- ports of the trestles lower down, for these are only used to hold the trestles together?—That is so. $</p>
        <p>Was there anything to prevent this work being done when the shaft was not going?—l don't think so.</p>
        <p>Could it have been done in overtime F—The shaft was going at nights. When you were on the scaffold were you as near to the shaft as deceased was when he was killed?—Yes, sir. The Inspector: Was the shafting above or on one side of the planks?—The staging was at one side.</p>
        <p>Had you been warned as to the danger of ap- proaching the shaft?—No. Mr. Browne: Could you have worked with the one 7ft. brush that was there?—l don't think we could on the scaffold. We could on the floor, but the scaffold was too high for it. Are these whitewashings generally done at week-ends?—l could not say; I have only been employed by the firm for three months. What I have seen done has been at week-ends. Were you aware the job had to be done in a hurry ?—No. Four of you were on it the day before, and there were only two on Thursday F—Yes. This place was wanted by the heads for a specific purpose at a specific time?—l could not say exactly.</p>
        <p>Not a " Rush Job." And the job was being rushed?—l don't think it was a rush job. Did you consider it dangerous to be near the shaft?—l did not like the idea of being too close to it. Mr. Steel: The dangerous' position was about two feet from the shaft?—Yes. Is the.shaft three feet four inches above the scaffold What kind of brush was Jackson using?—A "Turk's head," about 4ft. or Mt. in length.</p>
        <p>CAUGHT BY THE BACK. Edward Chew, painter, 4, Albert-street, said he was doing glazing work in the shop. He heard a shout and saw Jackson being taken round by the shaft, his back being close against it. He was unable to stop the machinery, but Aspinall was successful. The Coroner: How far was deceased from the shaft when he was on the planks?—About two feet. _</p>
        <p>Where did the shaft catch Jackson ?—ln the middle of the back. If he had bent forward he would not have been touched, but he must have leaned backwards. What strikes everyone is why was this work not being done when the shaft was stopped?—l have nothing to do with the job. Mr. Browne: Did you consider it to be dan- gerous when the shaft was going round-4 did not think he would have to go close to it. It would be dangerous in any case where there is a revolving shaft?—Yes. Mr. Steel: How do you think he met with the accident ?—He might have done it when he was bending down to get more whitewash on his brush.</p>
        <p>You were asked about it being dangerous. I suppose you think it is dangerous now F—Yes. THE FOREMAN'S STORY. Thomas Ayres said he was the charge hand, and the whitewashing was being done under his supervision. ' The Coroner: Why was this work not being done when the shaft was stopped ?—I had no orders to do that. My orders were to clean the shop out and whitewash it. You assumed that to be whilst the shafting was runniug?—Yes, How did it come about that this man was allowed to be so near this shaft, going round at 100 revolutions per minute?—l could not say exactly. -</p>
        <p>You saw the scaffolding?—l told him on the Wednesday that when he came near the shaft he must "shift" to the other side. On the Thursday morning did you see the trestle in the place in which it was when the accident occurred?—l could not say exactly. I was called out, and when I came back the acci- dent had happened. Did you see the trestle when you went in?—l did not notice it.</p>
        <p>Could Trust Jackson Anywhere. You don't justify putting a man ow a scaffold-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>OLD WORLD TIND THE NEW.</head>
        <p>THE OLD WORLD TIND THE NEW.</p>
        <p>THE CONCERT OF SOUTH AMERICA. THE MEDIATORS: "Pardon, Madain, but you have had a lot of experience of this kind of thing EUROPA: "Oh., we just talk and talk—and then talk !"</p>
        <p>ing where there is a revolving Aaft?—l could trust Albert Jackson anywhere. You can trust people till you find your trust is misplaced.</p>
        <p>• because I was called away. What time did you see. the scaffold last?— About four o'clock on Wednesday. Do you swear you told Aspinall and Jackson they must not go to within three feet of the shaft?—l did not say three feet from the shaft. Further questioned by Mr. Browne, witness said het did not tell Aspinall, but Jackson; the former was doing the walls, and was not forced to go. up the scaffold. They had no orders to do the work when the shaft was stopped. They were not rushed with the job. The job could ,have been done at week-end if they had had orders.</p>
        <p>Did you think it reasonable to ask a man to work on a scaffold two feet from a revolving shaft?—l did not tell him to do that. I don't gather from you what orders you gave him?—l told him to shift his trestle to the other side when he got near the shaft. Why did he not shift it?—He would have his own reasons for that.</p>
        <p>_ I should have thought you would have looked at the scaffold to see if it were far enough away from the shaft?—No reply. Supposing he had had a " dazy " bout or his foot slipped? The thing was full of dangerous possibilities?—No reply. The Inspector: You saw it immediately after the accident?—Yes.</p>
        <p>Mr. Steel: In a large works like Whitecross everything has to be done as quickly as pos- sible?—Yes.</p>
        <p>The instructions given to Jackson were differ- ent from those given to Aspinall?—Yes. How long would it take to move the scaffold? —A few minutes.</p>
        <p>A Serious Point. Part of this staging was at one side of the shaft, which was above, and the other on the other side?-one _plank was over.</p>
        <p>The Coroner: Now i 8 this right or wrong? It is giving a wrong impression to the jury and everyone. The other witnesses have led us to believe that if the man came backwards his heels would be on the outside edge of the last plank, and he would still be two feet from the shaft ?—Yes,_ he would.</p>
        <p>, Would he be two feet from it in any position? If what you say is right, by moving his posi- tion he could put himself directly up to the re- volving shaft. Let us have that intelligent witness, Aspinall.</p>
