        <p>p,c 'tY-STREET P.S.A. SOCIETY. :., 14 *4l')Z,'!.ection With the above society the prize . Aii%tills3;4" took place on Sunday. 31r. H .41 AtIIIII. reSlCled• Miss Henderson, of St. . Mr. itrlvere • Waddell, of Liverpool. Two A rendered by Whitmore, lit,t' ie TY God to Thee," land "But the 11141)44111„14(iful..". Mr. G. H. Cos was the 116, 41" '.Smith read the 'Men.</p>
        <p>JOHN WESLEY—continued.</p>
        <p>of the severe frost) and no assistance from the parish wherein they lived: were reduced to the last extremity. I made another collection on Thursday, and a third on Sunday; by which we were enabled to feed a hundred, .sometimes a hundred and fifty, a day, of those whom we found to need it most.</p>
        <p>Account of Stewardship. I gave the Society an account of what had been done with regard to the poor. By the con- tributions and collections I had received about one hundred and seventy pounds; with which about three hundred and thirty poor had been provided with needful clothing. Thirty or forty still remaining in want, and there being some debts for the clothes already distributed, the next day, being Good Friday, I made one col- lection more of about six and twenty pounds. This treasure, at least, neither rust nor moth shall corrupt, nor thieves break through and steal.</p>
        <p>In the morning I met the Strangers' Society, instituted wholly for the relief, not of our Society, but for poor, sick, friendless strangers. I do not know that I ever heard or read of such an institution till within a few years ago. So this also is one of the fruits of Methodism. #.</p>
        <p>Loan Society. I made a public collection towards a lend- ing-stock for the poor. Our rule is to lend only twenty shillings at once, which is repaid weekly within three months. I began this 'bout a year and a half ago; thirty pounds sixteen shillings were then collected, and out of this no less than 2155 persons have been relieved in 18 months. Upon the whole, I must once more earnestly entreat you to consider yourself and God and eternity. As to yourself, you are not the pro- prietor of any thing, no, not of one shilling in the world; you are only a steward of what an- other entrusts you with, to be laid out, not according to your will, but His. And what would you think of your steward if he laid out what is called your money, according to his own will and pleasure? Is not God the sole proprietor of all things? And are not you to give an account to Him for every part of His goods? I left no money to anyone in my will, because I had none.</p>
        <p>Dangbr of Riches. I went on to Macclesfield, and found a people still alive to God, in spite of swiftly increasing riches. If they continue so, it will be the only instance I have known, in above half a century. I warned them in the strongest terms I could, and believe some of them had ears to hear. I met those of our Society who had votes in the ensuing election, and advised them (1) to vote, without fee or reward, for the person they judged most worthy; (2) to speak no evil of the person they voted against; and (3) to take care their spirits were not sharpened against those that voted on the other side.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>ASPI NALL RE-CALLED.</head>
        <p>ASPI NALL RE-CALLED.</p>
        <p>--- • The Coroner (to Aspinall),: Now, Aspinall, could this man by moving any way on that scaffold get close up to the revolving shaft?— Yes, sir, he could touch it.</p>
        <p>We are up -fig-a-in-WS-On:thing now. He could if he liked have touched it. Mr. Steel, how .are you going to juirtify that? Putting a man on there, and if he moves he can touch a revolving shaft with his body? Mr. Steel: How long was it before the acci- dent that the scaffold was removed?—lt was put right the night before for the morning. We were in a funny position. The Coroner: It is quite clear that this man could voluntarily or involuntarily get his body in contact with this, revolving shaft. Mr. Steel: It is what I understood that this shaft was two feet away from the edge of the planks. Mr. Browne: You have thought wrong, then. The Coroner: Mr. Brothers understood the position, and he has helped us to ascertain what was the true state of affairs. He could have touched the shaft from a position on the scaffold.</p>
        <p>Mr. Steel: That is not what I understood. The Coroner: That is what this witness says. If you can bring him down, do so. Mr. Steel (to witness): Assuming the edge of the •scaffold had been two feet away from the shaft, could he have reached over the shaft for whitewashing?—The ' planks were practically underneath.</p>
        <p>Needed a Long Brush.</p>
        <p>Assuming he had had instructions not to go within three feet of the shafting, could he have reached over?—With a long brush. Did you hear Mr. Ayres tell him he was not to get nearer than three feet to the shaft ?—I did not hear him. We were told to keep away from the pulley.</p>
        <p>Although you were told not to go near it you whitewashed at the back of it?—We could not whitewash the back because the spokes were going round. You will see by the photograph that it has not been whitewashed. Did you not get nearer the pulley than you ought to have done? The Coroner: I don't think that is fair, "You ought to have done." He does not admit they ought to have done anything. Mr. Steel: I thought he admitted with re- gard to the pulley.. The Coroner: We are not talking about the pulley. It was the foreman's place to see the thing was right.. _ _</p>
        <p>- - Mr. Steel: Who fixed the scaffold?—The two Brothers Jackson.</p>
        <p>The Coroner (to witness): I think they ought to make you charge man. You mould be very useful in that capacity.</p>
        <p>AYRES RE-CALLED.</p>
        <p>The witness Ayres was re-called, and said that Aspinall was near when he gave Jackson in- structions. The machinery was making a lot of noise at the time.</p>
        <p>The Coroner had some difficulty in elucidat- ing the exact position of affairs from the wit- ness and said he could not follow Ayres. What the witness was telling them was so inconsist- ent, for Aspinan did not hear any instructions about the shaft. Mr. Browne: Is it apart of your duty to see where the scaffold is?—When I went in this man called me out.</p>
        <p>Was it part of your duty to see the scaffold was not in a dangerous position?—Yes. Did you fulfil that duty P.—Not that morning,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>VERDICT.</head>
        <p>THE VERDICT.</p>
        <p>After a long summing up by the Coroner, the jury retired, and on their return the foreman said: "We find death was accidental. We believe the foreman's statement, but we think he ought to have paid more attention to the scaffold, and the work should have been done when the shaft was not running." Mr. Steel expressed the firm's sympathy with the relatives. Jackson was a reliable man and capable of doing the work he was put to do. It mdst have been quite a misunderstanding on the part of Ayres, for his instructions from the chief engineer were that the work was to be done at night. The Coroner: The jury have given a sensible verdict. The foreman expressed the sympathy of the jury with the relatives.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>ST. HELENS COUNCIL CRITICISED.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS COUNCIL CRITICISED.</p>
        <p>St. Helens Council was criticised by Father Hearne; rector of Sacred Heart Catholic Church, at the foundation-stone laying ceremony on Sun- day of a new .t 1,600 institute for the Catholic Young Men's Society:</p>
        <p>Fat.lier Hearne said he did not think any of them liked the reading of the 'papers. for some time past, and they were inclined to think there were some in the St. Helens Town Council who were not at all in their favour, but were very much against them. The policy of laissez faire was about exhausted. They had tried to get their 'just demands; but things were going worse. Still, with such a body of men as the Catholic Young Men's Society, united for the common interests of the faith, they could do very much to destroy this particular attitude in St. Helens. He would nqt say that he was referring to the Labour, Liberal, or Conservative party; but there were some who were not in favour of them (the Roman Catholic body), and it was for the Catholics to see in the coming election if they could alter things a little bit, and look to some of their Catholic young men to come forward for the Council. They wanted some solid Catholics in the Council, not so much for "spouting," but determined to see that the Catholic schools obtained their just rights.</p>
        <p>• . Carrying Lady Juliet Duff as a passenger, Mr. Gustav Hamel flew from Brooklands to Oxford by way of Reading.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>SMOKE OR FRESH AIR?</head>
        <p>SMOKE OR FRESH AIR?</p>
        <p>Mr. C. E.-Parker and the Ventilsl of Schools.</p>
        <p>Discussing the question of school byr raised in the School Medical Officer's re which was considered by the Warrington , cation Committee on Monday, Mr. Parker raised.an important point about„. ventilation. The report stated that tiled tilation of the majority of the schools hill, recent years been improved, but that "co ventilation by windows can be carried out n favourable climatic conditions, it is imP°.ss,„ to keep them sufficiently open when the svi; blowing directly on that side of the won),</p>
        <p>"DIRT AND GRIT."</p>
        <p>Mr. C. E. PARKER said he did not know the doctor had in mind when he spoke 8, climatic conditions, but he knew to wlis' would refer in several of the schools. not a question of the amount of air coral when the wind was blowing from a cer, quarter, but of what was in the air—the and grit which entered the school. That aPP with special force to two or three schools, whether the conditions were related as c and effect or not, the health of the teachers, children in some of them was decidedlY than the average. I should not specially have referred t° matter, he added, if I thought this toent doing what it could to reduce this evil, buil opinion has been—and I have gone into it carefully—that the control with regard to emission in Warrington has been ver.Y and negligent for a great number of St] While I hope it is possibly a little better it was, still I think it is very inefficient.</p>
        <p>WHY WINDOWS WERE SHUT.</p>
        <p>ME. FAIRCLOUGH didinot think it was becc; of the dirt and grit that the school wil/d were kept shut. The teachers complailleoi draughts. He thought that was the of the doctor's reference, and he knew Ifa the fact in schools with which he (311' (dough) was connected. hie Mr. PARKER: What I have said aPP schools which I know of. _</p>
        <p>.....-.. - Alderman BENNETT said when people ,°1 windows with the idea of getting fresh el' wanted fresh air, and not smoke, fumes, grit. It should be the duty of the Corll°l. authorities to see that the fumes and din reduced to the minimum. • Mr. UPSON asked if the Director of had received complaints from certain sel'' .;n1</p>
        <p>to the impossibility of keeping the open, because of the vast volumes of sinw entered the schools when the wind Was certain direction?</p>
        <p>Councillor Poissarom thought they otil tackle the question of ventilation in 3.,,1</p>
        <p>manner. He was not an advocate of nu'</p>
        <p>Penditure, but he thought the COrP°7 could well supply . electric motive Po`e mechanical ventilation.</p>
        <p>Vesuvius, like Mount Etna, is active, sl" and flame rising from the crater.</p>
      </div>
