        <head>(S e-SOC/AL</head>
        <p>(S e-SOC/AL</p>
        <p>ST. PETER'S SUNDAY SCHOOL ANNIVERSARY.</p>
        <p>The anniversary of St. Peter's Sunday School was celebrated on Sunday, when the pulpit was occupied in the morning by the Vicar, and in the evening by the Rev. G. C. Briggs, vicar of Christ Church, Latchford. In the afternoon a service of song was given. The Rev. G. C. Briggs based his sermon in the evening upon the words "This is the first and great commandment," and said that when they heard the familiar words, "Thou shalt love the Lord thy God," in many respects it seemed that they ran contrary to some present-day currents of thought and opinion. It was because many contended that it was the first duty of man to provide himself with a sound system of morality that they were so frequently told that it was more important to undertake the moral training of children rather than their religious educa- tion. It seemed to be taken for granted that the teaching of moral principles was in itself sufficient to produce a good life. But it was not sufficient merely to know what was the right thing to do. There must be some motive power; that could only be found in the affections. That was what the Lord proposed to men—not some moral system, however perfect, but a high affection.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>WYCLIFFE P.S.A.</head>
        <p>WYCLIFFE P.S.A.</p>
        <p>The Rev. T. T. James presided over the open meeting or the Wycliffe P.S.A. Society on Sun- -day, when Mr. C. R. Lewis, of Widnes, deliv- ered an instructive address"ln the beginning." _ .</p>
        <p>The speaker said that although at the time of the creation everything was in a state of chaos, 'evolution was in progress. After the firmament ' and the heavenly bodies and the realms of , Nature had been made, man came into being. Genesis was no borne out by the scientists of early days, but was more in agreement with modern scientists. Genesis, however, said Man was created fully grown, and this science denied, saying that Man originated from a life-giving substance, and developed gradually, in accord- ance with Darwin's theory. But, although people professed to know much, they were ignorant of their true development. There was a triangular view of God and Man. At the top of the scale was God, and at the bottom was the slave. Between the two ex- tremes were different characters. Near the top were the higher and nobler beings; near the bottom were the depraved men of poor intellect. But all were fulfilling the will of God. Madame Sara Silcock rendered "Pious orgies" and "Beyond the dawn" with feeling, Mr. Payton accompanying on the organ. Messrs. William Beswick and B. Farrar (secretary) took part in the service. A vote of sympathy with Mr. C. Williams, who had lost his wife by death, was passed. A collection was taken in aid of the National Institute for the Blind, the Millman referring to its deserving objects.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>SANKEY SUNDAY SCHOOL TEACHERS HONOURED.</head>
        <p>SANKEY SUNDAY SCHOOL TEACHERS HONOURED.</p>
        <p>The services in St. Mary's Church, Great Sankey, were conducted on Sunday by Dr. Chavasse, Bishop of Liverpool. Before com- mencing a confirmation service held in the after- noon, the Bishop said that he had a pleasing duty to perform. On Monday in Liverpool he would present a letter of recognition to all the Sunday school teachers in the diocese who had taught in the Sunday school for more than 21 years, and he was pleased to say that seven of these were from Great Sankey. The Vicar had very kindly and thoughtfully suggested that as he was on the spot he could distribute the letters that afternoon and save the recipients the journey to Liverpool. The letter was in the form of a small illum- inated address and signed by the Bishop, and was as follows :—" Liverpool Diocesan Sunday School Institute Teacher's Certificate. Francis James, by Divine permission, Bishop of Liver- pool. Greeting.—Whereas it hath been duly certified to us that you, being of high character, apt to teach and willing to receive instruction, have rendered faithful service as a Sunday school teacher for a period exceeding 21 years, we, therefore, in high appreciation of this your office, do present unto you this our letter of recognition with the earnest prayer that you may thereby be encouraged to labour even more abundantly for the Church of Christ in any parish within our Diocese of Liverpool where you shall be appointed by the incumbent there- of." The recipients were Captain Hough, who had rnusgdhetu,4l3lyeyaersars; ;MjiassmesTinvisley l w3alrdy,ea3rOs ;y s; CaptainGdarßn.etGt,rah2sam, year2ss; years. Lightfoot, 25 years; an males and 4 vec3afnedmidaaletess . were then confirmed-12</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>RELIC/a</head>
        <p>RELIC/a</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY 23, 1914.</p>
        <p>JOHN WESLEY.</p>
        <p>His Work as a Social Reformer Revealed in His Journal. GREATEST 18th CENTURY FORCE.</p>
        <p>There are few books which give such interest- ing particulars of the conditions of England as the Journal of John Wesley. Augustine Birrell says : " If you want to get into the last century, to feel its pulses throb beneath your finger, be content sometimes to leave the letters of Horace Walpole unturned, resist the drowsy tempta- tion to waste your time over the learned triflers who sleep in the seventeen volumes of Nichols. nay, even deny yourself your annual reading of Boswell, or your biennial retreat with Sterne, and ride up and down the country with the greatest force of the eighteenth century in Eng- land."</p>
        <p>At the recent gathering of the Liverpool Dis- trict Synod, pointed reference was made both at the synod and at the public meetings to the position of the Wesleyan Church in regard to social service. In Wesley there was a rare com- bination of religious intensity, intellectual breadth of view, and a lofty social enthusiasm. His aim was to manifest the love of God in far-reaching service to men. In his thought, as in his Master's, there was no difference be- tween religious and social service. In regard to definite organisation for this service he founded an orphan home, established a loan society, more than one dispensary, and a home for widows. Up to the age of eighty he regu- larly visited and ministered according to their needs to those who lived in slums, in prisons, and in 'workhouses. He established a poor fund which was a reality, not the poor make- believe found at present in Wesleyan Churches. The following is from his Journal:— I reminded the United Society that many of our brethren and sisters had not needful food; many were destitute of convenient clothing; many were out of business, and that without their own fault; and many sick</p>
        <p>and ready to perish; that I'had done what in me lay to feed the hungry, to clothe the naked, to employ the poor, and to visit the sick; but was not alone sufficient for these things; and, therefore, desired all whose - hearts were as my heart- 1. To bring what clothes each could spare, to be distributed among those that wanted most. 2. To give weekly a penny, or what they could afford, for the relief of the poor and sick. My design I told them is to employ for the present all the women who are ont of business and desire it, in knitting. To these we will first give the common price for what work they do, and then add accord- ing as they need. Twelve persons are appointed to inspect these, and to visit and provide things need- ful for the sick. Each of these is to visit all the sick within their district every other day, and to meet on Tuesday evening to give an account of what they have done, and consult what can be done farther.</p>
        <p>Here we have all the principles embodied of our modern Leagues of Help, principles which ought to be in operation, with suitable adapta- tions, in every church. Wesley brought the matter before the United Society and not a section of social workers. He called everyone to contribute and that weekly. To preserve the self-respect of the women, he arranged for them to earn their living by knitting. The standard rate, or, as Wesley calls it, the "common price," was to be paid—no sweated labour. We have the appointment of individual persons to look after individual cases, and of the weekly meeting at which all the helpers were to gather together, consider the cases, and consult "What can be done farther." This last clause is Wes- ley all over. Wesley believed that the true con- ception of society would come as the gift of Christianity, that society should be recon- structed on the basis of the kingdom and brotherhood, as laid down by Christ. As the Master sent His disciples to break down race barriers, Wesley and his followers sought to break down class barriers by including all in the brotherhood of Christ, and he treated all as men having bodies as well as souls. The following are selected</p>
        <p>EXTRACTS FROM THE JOURNAL, - bearing out the above contention:— I appointed to speak with those who had ap- plied to us on a physical account. I found there had been about six hundred in about six months. More than three hundred of these came twice or thrice, and we saw no more of them. About twenty of those who had con- stantly attended, did not seem to be either better or worse. Above two hundred were sen- sibly better; and fifty-one thoroughly cured. The entire expense, from the beginning till this time, was about thirty pounds. Upon reviewing the account of the sick, we found great reason to praise God. Within the year about three hundred persons had received medicines occasionally. About one hundred had regularly taken them, and submitted to a proper regimen. More than ninety of these were entirely cured of diseases they had long laboured under. And the expense of medicines for the entire year amounted to some shillings above forty pounds.</p>
        <p>All my leisure hours this week I employed in visiting the remaining poor, and in begging for them [at 80 years of age]. Having collected about fifty pounds more, I was enabled to relieve most of those that were in pressing distress. Monday and the four following days.l went a-begging for the poor. I hoped to be able to provide food and raiment for those of the Society who were in pressing want, yet had no weekly allowance; these were about two hun- dred, but I was much disappointed. _ . _ _</p>
        <p>_ On this and the four following days I walked through the town and begged two hundred pounds in order to clothe them that needed it most. But it was hard work, as most of the streets were filled with melting snow, which often lay ankle-deep; so that my feet were steeped in snow-water nearly from morning till evening. . .</p>
        <p>[Silvester Horne says of this: "Who is this reckless young enthusiast, defying rheumatism, and bronchitis, and a score of other ills? It is grand old John Wesley, aged eighty-two, tramping the streets of London, through snow- slush in January, to beg warm raiment for the poor."] I was careful to take an exact account of the Society. I was surprised to find only a hun- dred and fifty-nine; I thought they had been double the number. I hope by the assistance of God, within four months, to see that none of these want either food or raiment.</p>
        <p>Visit to the Slums. On Friday and Saturday I visited as many more as I could. I found some in their cells under- ground; others in their garrets, half-starved both with cold and hunger, added to weakness and pain. But I found not one of them unem- ployed who was able to crawl about the room. So wickedly, devilishly false is that common objection: "They are poor only because they are idle."</p>
        <p>His Tenderness. I reduced the sixteen stewards to seven, to whom were given the following instructions: " If you cannot relieve, do not grieve the poor. Give them soft words, if nothing else. Abstain from either sour looks or harsh words. Let them be glad to come, even though they shoula go empty away. Put yourself in the place of every poor man, and deal with him as you would God should deal with you." I visited and administered the Sacrament to our poor widows, four and twenty of whom are tolerably provided for, in our widows' house. I, preached at Hannam, four miles from Bris- tol. In the evening I made a collection in our congregation for the relief of the poor without Lawford's Gate; who, having no work (because</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <p>Continued in preceding column.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>CHAPTER OF THE CLERGY. Church of England v. Church of Rome. PRAYER BOOK REVISION.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER OF THE CLERGY. Church of England v. Church of Rome. PRAYER BOOK REVISION.</p>
        <p>A Chapter of the clergy of the Rural Deanery of Winwick was held at the Church House, Bridge-street, on Wednesday, the Rector pre- siding. The speakers on the Bishop of Oxford's Letter to his Clergy, which has caused much discussion in the Church, were the Revs. S. E. Dymott and H. Gray, and on "The Revision of the Prayer-Book" the Revs. .J. W. Tyrer and Canon Morley Stevenson.</p>
        <p>WELCOMED CRITICISM. The Rev. S. E. DYMOTT said the Bishop of Oxford dealt with three matters—Biblical criti- cism, Protestant Federation (especially in Mission Fields), and the Romanising of the Church of England. The Roman Church did not like criticism, butithe Church of England welcomed it. Men in the Church of England could express their private opinions and different views. He thought the position of the Church of England in regard to Protestant Federation especially in Di ission Fields was quite clear. They had undoubtedly the divine sanction. They believed they had a firm foundation for their own Church, and they must take care that in relation to other religious bodies they did noth- ing to minimise their Church, which had the Divine sanction. In the Church of England they welcomed Biblical criticism, or, to be more accurate, the examination of the Bible: Why should they be afraid? They all wanted the I truth, and truth Would stand inquiry; and had of truth, could only help to make truth inanoqrueiry, clear. reTmhoevinregs results misconceptions they s of examifiation I done nothing to upset the Divine revelation. Was the Christian faith simply the outcome of the development of man's idea of God, or was it as the Church had always held—the direct revela- tion of God to man? If it were the former the infinite gulf between Creator And man was still unbridged. If it were, on the other hand, a direct revelation of God to man, then it was rightly described as "the faith for all, delivered unto the Saints," the faith they earnestly .con- tended. Where had they to-day critics with the intellect of St. John or St. Paul? and cer- tainly those living at the time when the events took place were more able to tell them the truth than those who now sat in their studies. The Christian conscience was the outcome of the Christian faith.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>SUPERNATURAL CREATION.</head>
        <p>SUPERNATURAL CREATION.</p>
        <p>-....—.. The Rev. RoilAcs GRAy said he thought they understood that the Church made much, and would continue to make much, of the necessity for faith as one 9f the necessary conditions in revealing religion, but such faith could only rest on the certain warrant of the Holy Scrip- ture. The Church was a supernatural creation, instituted by Christ, Who spoke with the voice ' of God, and they believed that that was not a form of faith, but THE faith. They were living in an age of inquiry, and the inquirers had put n their h conclusions, but they had hardly disturbed many atnheir convictions at all. The Rev. E. C. E. CARLETON said it was their duty to express their thanks to the two gentle- men who had so eloquently addressed them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>INCREDULOUS PEOPLE.</head>
        <p>INCREDULOUS PEOPLE.</p>
        <p>The Rev. A. WHITE (Ashton-in-Makerfield) con- tended that those who lived in Christ's day con- firmed what they believed to-day. Why should miracles be labelled incredible? Was it because what was represented as having taken place was impossible? That could be the only ground for labelling them incredible. They ought not to use the word incredible, because they were limit- ing the power of God. Perhaps they might ask, instead of miracles being incredible, was it that they were incredulous? If the miracles were such that Christ's own opponents would accept, was it for them to-day to say miracles were incredible? The Rev. B. PERCIVAL said he had noticed a wonderful familiarity with Christ amongst the working classes, who believed whole-heartedly that they could commune with Him now. The RECTOR said they were full of gratitude to the Bishops of London and Oxford for the way in which they had spoken out. They could not criticise in the Church of Rome, but they could open their mouths in the Church of England.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>PRAYER-BOOK.REVISION.</head>
        <p>PRAYER-BOOK.REVISION.</p>
        <p>On the above subject, the Rev. J. W. TIMER, one of the editors of the Dictionary of the Prayer-Book, produced three revisions—the American, Irish and Scotch. He said the Prayer-Book needed re-arr,nsing very much. If they were to have a revision it would only commend itself to Churchpeople if they left doctrine alone. To the uninitiated the Prayer Book was a mystery. He hoped if Convocation proposed to alter it they would not allocate it to a committee. In the revised Prayer-Book they wanted a number of extra prayers, which would be suitable for all events and conditions, such as harvest festivals, &amp;c.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>250-YEAR-OLD BOOK.</head>
        <p>250-YEAR-OLD BOOK.</p>
        <p>Canon MORLEY STEVENSON, who collaborated in the compilation of the Dictionary of the Prayer- Book, said it was 250 years since the book was reN ised, and the time was now ripe for wise changes to be made. He thought they lost by having the Psalms arranged as they were at present. Some Psalms were more fitting for public worship than others. He would also like to see suitable chapters of the Bible chosen for the second lesson on the Sabbath Day. They should make the Prayer-Book as simple as pos- sible for the ordinary man in the congregation. The Rev. E. C. E. CARLETON said they should remember the great risks and dangers they would have to face if they entered upon a ques- tion of that kind. They would have two Prayer-Books going, both of a different charac- ter. They should rest and be thankful with what they had. Canon STEVENSON: The process of revision is going on now. The RECTOR said they would have to approach the revision of the Communion Service with great care.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>DIOCESAN FINANCE. The Irish Question Discussed.</head>
        <p>DIOCESAN FINANCE. The Irish Question Discussed.</p>
        <p>The presided in the evening, when t Finance Scheme and "The e Duty of a Patriotic Churchman to Ireland" the Rector again were discussed. The Rev. B. H. WATTS, Earlestown, opened the discussion of the Diocesan Finance scheme. The scheme had received better support than was expected. The getting of the money would be all right, but if they were going to sto- p there the scheme would fail. The !des, was that of binding all the Churchpeople into one common bond and make the business part of the Church of England as successful as the commercial part of England was. They needed to educate all Christians to give regularly and proportionately to their means. There was no doubt that the reason many did not give regularly was because they could only afford a very little, and this they considered too small to give. There were a good many people who did not know how the Church of England existed, they had no idea as to how clergymen became 'clergymen; all they did know was that the clergymen came and went. They should all knoll: what became of the clergy who lived in their parish. Some found their way into the Workhouse. 'she form cf the scheme brought two Impor- tant alterations in parochial and rural deanery life. First it seemed impossible to work the scheme without a Parochial Council, and secondly a roll of members would be necessary, for although they got as far as they could a roll of communicants and of church attenders, they had no regular roll as a rule. Although it seemed a simple thing, it was, nevertheless, an important step when they asked for the money of the diocese to pass through their treasurer's hands. The clergy whose livings were miser- able might receive some compensation from the fund. They could not appeal to their own con- gregations and unfold the horrors of clerical poverty, for the congregations had their own troubles. They heard of cases where men were actually starving in their vicarages and unable to give their children the opportunities they would like. They did not want to feel that men who had given up their life for God in the Church of England had not only sacrificed their Own life, but the happiness of wife and children.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>TEACHING CHURCHMEN.</head>
        <p>TEACHING CHURCHMEN.</p>
        <p>Mr. T. H. SMETHURST said the scheme would be the means of teaching Churchmen that they were members of a Church which was larger than their own parish, and thus do away with the spirit of parochialism, which was very much</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>Pll</head>
        <p>Pll</p>
        <p>Keeping House (St Keeping We</p>
        <p>The woman who "keeps house" ha i enough to do when she is in good, sound health; but if she is weak, tired all the time, and suffering from morn to night with an aching back, house work becomes a heavy burden. Doan's Backache Kidney Pills have brought new life, health and strength to thousands of women afflicted in this way. Women are subject to kidney disease. The clothing they wear—the work they do, with its worry and strain—the lack of proper exercise— all tend to it.</p>
        <p>Backache, bearing-down pains, , headache, dizziness, faintness, fits of "blues," are common symptoms of kidney disease. When any one of these ills appears, together with irregularities in the action of the kidneys, it is evidence that the kidneys are weak and require help. Doan's Backache Kidney Pills have helped thousands of weak women through trying times when kidney disease means so much added misery. They do not disturb stomach or bowels and contain no poisonous, dangerous nor habit- forming drugs. They are a safe medicine for women and men, and their success is due to the fact that the formula is good, and that Doan', Pills are a special medicine for kidney an bladder diseases only.</p>
        <p>CANAL ROMANCE.</p>
        <p>A Warrington</p>
        <p>Opinion. • Mrs. C. Porter, of 51, Marbury-street, Latch- ford, Warrington, says: "I caught a severe cold on my kidneys about six months ago, and as a result ,I began to suffer with fearful stabbing pains in the small of my back. The attacks would come on very suddenly, and nearly brought me to the ground at times. It felt as if I was being stabbed with knives. "When I got to bed the pain was so bad that I could not turn over. I got up in the mornings feeling as tired as if I had just done a day's work. I was sleepy and listless all the time— all my energy seemed to leave me. "There were other distressing signs of kidney weakness, so I began a course of Doanfs Back- ache Kidney Pills, and soon I began to feel better. My back did not ache so much, and as I persevered with the Pills the pains left rae altogether, and soon I was quite well again. I heartily praise Doan's Pills, for they have done me a power of good. (Signed) "C. PORTER."</p>
        <p>Iss</p>
        <p>Street, London, W.</p>
