      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>NOAH'S</head>
        <p>NOAH'S</p>
        <p>boxes only, six boxes 1319. 41</p>
        <p>to be deplored It would cause people to think that they were interested in the larger Church. In his opinion the Diocesan Authorities were, for the first time, really making an effort to interest the laymen.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>HOME RULE ATTACKED.</head>
        <p>HOME RULE ATTACKED.</p>
        <p>The lasi item for consideration was " The Duty of a Patriotic Churchman to Ireland." Mr. W. H. STANSOIELD made a strong attack upon the Home Rule Bill, and stated that a number of the Nationalists themselves were opposed to the measure. Reference was made to the secret societies in Ireland which defied the law, and to boycotting, and Mr. Stansfield pointed out that under the Union the country had developed wonderfully. Home Rule was vigorously opposed, not only in Ulster, but by all sections who had anything to lose. The most progressive men in Ireland were opposed to the Bill. He quoted instances from Ireland and Canada of the religious bigotry of the followers of the Church of Rome.</p>
        <p>GREATEST CRISIS. Mr. A. J. JOLLEY said they were standing very near one of the greatest crises they had ever known, and they would have to go to the bed- rock of religion before they could get a settle- ment. The Rev. E. C. E. CARLETON also attacked the Bill. The RECTOR said he had a remedy which would be difficult to apply. (Laughter.) It was to teach true Catholicism. He would like the Nationalists or Roman Catholics to take the motto "Pro Rege et Patri," and to take "patri" as including the British Isles. He would like the Protestants and the Ulster men to take the motto "Pro Deo et Ecclesia" ("For God and the Church "). He wished them to look upon the Church as the Holy Catholic Church of Christ.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>GRAMMAR SCHOOL. Education Committee Deputation to Meet Board of Education.</head>
        <p>THE GRAMMAR SCHOOL. Education Committee Deputation to Meet Board of Education.</p>
        <p>The position of the Grammar School in regard to the grant from the Education Committee was again mentioned at the meeting of the Educa- tion Committee on Monday—Councillor H. Roberts presiding. The School Management Sub-committee had passed the following: "The committee proceeded to the deferred consideration of the correspond- ence with the Board of Education with refer- ence to the proposals of the Governors of the Grammar School, and after discussing their relation to the general provision of secondary education in the borough, it was resolved: "That the Board's suggestion that a confer- ence be held between the autllorit Y and the Board be accepted; and that the Chairman of the Education Committee (Councillor Roberts), the Deputy-Chairman of the Education Com- mittee (Mr. Jolley), the Chairman of this com- mittee (the Rev. Canon Stevenson), Mr. John Upson and the Director of Education be ap- pointed to meet the Board for this purpose.'" Mr. Burgess, the Grammar School represen- tative, inquired if the Chairman could furnish any additional information. Councillor Roberts It is all set out in the minute. Mr. Burgess said that the Board of Education had asked whether it was the intention of the local education authority to continue their pres- ent guarantee of grant to the school. He wished to know what had been done in the matter? The Rev. Canon Stevenson said the object of the deputation was to discuss that very question with the Board. Councillor Roberts: That is so.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>CHILD IN TORTURE WITH RASH.</head>
        <p>CHILD IN TORTURE WITH RASH.</p>
        <p>40, Barlboroug,li-street, New Cross. London, S.E., England.— My little girl three years old came out in a rash all over her face. The irri- tation was so great that she used to scratch so much the skin broke out into running sores. It spread all over her head and body. The torture was terrible; I had to tie her hands to keep her from scratching. It still got worse. She could scarcely see out of her eyes. I had no rest night or day with her and the disfigurement was so bad I was ashamed to have my friends see her. I began to get worried. "I tried several cheap ointments, but it still got worse. I began to think it would prove fatal when a neighbour told me of an advertisement of Cuticura, Soap and Ointment, and I at once sent for a sample. The first application re- lieved her and I bought a supply. After using four or five boxes of Cuticura Ointment and the Cuticura Soap my little girl was completely cured." (Signed) Mrs. A. Atkins, Jan. 21, 1914. Although Cuticura Soap and Ointment are sold by chemists throughout the world, a sample of each with 32-p. Skin Book will be sent post free upon request. Address post card F. NewberY and Sons, 27, Charterhouse-square, London, E.C.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>BURTON WOOD GUN AFFAIR. Accused Remanded on Bail.</head>
        <p>BURTON WOOD GUN AFFAIR. Accused Remanded on Bail.</p>
        <p>Accused of shooting Emma Wood and other persons at Burtonwood, John Weston, Jackson - street, Burtonwood, again appeared before Mr. J. Bailey at the Warrington Occasional Court on Tuesday. Superintendent Forster said that Mrs. Wood was progressing favourably when asked by Mr. Browne, who appeared. for prisoner. He was hoping that all the witnesses would be in such a state of health as would enable them to be present in a week. Mr. Browne applied for bail and said that the defence was that his client thought that -there was a blank cartridge in the gun. Asking for bail, he might say that although he nominally represented the prisoner, he held the unique position of being instructed by nearly the whole of Burtonwood, who thought the affair was an accident. The feeling in Burtonwood was very much in favour of the prisoner, and the people believed there was no intention of doing any harm. The husband of the woman who was injured had instructed him that day. The Superintendent did not object to bail of four sureties of XlO each and prisoner in a simi- lar sum, and it was granted, prisoner being re- manded for a week.</p>
        <p>rCAN'T BEAR TO HEAR YOUR CHILD CRY. Keep his stomach in order and you will soon find his temper improved. Next time he seems irritable, try a Steedman's Powder. Nothing else is just as good. Observe the EE in Steedman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>Developments in Shipping During</head>
        <p>Developments in Shipping During</p>
        <p>the Past Twenty Years.</p>
        <p>STRANGE VESSELS ON SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>"EXAM I NER "SPECIAL.</p>
        <p>Twenty years past on Thursday the Man- chester Ship Canal was. officially opened for traffic. On 21st May, 1894, Queen Victoria de- clared the stupendous task of cutting a water- way into the heart of the country completed when she severed a ribbon at the Manchester end of the Canal. Not since the opening of the Manchester and Liverpool Railway had such an epoch-making event occurred in this part of the country, and naturally the Canal opening was of more interest to Warrington from the fact that it passed the door of the town. -</p>
        <p>_ In books recording the doings of engineers, the cutting of the Canal is spoken of as a romance. Rightly so, for at that time it was the greatest feat in canal-making the world had seen—even greater than the French feat of cutting the Suez Canal. If prior to 1894 it was a romance of engineering, since that date we have seen unfolded in many tableaux the romance of commerce, and we have not yet come to the end of the chapter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>WARRINGTON'S LOST CHANCE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON'S LOST CHANCE.</p>
        <p>Thanks to the Canal Manchester now ranks as the fourth shipping port in the country. It is generally conceded that Warrington might, had it been live enough at the time, have had a share in the shipping prosperity. As a distri- butory centre, Warrington, tapped by five different railway companies, stands in a unique position, and had a dock been provided when the Canal was made, who knows but Warring- ton may now have been a port of some im- portance?</p>
        <p>Except for the lay-by at Walton, chiefly used for timber disembarking, Warrington has no means of utilising the waterway at its door. Manchester gets all the trade, and it is rather curious how goods destined for our borough are carried past our door and then sent back.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>TYPICAL EXAMPLE.</head>
        <p>A TYPICAL EXAMPLE.</p>
        <p>The Jersey potato trade, which has com- menced, furnishes a good example. Ships laden with new potatoes from the favoured isle in the Channel, weekly pass up the Canal to Man- chester. There the baskets of tubers are dis- charged, to be trucked to Warrington, and to pass through Warrington to all parts of North Wales. It is the same with the fruit vessels which come in before Christmas.</p>
        <p>One day in Manchester docks I saw a large quantity of goods manufactured in Warrington arrive by truck to be laden into the hold of a liner bound for Australia, where, by the way, the farmers greatly appreciate the wire-netting made in Warrington as the only means of keep- ing at bay the thousands of rabbits ready to strip bare the cropped fields. Thus the cost of carriage to Manchester was entailed, whereas if Warrington had a dock—why need one specu- late upon that point any further.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>CRAFT OF THE WORLD.</head>
        <p>CRAFT OF THE WORLD.</p>
        <p>I was reading the views of one of the chief promoters of the Canal scheme a short time ago, and speaking in the early "80's," he said that if the Canal was used primarily by coastwise traffic and a few oversea ships to bring in cot- ton for the Lancashire mills, and thus save a " break in bulk" at Liverpool, the creation of the Canal would be justified. He is now dead, probably the last of the pioneers of the scheme, but he lived long enough to see his desire grati- fied. To-day the Ship Canal is almost an inter- national waterway, although in the centre of England. Craft from all over the world slowly plough their way up to Latchford, to be lifted from comparatively salt water into the turgid mixture of the waters from the Mersey and Irwell, by one of the two simple locks situated at Latchford.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>SOME STRANGE SNIPS.</head>
        <p>SOME STRANGE SNIPS.</p>
        <p>—...-- Wise in their generation, the engineers made allowance for growth in the size of shipping, and it is well they did so, otherwise the 10,000 ton vessels now fairly frequent could not have made the passage. Twenty years ago the ad- vent of a 2,000 ton vessel aroused interest; to- day the 10,000 ton vessels pass along without comment. The latter may not be the limit in weight; the limit in width of beam, however, was reached a few weeks ago by the passage of a vessel which had only 18 inches clear way in Latchford's largest lock. Stranger craft than that bulky giant have passed along the waterway. Many of my readers will remember the furore created by the 'passage, several years ago, of a flotilla of torpedo boat destroyers, the first time vessels rated as warships ever went so far inland.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>REAL BATTLESHIP.</head>
        <p>A REAL BATTLESHIP.</p>
        <p>Mr. Winston Churchill celebrated his appoint- ment as First Lord of the Admiralty by going one better than his predecessor. He sent a cruiser with attendant destroyers up the Canal to Manchester. This happened two years this Whitsuntide, and one need not recall to memory the scene witnessed as the cruiser Bristol slowly threaded her way up to Latchford on the after- noon of 24th May, 1912. _ _</p>
        <p>The visit was admitted to be an attempt' to boom recruiting for the Navy, and it proved a success. Every Lancashire town, including Warrington, provided its quota as a result of the visit. Still we have naval auxiliaries pass- ing periodically along the Canal in the shape of the armed merchantmen, Shropshire, Argyll- shire, Wiltshire, &amp;c. Twenty years ago it was never dreamt that the Navy would voyage along the Canal, nor that such ponderous craft would use the water. What may happen in another two decades no one can predict. Perhaps motor driven boats may out-number the steam propelled vessels. Already motor driven vessels have made their appearance on the Canal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>A Nonconformist Chaplain for the Warrington Workhouse.</p>
        <p>A FORMER INMATE'S FORTUNE.</p>
        <p>A Nonconformist chaplain is to be appointed to conduct services at the Warrington Work- house. This was the decision of the Guardians on Wednesday, when the new Chairman, Mr. A. J. Jolley, presided. The minutes of the House Committee stated that it was recommended the committee sub- mit to the Board the urgent necessity of ap- pointing a Nonconformist minister to attend to the religious ministration of the Noncon- formist inmates in the hospital, who would also be responsible for the holding of a public religious service in the Workhouse one evening each week for the benefit of all the Nonconform- ist inmates who might care to attend. The minister to be appointed is to be given a salary such as the Board might think adequate for the service rendered, and the Warrington Free Church Council are to be requested to nominate one of their ministers and submit the name of the person nominated to the Board for approval. The minutes were approved</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>SYMPATHY FOR THE HORSE.</head>
        <p>SYMPATHY FOR THE HORSE.</p>
        <p>The -it-ev: A. -Ai. -MITCHELL": ;her.i—th;Minutes dealing with the Padgate Homes Committee came up, said arrangements should be made to convey the members to the Homes without put- ting undue strain upon the 'bus horse. (Laugh- ter.) On Tuesday ten members were conveyed in a heavy 'bus with one horse. It was wrong to treat a horse so.</p>
        <p>Mr. Hums: You should walk. The Rev. A. M. MITCHELL said he had only been twice to Padgate Homes, so had not delayed lin calling attention to the matter. Members would desire that the horse should be treated with more consideration. He suggested that a lighter conveyance and a stronger horse or two horses should be provided, so that the members "could go out in proper style." (Laughter.) He thought Guardians should be humane to their finger tips. (Laughter.) The CHAIRMAN said some of the new members were still in the first burst of their enthusiasm, and that accounted for the large attendance. (Laughter.) He had known the time when they could hardly get a quorum. Mr. Gomm.: In contradiction to what Mr. Mitchell said, I noticed that the horse ran away the other day in the direction of Winwiek. (Laughter.)</p>
        <p>The Rev. A. M. Unarm,: Mr. Garnett is surely drawing upon his imagination. (Laugh- ter.)</p>
        <p>CONTRACTORS' EXTRA COSTS. The Building Committee reported the receipt of a letter from Messrs. W. and S. Owen, archi- tects, enclosing a letter from Messrs. W. and A. Ashton, contractors for the new dining hall, asking for an additional grant to the contract price on account of the employers' payments under the Insurance Act and the increased rate of materials and wages. The contractors pointed out that the contract was signed before the _ Insurance Act came into operation, and no allowance was made. This cost the firm £6O on the contract, and the increase in the cost of materials and wages was £9O. The .committee recommended that £5O be granted in respect of the increased charges. This the meeting agreed to, and the proposal was adopted. •</p>
        <p>BOUNDARY QUESTION. Mr. Bottomley, the clerk, read a letter from the Clerk of the Lancashire County Council, in which it was stated that the Local Govern- ment Board suggested that the Lancashire and Cheshire County Councils should confer about the boundary of the counties between Warring- ton and the High Level Bridge at Warburton. The letter suggested that the Board should send delegates to a meeting to be held at Pres- ton on the subject. Mr. MATHER was glad at the suggestion, and thought the counties ought to come to an amicable arrangement. He moved that the Chairman, Mr. Furness and Mr. Bottomley represent the Board at the Preston meeting. Mr. 31cliixLmr: Then there will be some talk about deputation expenses at the end of the year. _</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN: The boundary is a question which wants dealing with firmly. The CLERK: Lancashire has been trying for years to come to an amicable settlement, but Cheshire would not look at it. The proposition of Mr. Mather was carried unanimously</p>
        <p>FORMER INMATE'S FORTUNE. The CLERK reported a settlement in a case where an old man named Marsh, after taking. his discharge from the Workhouse, died and left £350. lie had struck the name of his wife out of the will, and the money was left to a brother. As Clerk to the Board he had taken the matter up on behalf of the wife, and the matter had now been settled, the woman re- ceiving £225, plus the furniture and a few articles of jewellery. The other side received the remainder of the money, but had to pay the funeral costs and the outstanding debts of the deceased. The CUAIRDIAti : I think Mr. Bottomley to be congratulated on the action h_jute s taken on behalf of the woman. (Applause.) ought</p>
        <p>ASSISTANT CLERK'S SALARY. On the motion of the CIIAIR3IAN, it was unani- mously agreed that the salary of Mr. R. I. Partridge, first assistant clerk, be advanced by ..t2O a year. •</p>
      </div>
