      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>FRENZIED OPPOSITION.</head>
        <p>THE FRENZIED OPPOSITION.</p>
        <p>It is clearly a case of win, tie, or wrangle with the Unionist Opposition. The onward march of a great and just cause, which involves the granting of justice to the Irish people, has, long since, made winning from a re-action- ary's point of view impossible. So the Opposition have fomented rebellion in Ireland and tampered with the honour of the Army in an endeavour to raise up the conditions of a tie. But they have failed also in that. Now they are in the throes of the wrangle stage, which does not stop short of the " Leader" of the party insulting the SPEAKER Of the HOUSE OF COMMONS. Mr. ASQUITH had promised to bring forward an Amending Bill, and. he said its terms would be an- nounced ,later in the House of Lords. The disappointed hooligans, who think they have a right to know off-hand the PREMIER'S inmost thoughts, began to sing "Adjourn," and when he had toler- ated five minutes of the harmony the SPEAKER asked MT. BONAR LAW if the demonstration was being carried on with his consent and approval. Union- ists shouted to their " Leader " not to ,answer. Obedient, as a leader should De, Mr. BONAR LAW told the SPEAKER that he would not presume to criticise what the SPEAKER considered to be his duty. But he (Mr. LAW) knew his duty, • and that was not to answer any such question. So the " Leader "of one of the most impotent Oppositions known to the House of Commons, has added to the accusation of the PREMIER that he was lying, the intimation that the SPEAKER does not know his position__ which is making considerable progress for one who was given the leadership not because it was his due, but as a peace offering to other and more power- ful rivals for the post. The hypocrisy i of t all is too palpable for anything. Concession after concession has been made to this rowdy crew, only to be met by such declarations as Sir EDWARD CARSON'S that they are "not out for a settlement." The Unionists have not made one effort, in fact, to meet the concessions that have been held out. But they had made another use of them LORD WOLMER, this week, speaking on the Welsh Disestablish- ment Bill, evidently lamented the fact that .Wales had not been able to arm in opposition to that measure. "13v arming themselves," he said, "the people of Ulster had been able to extract concessions from the Government Which Welsh Churchmen had not been able to extract." So that, even LORD WOLMER admits that concessions have been made, such as would be worth• fighting for in Wales. But Mr. LAw and his party have presented an abso- lutely deaf ear to reason. That is the fact the electors will remember when the next appeal is made to the country. We want rational statesmen, and not unruly, arrogant showmen at West- minster.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <p>BIRTHS. MARRIAGES. DEATHS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>PASSING WEEK.</head>
        <p>THE PASSING WEEK.</p>
        <p>TIIE POLITICAL SITUATION.</p>
        <p>It is some time since Mr. Harold Smith chal- lenged Mr. Crosfleld to fight the next election upon the question of Home Rule, and Mr. Cros- field, in reply, said that he would undertake no such foolish engagement. By the next election, Mr. Crosfield foretold, • Home Rule, as a political controversy, would be a dead issue, and Warrington would look remark- ably ridiculous struggling on a 'question that was settled, whilst the rest of the country was fighting upon the great and vital question of land reform. Mr. Crosfield's words are being vindicated, and the policy he outlined for the next contest enters upon its active stage neXt Monday. Home Rule, Welsh Disestablishment and all the other great measures at which the present Government aimed at passing imme- diately will soon be accomplished facts, bay the Opposition bloodhounds never so furiously. No more time is to be wasted, locally, upon them; it is now to be full steam ahead with what may be called live politics. The burdens under which most of us suffer • through land laws that have been made by landlords are to be passed in review, and the proposals the Liberals have for remedial treatment will be discussed at a great land campaign, the first instalment of which will begin next Monday, and go on for nearly a fortnight. Mr. Cros- field will be present, and some well-known speakers will support him.</p>
        <p>CHILDRENAND CINEMA SHOWS. Very few people will be found to cavil at the decision of the Education Committee to do all that in them lies to prevent the many ills that the conditions connected with the picture show houses are capable of inflicting upon children. The few opponents of the scheme for lessening the dangers argued along three lines— Why single out the picture shows? Why single out the poor children? Why interfere with the liberty of the subject? kll these arguments are as unsubstantial as it is possible for them to be. The picture shows are not necessarily singled out, but if the cham- pions of the liberty of the subject wish to see this interference extended to other Places they can have their contradictory requirements allowed. A beginning is being made here, because the picture shows are so popular, and the need for action is so palpable. Mr. Long. land effectively point-id out that the poor chil- dren were not singled out, for if children under fourteen were excluded from the cinema houses during the time the elementary schools were open, all children under that age would be excluded, whether rich or poor. The "liberty of the subject" argument is about the weakest in the discussion. There are many things in respect of which some subjects do not deserve to have freedom, and in the interests of the State that freedom is gradually being withdrawn. The Education Committee think the subject should be restrained, in the interest of the State, from allowing his child to sit in cheap places so near to moving pictures as to blind the child. They therefore ask tile Watch Committee not to grant any licences unless the picture house managers undertake to comply with certain moderate and necessary conditions. That children suffering from, or flaying been in contact with, infectious disease 6hould be allowed to mix at will with healthy people in crowded places, after having been excluded from school, would be to allow the liberty of the subject claim to run very mad indeed.</p>
        <p>TIIE VALUE OF THE CINEMA. There can be no doubt that the cinemato- graph is a valuable educational form, and, under proper rules and regulations, an accept- able auxiliary for the teacher. The cinema managements might devise a scheme for the special treatment of children by, say, arrang- ing for shows At reasonable hours , With pictures only of a kind suitable for those of tender years,</p>
        <p>, Shows at which the ordinary seats, and not only those near the screen, would be available. Such shows might be patronised during school hours by permission of the Education Committee, and the proprietors would lose nothing by the provision. A head master, giving his opinions on this subject, in last Sat- urday's "Schoolmaster," says that a great in- strument of culture is left unused by the schools of England, and that instrument is the cinema; and, after having advocated special houses for children, he proceeds</p>
        <p>" Better still it would be, of course, if every education authority built itself a central cine- matograph hall for the use of groups of schools. The expenses need not be heavy, nor need the initial cost be great. And we might have an educational society producing tutorial films to go on pilgrimage from town to town. Masters can act on their own initiative and get permis- sion to use the school hall. Expenses of pro- duction can be met, as I have met them, by a series of special cinema evenings for parents and friends. They crowd down and experience has convinced me that the hunger for good pictures, of real educational value, is very deep."</p>
        <p>" KINEENIA." Mr. Kingdon startled the Education Com- mittee on Monday night by flinging at the heads of the members a ripw way of pronounc- ing cinema. It is a word which has given a deal of trouble since its introduction to com- mon usage. People sometimes use the whole word Cinematograph and sometimes they cut it in two, according to the modern lazy method. Sometimes they make the initial " c " into " s " sound, and sometimes into "k.' Now Mr. Kingdon boldly added another pronunciation difficulty by introducing not an initial, but a medial novelty. He used the "k " and made the "e " after " kin " a distinct syllable, and gave it the long sound, the result being— kin-ee-m a t-ograf. Members laughed and spoke of moving pictures by any other than the name usually used. Mr. Henry Roberts said he would like to hear Mr. Kingdon's word again, and Mr. Parker asked, What was the name of the beast? The word is derived from the Greek " kinematos," motion, which settles the "k," but the " e " is variously pronounced in different English combinations.</p>
        <p>"A HEAL GOOD TIME. An advertisement this week reminds us forcibly of the fact that nowadays the pleasures of a visit to the seaside in summer are not altogether reserved for the rich. The growing fashion of having a proper holiday has gravi- tated to the poorest, thanks locally to the efforts of Councillor W. H. J. Jenkins and his co- workers on the Warrington Children's Sum- mer Camp Committee. Whitsuntide will see the camp brought once again into use, and we hope it has a happy and prosperous season ahead. From now till October the grounds and huts near Prestatyn will be available as a kind of convalescent home for Warrington adults and children upon terms to be had from Coun- cillor Jenkins. There is one interval when the camp is not available for the general public— the month that covers the last portion of July and the first portion of August. That glorious' time is set apart for the fulfilment of the primary object of the camp, to give poor War- rington children a glimpse of the sea and a 1 respite from the smoke-laden slums and slum conditions which they have to endure. Between 500 and 600 poor boys and girls are taken to Prestatyn every year, and the army of those who have "seen life" from a point of view that would never otherwise have been theirs, is now approaching 6,000. That the youngsters appreciate a treat that makes a lasting impres- sion upon their minds is clear from a letter which has just reached Warrington in the fol- lowing terms : Aramoho, New Zealand, 5/4/14. Mr. Jenkins, Dear Sir,—Enclosed 2/6 postal order, a small Easter gift towards outing. May God's blessing rest on you and your committee's noble work. 'Wishing campers a real good</p>
        <p>time.—Yours sincerely, Percy Jarman. Master Jarman, who is evidently one of those who in the past have had a "real good lime" in the neighbourhood of the Fish Mountain, addresses the letter to: "Mr. J. J. Jenkins, sec. or treas., Children's Holiday Camp, Warring- ton, England." The J. J. J. is not quite accu- rate, but it is a fairly good shot at such a distance. We hear that Messrs. Peter Walker and Son have volunteered to do all the repairs needed at the camp, to brick the foundations of the pavilion, and to put the place into good order generally for the coming season—a service which is fully appreciated by the committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>RUGBY.</head>
        <p>RUGBY.</p>
        <p>At the annual meeting of the Warrington Football Club, to be held next Thursday, it will be reported that last season provided a profit of nearly £5OO, which is highly satisfac- tory. The strike left its mark on the results of the games, and echoes of that unfortunate incident may be heard On Thursday. We hope, however, that now it i over it will be forgotten. The strong desire to build up for the future ought to be sufficient inducement to all to bury the past dead. One pleasing feature of the re- port is that which deals with the way in which the committee men attended to their duties. mr. Osborne, who is last on the list of attend- ances, was present at 44 out of 55 meetings, but he represented the club on the Northern Union Committees, and was often called away. Mr. T. P. Bradshaw, the secretary, headed the list with 55, "a highest possible." „</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>PLUCKY SHAKESPEAREANS.</head>
        <p>PLUCKY SHAKESPEAREANS.</p>
        <p>Our local band of Shakespearean players " made " a slight loss by their recent production of "The Merry Wives of Windsor" and "Much Ado About Nothing "—rather an ungracious re- turn on the part of the town. to those who are making so gallant an attempt to popularise the plays of Shakespeare. It is only a small loss, fortunately, and instead of lamenting and help- lessly protesting against the ingratitude of the public, the players have resolved to appeal to the fancies and the pockets a the public by means of a pastoral comedy. The chief danger about acting in pastoral surroundings is the weather, but, as Mr. Law slyly remarks, "an astute manager would cover himself in that respect by selling plenty of tickets in advance." Mr. Mason, who accepted the position of hon. secre- tary to carry the society over a trying time, has, to the regret of the whole company, re- signed. Mr: C. H. Clarke has been elected in his stead.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>MUSICAL SOCIETY AND THE PARR HALL ORGAN. _ _</head>
        <p>MUSICAL SOCIETY AND THE PARR HALL ORGAN. _ _</p>
        <p>While the subscriptions towards the fund for purchasing a new organ for Parr Hall are com- ing in somewhat slowly, it may be pointed out that the Musical Society are doing their duty in the matter. They have themselves opened a list, which is expected to bring in a good aggre- gate contribution, but, in addition to this, about seven-eighths of the amount already raised has been given by members of, or subscribers to, the Musical Society. Really, the Musical Society, who have a very fine orchestra avail- able and are largely independent of an organ, will benefit leps than anyone else by the pro- vision of an organ—which will belong to the public, and will be under the control of the Council.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>WARRINGTON WELSHMEN:</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON WELSHMEN:</p>
        <p>_ The Welshmen in our midst are to have their society—the same as local Scotsmen and local Irishmen. A glance at the names of those sup- porting the movement will give some idea of the good material upon which this new organisa- tion will have a call. May we suggest that the members should imitate their great country- man, Mr. Lloyd George, and act in an original kind of *ay. They might begin by refusing to hold their meetings on a Thursday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>HOLIDAYS. DEAN AND DAWSON'S-WHITSUNTIDE EXCURSIONS.</head>
        <p>THE HOLIDAYS. DEAN AND DAWSON'S-WHITSUNTIDE EXCURSIONS.</p>
        <p>The Great Central Railway Company, through their agents, Messrs. Dean and Dawson, Ltd., have sent us a comprehensive list of excursions which will be run for the forthcoming holidays. Special expresses will be run to the principal points on the company's system, including London (M'bone), South of England, Isle of Wight and Channel Islands (via London), Mid- lands, Nottingham, Loughboro', Leicester, Rugby, Oxford, Stratford-on-Avon, Cleethorpes, Hull, Lincoln, Yarmouth, Lowestoft, Cromer, Eastern Counties, Bridlingtor!, Filey,7S,Caßrrbiodig•oe'. and North-East Coast, Sheffield, r Worksop (fo the Dukeries), Brigg, Grimsby Docks, Rudyard (for Rudyard Lake). The _ week-end and Saturday to Monday tickets will also be extended. Tickets in advance may be obtained at the street, wanadrrifnrgotmon.Mr. Isherwood, 3</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>CINEMA AND RAILWAY ENTERPRISE.</head>
        <p>THE CINEMA AND RAILWAY ENTERPRISE.</p>
        <p>Nearly 3,000 people were present at the Lon- don Coliseum on Wednesday, when a remark- able series of cinema pictures obtained by the Great Western Railway Company telling "the story of the Holiday line" were shown. A pic- ture showing a view on the Great Western Railway main line to Cornwall, taken from the footplate of an engine, received an extraodinary ovation. The pictures showed the country sur- rounding Royal Windsor, Shakespeare-land, the valleys of the Severn and the Wye, North and South Devon a nd t o fSoutihi e- n Cornish Riviera. One of the most attractive films was that depicting Swindon, the engineer- ing centre of the Great Western Railway, in which the construction of a 4-cylinder engine at n Ireland, Wales, Swindon works, the driving of au express pas- senger train, and taking up water at full speed were graphically displayed.</p>
        <p>L. and N.-W. WHITSUNTIDE TRIPS. The L. &amp; N.-W. Railway Company, as usual, offer a wide range of cheap trips for those who mean to travel at Whitsuntide. By this system it is possible to reach almost any part of the kingdom. Trips are to be run, amongst other places, to London, South Coast, West of Eng- land, Scotland, Ireland, Wales, Midland and Eastern' Counties, stations on the North Eastern line, Liverpool, New Brighton, Eastham, Man- chester, &amp;c. Full details can be had at the stations.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>GOLF.</head>
        <p>GOLF.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON CLUB. The second qualifying round for the club prize was played on Saturday. In the first division three members tied with four down each for the first place—Dr. Hutton, Mr. F. S. Horner and Mr. H. Dolan. These three will play off, two to qualify. Mr. H. Woods qualified in the second division with two down, and won the silver memento. Mr. S. Fairhurst also quali- fied with four down. Below are other returns:</p>
        <p>FIRST DIVISION. J. E. Dirties Dr. Peacocke , 5 „ T. B. Handley W. Greenhouse C. H. Unsworth E. A. Connor 7 „ J. M. Murray SECOND DIVISION. A. Bottomley F. Warburton A. Warburton J. 11. Clarke</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>NO SUNDAY GOI</head>
        <p>NO SUNDAY GOI</p>
        <p>Members Decide to Postpone Proposed Innovation.</p>
        <p>STRONG OPPOSITION.</p>
