      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>WELLDON AND CHANGING TIMES.</head>
        <p>WELLDON AND CHANGING TIMES.</p>
        <p>to.l'dlt Must be remembered," Bishop _W. elldon 44 Ira' "Daily Dispatch" interviewer this week 'Etrichester, where there is an outcry against 1 t'4 43' golf and Sunday motoring, "that the 141 i • Nilat.l°ll-8 of life are always changing. Never, cl 14; Ps, have they changed more rapidly than ,beti:tkg the last fifty years. It cannot be ex- .' that with our changing conditions the Illst4lees of society will remain unchanged. For the stress of life in mechanical indus- . I)3' ;47as become, not only by its intensity but s luonotony, so burdensome that men an d MA 414 are inclined to spend their leisure, ti4:,;er on week-days or on Sundays, in relaxa- , ,c)P Welldon recalled the time when people '&gt; 4110 a 5 church because they gained there, or ,t's ' Ileriatt they did, information and instruction, most mtin, hi14rt4153 even interest, which was for •Uttsts unattainable elsewhere. ‘ 4l'vea Performs a considerable share," rTr,"i a he i the e obio: of the work which was once monoP ef a the pulpit. A person desiring the benefit 4 1441011 can read sermons at home as well -4r them in church or chapel. I:3AY OF REST A BLESSING. itf ," When all is said, the desecration of Sun; 'e'enS everywhere indicate a laxity of roll- feeling It means that the thought of Ofe ex's less energetically or habitually upon tkiru",s„teg Christians. Anyone who regards °r herself as being responsible to an 'e!•batliv God cannot wish to live *ithout ' 'To Public acknowledgment of that Power. Itolf ~43end Sunday, then, in a motor car or on tla,-"urse is to lower and weaken the standard 4 ;;tttil „11.4ti0 i nal life. It is selfish conduct, as s tjelt° People who want to amuse or indulge lir ;;Ires without staying to reflect whether d-°11)3117 would be made better or worse. Ri •lor:seerators of Sunday are apt to forget or thtl.l(l. ir'jury which they inflict upon others. i—luk that the public worship of Almighty :11144: beings 4 Primary duty incumbent upon all Sunday is set apart for that la4ellDby the usage of Christendom, and it be e rule of every Christian to attend if possible every Sunday. Sun- ' steY of rest and devotion is an incalcul- ;,,,_ blessing."</p>
