      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY 23, 1914.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY 23, 1914.</p>
        <p>Scarlet Fever.</p>
        <p>There were 63 cases of scarlet fever in the Warrington Hospital last week. Eighteen new cases had been reported.</p>
        <p>Ascension Day:</p>
        <p>Ascension Day. was celebrated by the War- rington schools on Thursday by a general holi- day. Services were held in some of the churches.</p>
        <p>Iristruction for Recruits. This afternoon the Warrington Territorial recruits will proceed to Altcar for instructional practices with the Adjutant.</p>
        <p>Army Films. A special parade for all Territorials will be held on Monday evening, when they will attend at the Star Kinema House to view the British Army film, as recently shown before the King.</p>
        <p>Efficient Territorials.</p>
        <p>The efficiency medal has been awarded to the following local Territorials: Sergeants T. Boss- ward, H. Whitfield, and Private J. Nolan.</p>
        <p>Grammar School Examination.</p>
        <p>An examination will be held at the Boteler Grammar School on 6th June to elect two or more Boteler Scholars, the scholarships cover free tuition and books.</p>
        <p>Children's Summer Camp. The camp at Prestatyn is to be open for visi- tors at Whitsuntide, and particulars can be had from the secretary, Mr. W. H. J. Jenkins, Bank Chambers, Warrington.</p>
        <p>Honour for Crieketer. The Warrington Cricket Club professional, Hannam, has been honoured by the County Com- mittee selecting him to play for Lancashire Second in the match with Cheshire, commencing on Whit Monday.</p>
        <p>Statham Festival. The festival, the proceeds of which will be devoted to the old folk and school children, will be held on Whit Monday at Statham. There will be excellent dancing, music, and other attractions. Holiday-makers should flock to Statham for this pretty festival.</p>
        <p>Warringtonians' Success. Of the ten candidates who were successful in passing the recent meat and food inspection examination at the Liverpool University, two were Warringtonians. These were Messrs. G. W. Lloyd and J. Monks, both Corporation sani- tary inspectors, the former being qualified. Death in the Tea-cup. An inquest was held by Mr. P. A. Jones, deputy coroner, on Thursday, on Ruth (7), daughter of Robert Houghton, bricksetter's labourer, 166, Evelyn-street, who died in the Warrington Infirmary on Wednesday night as a result of scalds received on the chest from a cup of tea being spilt upon her.—A verdict of "Accidental death" was returned.</p>
        <p>Tramway Men's Wages. ' The tramway men, at a meeting early on Sun- day morning, appointed a deputation to meet the Tramway and Electricity Committee on the wage question. The committee offered a id. per hour increase to meet the loss sustained in wages by the suspension of the Sunday morning services, but the men asked for a half-penny. The guards state that the new arrangement means a loss of about Bs. per month to them. Lovely-lane Council School. At the meeting of the Education Committee on Monday Mr. Fairclough said four sets of plans had been received for the new school in Lovely-lane: No. 1 had been accepted subject to the detailed specification submitted receiving the approval of the Surveyor. In the event of No. 1 not fulfilling the condition of the com- petition, the Committee would consider the plan numbered 3. He could not give the names, because the envelopes had not yet been opened.</p>
        <p>First " Examiner " Printer Dead. Mr. Albert Edward Smith, printer and news- agent, Haydock-street, died last Thursday at the age of 68, having gradually declined in health since he lost his wife, a few months before. Mr. Smith was one of the employes at this office when the first Examiner"" was printed in 1868, but for the last thirty years he had been a printer on his own account. He was well known in friendly society circles, and at the funeral at the Cemetery on Sunday several lodges sent representatives to do honour to his memory.</p>
        <p>No Education Deputation Yet. At a meeting of the General Purposes Com- mittee of the Education Committee on Monday, the Chairman (Councillor Henry Roberts) re- ported that at a meeting of the Town Council he had, on behalf of the Committee, promised that a resolution referring to the deputation to the Board of Education with regard to the further recognition of Wycliffe temporary Council School and to the supply of school accommodation in the borough generally, should be reconsidered. It was resolved that the operation of the reso- lution be deferred sine die.—The minute was approved later by the Education Committee.</p>
        <p>Bishop of Liverpool Visits. Dr. Chavasse, Lord Bishop of Liverpool, preached at Holy Trinity Church on Sunday evening to a large congregation, taking as his text Deuteronomy vi. , " Thou shalt love the Lord thy God with all thine heart, and with all thy soul, and with all thy might." The Bishop went on to describe the four staves of the golden ladder by which Christians could climb to love God or to love Him more: (1) Thirst for God; (2) faith; (3) service; (4) love of one fellow creatures. The teaching of the Bible was that if we longed intensely for any spiritual gift, sooner or later it would come to us. The collections taken at both morning and evening services were devoted to wiping off the deficit of £lOO on the day school fund.</p>
        <p>The Leather Trade. Reports all round are very favourable as re- gards the trade of last week (says "The Leather World "), and although here and there a few are complaining that the market is not so active as it should be, it is generally admitted there has been a great improvement during the last couple of weeks, and that the demand is now showing a strength and a magnitude such as we have not seen for some time. A feature very marked was the disposition on the part of consumers to look beyond their immediate needs, and at to-day's prices many are open to cover some months ahead, hut tanners are not very encouraging, and are generally against offering , anything other than prompt or very short dates. 1 There is certainly a general feeling that prices will be on a higher level soon, and with stocks so small and foreign competition for the time entirely out of the reckoning, it has to be ad- mitted there are substantial grounds for this view.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>Life and Death.</head>
        <p>Life and Death.</p>
        <p>The births in Warrington last week numbered 38, giving a rate of 27.7, compared with 23.9 in the same week last year. The deaths totalled 21, giving a rate of 14.5, compared with 13.3 last year. New School Manager.</p>
        <p>Mr. Josiah Longland having resigned his posi- tion of representative manager of St. Peter's School, Mr. James Jarman was appointed, at the monthly meeting of the Education Committee held on Monday evening, to fill his place.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>Territorials' Parade.</head>
        <p>Territorials' Parade.</p>
        <p>The 4th Battalion South Lancashire Regiment held the first battalion parade of the season on Saturday in Appleton Park, by permission of Colonel Lyon. They numbered 14 officers and 200 rank and file. Owing to the illness of Lieutenant-Colonel Fairclough the battalion was commanded by Major F. M. Appleton.</p>
        <p>Mayor's German Trip. Having accepted the invitation of the Ober- burgermeister of Cologne, and the British Con- sul, conveyed through the British Municipal Society to the Mayors of England to visit Cologne during the week preceding Whitsuntide, on the occasion of the exhibition of German in- dustry, handicrafts, commerce, &amp;c., the Mayor will leave shortly for Germany.</p>
        <p>An Old Warrington Cricketer. Mr. T. C. Lawton, late of Warrington, captain of the Blackpool Cricket Club, whose age is over 50 years, performed a remarkable bowling feat on his birthday last Saturday. Bowling against the Fylde Eleven, at Whitegate Park, Mr. Law- ton took five wickets with five balls in one over, and seven wickets with 13 balls, having only ono rim scored against him. The feat is to be suit- ably recognised by the Club Committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>Mnsic.in Bank Park.</head>
        <p>Mnsic.in Bank Park.</p>
        <p>Mr. T. J. Down's Military Band, under the conductorship of M!.. Arthur Down, will play the following selection of music in Bank Park on Thursday, commencing at 7.15 p.m.: March, "The Flash of Steel" (Colin); morceau, "Edina" (Vandervell) ; overture, " Hayd6e" (Auber); valse, "La Plus Belle" (Waldteufel); serenade, "Love in Arcady" (Wood); selection, "The Merry Widow" (Lehar); intermezzo, "Little Gadabout" (Colin); polka, " Neu- niarker " (Faust); 'God save the King."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>Smart's College Success.</head>
        <p>Smart's College Success.</p>
        <p>Smart's Business Training College have secured a number of successes in the speed examinations in shorthand held by Messrs. Pit- man and Sons. The results are: 140 words per minute, Miss A. Marsh; 100 words per minute, Misses Lightfoot, Wain, and Coghlan; 80 words per minute, Misses Barlow, Burrows, G. Ball, N. Marsh, Penrice, Ormiston, and Vernon; 60 words per minute, Misses, Harris, Fearnley, M. Longton, and Talbot; special test, Misses Ball and Iris Calvert.</p>
        <p>New Territorial Bounty. A bounty of „t 1 is to be paid to Territorials who make themselves efficient at the 15 clays' camp. To qualify for the new bounty the fol- lowing conditions must be observed: (1) The full number of drills tten) must be performed annually before camp; (2) attendance at camp for full period of 15 days; (3) the annual course must be fired on the open range and a minimum of 30 points obtained. Condition (3) will not be included till 1915, but the result of this year's shooting will' count towards determining the 1915 bounty. After that the musketry quali- fication of each year will count towards the fol- lowing year's bounty.</p>
        <p>2,135 for the Lifeboat Fund. As a result of the Warrington ladies selling cornflowers in all the principal streets on Satur- day, in aid of the funds of the National Life- boat Institution, ,t 135 was realised. The method is more novel and popular than the house-to- house collection, which was laborious, and did not meet with nearly so much success. The principal organisers of Saturday's effort were: Mrs. Broadbent (President), Mrs. Henry Green- all (hon. secretary), and Miss Adams (assistant secretary). A number of school teachers assisted, and Miss Marsh (head of the girls employed at Messrs. Crosfield's) also enlisted a large number of helpers.</p>
        <p>Death and Funeral of Mr. John Coulson The death occurred on Saturday of Mr. John Coulson, of Wilderspool, at the age of 42 years. Mr. Coulson had been employed at Messrs. Fletcher, Russell's for a period of 14 years, having previously spent 14 years in the service of Messrs. Sutton, ironware dealers. He had been ailing for a period of two years from a malignant throat disease, and passed away last Saturday morning. He leaves a widow and three sons. The funeral took place at St. Thomas' Church, on Monday afternoon. Floral tributes were placed upon the coffin from "The Widow and Boys," Mr. and Mrs. William Coul- son, "His Sister and Nieces," "Effie and Gerty," and Mr. Mottram.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>Warrington Actor's Colonial Tour.</head>
        <p>Warrington Actor's Colonial Tour.</p>
        <p>Engaged by Messrs. Williamson to tour South Africa, New Zealand and Australia, Mr. James Jolley, a local actor, sailed for South Africa a week ago, in the "Braemar Castle" from Southampton, with the dramatic company. The tour will open at Johannesburg on June 29th. The repertoire of the company, which is one of the largest ever visiting South Africa or Aus- tralia, consists of "Within the Law," "The Land of Promise," "The Argyle Case," "Bought and Paid For," "The Third Degree," "The Lion and the Mouse," "The Yellow Ticket," and "Paid in Full." Mr. Jolley is the son of Mr. Thomas Jolley and is 27 years of age. He was at one time connected with the Warrington Volunteers Cycle Corps, and filtered the theatrical profes- sion at the Warrington Royal Court Theatre, when it was under the management of the late Mr. Joseph Potter. Later he joined "The Breed of the Treshams" Co., then Mr. Herbert Sleath's Co. in "A White Man," "Deputy Sheriff," &amp;T., and lastly Mr. Robert Courtneidge's Co. in "The Pearl Girl" and "The Arcadians."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>An Artiste's Claim.</head>
        <p>An Artiste's Claim.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County Court on Thurs- day, before Judge Stanger, the Two De Traffords sued Adam King for £4 17s. 6d. for wages due for a week's engagement at the Theatre Royal. —Plaintiff said that he was the last turn on all through the week, and he went out on the Friday night, thinking he would be the last turn again. The programme was altered in two minutes, and consequently he was not there to go on first, as the programme stated. His wife was there, but could not go on by herself.— Defendant said the plaintiff never made any attempt to go on first or last turn on the Friday. He was told to go on first turn. His contract was only for 10s.—James Watson, stage manager, said the programme was not altered. The stage was set ready for plaintiff to to on first. His wife said he had gone to the Hippodrome.—Bert Barker said he had let plaintiff's wife have a " sub " of 10s.—Defendant said plaintiff tried to appear on the Saturday, but he would not let him, as he had broken his contract. He had said he would not go on on the Friday, because he had worked a matinee, for which he had not received payment.—His Honour gave judgment for ..t3 ss. and costs in favour of the plaintiff.</p>
        <p>TENNIS, CRICKET, CROQUET, and other out-door Sports.—J. S. GARLAND, Complete Sports Out- fitter, 51 to 55, Bridge-street, Warrington.</p>
        <p>SPRING CLEANING.-Try the "DAISY" Vacuum Cleaner, 42/-, 63/-, 84/-. Free trial allowed.— J. S. GARLAND, Ironmonger, 51 to 55, Bridge- street, Warrington</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>Preachers for To-morrow.</head>
        <p>Preachers for To-morrow.</p>
        <p>Wycliffe Congregational—Morning and even- ing, Rev. T. T. James. Latchford Baptist—Morning and evening, Rev. J. S. Chedburn. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Bewsey-road Wesleyan—Morning, Rev. J. Gunnell; evening, Rev. H. Middlewood. Christadelphians—Evening, Mr. T. Hobson, Castleton.</p>
        <p>Elmwo-o-d-avenue Congregational (Sunday School Anniversary)—Morning and evening, Alderman W. G. Townend, Middleton; after- noon, Service of Song. Bold-street Wesleyan—Morning, Rev. H. Williamson; evening, Rev. E. Morgan. Legh-street Primitive Methodist—Morning Mr. A. E. Hoyle; evening, Mr. J. Griffiths. St. John's Presbyterian Church (Choir Sun- clay)—Morning and evening, Rev. William Reid. Cairo-street Chapel (Sunday School Anniver- sary)—Morning and evening, Rev. W. H. Drum- mond; afternoon, special service. Golborne-street Baptist (Sunday School Anni- versary)—Morning and evening, Mr. J. Louis Fenn, Liverpool; afternoon, musical service.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>Sun Insurance Company's Profitable --</head>
        <p>Sun Insurance Company's Profitable --</p>
        <p>Year:</p>
        <p>The annual general meeting of the members of the Sun Insurance Office will be held in London on Tuesday. The report of the directors states that there has been carried to pension fund £lO,OOO, to house property account .t 15,000 to writing down sundry investments £25,000, to employers' liability reserve £15,000, and to bur- glary and general reserve £10,000; leaving at credit £500,923 7s. 11d. An interim dividend of 7s. per share, less income tax, was paid in January, absorbing £79,100, and the directors have declared a further dividend of 7s. per share, less income tax, payable on the 3rd of July, which will absorb a further sum of 479,100, leaving unappropriated £342,723 7s. lid. The total funds of the office will now stand as fol- lows: Capital paid up, £480,000; fire fund, £2,128,614 12s. 11d.; accident fund, £8,660 135.; employers' liability fund, £144,139 9s. 9d.; bur- glary and general fund, £24,349 9s. 2d.; dividend reserve £150,000; pension fund, £59,819 10s. 11d.; balance at credit of profit and loss account, after payment of dividend, £342,723 7s. 11d.; making a total sum of £3,338,307 3s. Bd.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>GREAT BENEFIT.</head>
        <p>A GREAT BENEFIT.</p>
        <p>Mr. Warburton's Friends Honour Him at the Royal Court.</p>
        <p>If any evidence were needed as to the popular- ity of Mr. 11. L. Warburton, the energetic and courteous acting-manager of the Royal Court Theatre, it was to be found in abundance at the theatre on Thursday evening, when the second complimentary benefit was given to him, by kind permission of Sir Joseph Beecham and Mr. A. M. Loader. Every part of the house was filled, a fact which must have brought joy into the hearts of those who had set their minds on a "bumper." The programme was excellent, and it seemed almost incredible that so many good things could be crowded into one evening. Members of the Warrington Dramatic Society appeared with success in Stanley Houghton's one-act comedy, "The Dear Departed," in which two married sisters, always at variance, scheme, arrange, steal, and squabble over the effects of the fathvr whom they think. dead. The dia- logue throughout is of a very humorous charac- ter, and the audience is convulsed with laugh- ter when the father comes downstairs after mourning. Miss Bessie Forshaw gave an excel- having had a sleelent portrayal of the part of Mrs. Slater, and p, and sees his daughters in Miss Nellie Mullins made a capital Mrs. Jordan. Mr. H. G. Mosley and Mr. H. M. Ashton, as the husbands of the sisters, brought out the many humorous incidents in a clever fashion. Miss Ida Haddock made a charming tom-boyish Victoria Slates', and Mr. Sidney Wallhead Played the part of the fath.er in a commendable manner. He also acted as stage manager for the comedy.</p>
        <p>Some members of the Warrington Musical Society's Opera section gave very pleasing num- bers, Miss E. Wilkinson, Airs. E. A. Sanders, Mr. J. Babbitt and Mr. J. B. Poole delighting the audience with the quartets "When the bud- and "A regular Royal Wilkinson and Mrs. Sanders gQdaiunvegeenab.'l' bloom pleasing f rleina3dition of the duet "Night of Stars," and in the solos "Buy my strawberries" Sanders and Miss Wilkin- son were also heard to advantage. Mr. Bab- and "Carmenia" Mrs. bitt's effective rendering. of "The Trumpeter," w encore for him, and he responded with "Two greyt h eyes." by Mr.MPI W. Rutter, earned an Poole was vociferously ap- plauded for the able manner in which he sang "Up from Somerset." Mr. W. H. Payton was the accompanist. Mr. Walter Moores, the popular comedian, gave "My word !" in his inimitable way, and also pleased with "You wouldn't know the old place now, in which there was much cause for laughter. "Airs. Murphy on Honesty," given by Mr. H. M. Ash- ton in character, was very entertaining, and Miss Winnie Williams sang "The maid of the fountain" with pleasing effect. By kind Permission of Mr. Frank Macnaghten, Alfred Guest and Victoria Newlyn, a charming pair of tale tellers, as well as Randolph Sutton, a breezy light comedian, also delighted the audience.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>".`,.RAFFLES."</head>
        <p>".`,.RAFFLES."</p>
        <p>In addition to the other numerous items, Mr. Gerald Alexander's company, who, are present- ing "Raffles" all through the week, twice nightly, gave an excellent presentation of this interesting and novel play, which deals with the audacious coups of A. J. Raffles, the amateur cracksman. . The play centres round this gentleman thief, and a pleasant love interest is introduced in a charming manner. Mr. A. W. Tyrer infuses the part of Raffles with the neces- sary elemen.ts of cunning, culture and sang- froid. He is the spirit of the piece and is ably assisted by the other talented members of the company, including Mr. Theodore Kelly as Bed- ford, and Miss Nona Wynne as Gwendolen. An augmented orchestra, under the direction of Mr. Tom Boothroyd, played choice selections in admirable fashion. Miss Cooke and Mr. A. Trow also acted as accompanists.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>PRESENTATIONS AND THANKS.</head>
        <p>PRESENTATIONS AND THANKS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Warburton was the recipient of many gifts and congratulations were showered upon him. In a neat little speech he thanked Sir Joseph Beecham and Mr. Loader for making the benefit possible; the ladies and gentlemen who had so ably contributed to the programme; to the play- goers of Warrington, who had shown him the greatest kindnesses; the orchestra and staff, who were always loyal and devoted; to Mr. Waters and Miss Lowe, hon. stage manager and hon. secretary, for the way they had worked, and all his friends. The honorary acting-managers were: Messrs. Arthur Browne, J. W. Bullen, A. Walker, A. W. Turpin, E. Johnson, A. N. Chester, and H. Burthem. Each lady artiste received a pretty bouquet, handed up by the conductor. The plants, which had been arranged with artistic taste, had been lent by Mr. W. Powell, Fearnhead Nurseries.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>NEXT WEEK'S ATTRACTION.</head>
        <p>NEXT WEEK'S ATTRACTION.</p>
        <p>The attraction for next week is Mr. P. M, Brownmann's American drama, "An Indian Girl's Devotion," a dramatic story of New Mexico, of ranches, and those who live thereon; of cowboys, sheriffs, brave-spirited people of the wilds, dashing heroes, and fascinating heroin. The story has all those elements of excitement</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>REQ. T. FOX AS "BRONCO JACK."</head>
        <p>REQ. T. FOX AS "BRONCO JACK."</p>
        <p>and vivid interest that one has come to look for in plays of western characteristics, and Messrs. M. Glenville and Fred Osmond are pledged i produce the play at the Royal Court Theatre in first-rate fashion. The company is an excellent organisation, and three American thorough-bred horses take part in the play's development, the third act containing one of the most exciting and novel equestrian incidents ever seen.</p>
        <p>Anniversary. at Sankey Bridges. There were rejoicings at Sankey Bridges Wes- leyan Church on Sunday, when the school anni- versary was held and the function was one of Cie most successful in the series held throughout the past 38 years. The special preacher was the famous evangelist, the ißev. Thomas Waugh, of Southport. The service n the morning was held in the Schoolroom, owing to the alterations in connection with the erection of the,new organ in the chapel. The Rev. Thomas Waugh preached a powerful sermon from the text, Col. iii. 16. Special hymns were sung by the choir and children. In the evening, the service was held in the Parr Hall, the building being crowded to its utmost capacity. The preacher's subject was "The elder brother," from the par- able of the Prodigal Son, and he dealt with it in a very original manner. During the afternoon and evening the choir and children rendered special hymns and anthems, under the leader- ship of Mr. Levi Richardson, and the manner in which they acquitted themselves was in keep- ing with past tradition!. Mr. William Pacey ably performed the duties of accompanist. The afternoon service was also held at Parr Hall, the programme being chiefly of a musical character. An address was given by the Rev. H. Middlewood. Mr. J. Wadsworth, of Cadis- head, a former member of the Sankey Bridges Wesleyan Church, presided. Besides the special hymns by the children and choir, the anthem, "Rejoice in the Lord" was sung, and ' solos were rendered by Miss Dodds and Miss Clarkson. In the evening, solos were also given by Miss Brough and Mr. Ashworth. The collections amounted to a little over £39, and the workers decided to make the sum into £4O. Mr. Waugh was delighted with the day's ser- vices, and in response to a hearty invitation to take the services next year he readily assented.</p>
        <p>: .....•.••-•..... . ...••••........•.. ................. . „ ................... . . • •• :</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>MAY QUEEN-ELECT.</head>
        <p>THE MAY QUEEN-ELECT.</p>
        <p>during the week, under the tuition of Miss Spinks—even they are imbued with the " speed- ing up" idea. At a meeting of the committee on Monday evening, when Mr. Hudson presided, the reports of the collectors were received, and it was stated that about £l7 had been raised.</p>
        <p>. 1 ' I ,:f..-'';',i........ -1,.. ' /4: . ....,-..., ,'...'-,t'..'.;:', '...t.:'::::',g.t., ,•... gt,:- . ~..}4i;:.4'......., .....,,... .•...„ , . , ...„.... .. ~.. ....- , ....,,,,,.. . .. . ~ . • , , :, ...•,,.,....•,;,'. i... ';',;`,f .:,;"''''.' .....: ...,.....'„'..,,:?: i '.; '',:,,,,,, . .- ' 'AiO. ' '..h'P.'&lt;•,!'.......',',.);:if.i5:.'-..,•`..7Vt.. • ',•;', ,•... . .5....?•-••,.........i..!'•.•,,..5•••:,:,•".....:',/,':.•;;•:•.,.... • i !"%isti . •7&amp;;:":"•-".'."..' ,#....,1:' ' •'•:•'11:- ' . .. '',. • ~' .:-.. , ' ' ....„:•,..;,•';.'....•.' •, ' ' • ..,,,,,r.!.,,...,-. .. . . ....,-„,fr ~ . .. . .... .„. . . , ~ .., ..,. . . , 7.,. , : - . i</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>OTHER APPOINTMENTS.</head>
        <p>OTHER APPOINTMENTS.</p>
        <p>Additional appointments were made, including the following: Costume secretary, Mr. Sutton; secretary Ladies' Committee, Miss Pearson; Mr. Hudson and Mr. Webb; auditors, Messrs. Blount and H. Brown; Additional Com- mittee, Messrs. Reed, Richard, H Bradburn, and Margotts; whilst Mrs. Scragg's name was deleted, as it was stated she would be unable to serve on the committee.</p>
        <p>NO TRADESMEN'S OR FARMERS' TURNOUTS. It was decided that this year there should be no farmers' or tradesmen's turnouts. This course was taken on account of the danger to the children involved by the presence of horses. The turnouts, it was stated, looked very nice, but they had to consider the risk, for last time there were one or two "narrow squeaks," as one com- mitteeman put it. A further meeting of the committee was held on Wednesday night, when it was approved that Miss Dorothy Pearson be the " queen!,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>PROGRAMME.</head>
        <p>THE PROGRAMME.</p>
        <p>The festival will be held on the Football Ground, Pepper-street, on Whit Thursday. The crowning ceremony will be proceeded by a grand character procession, which will leave the Cross at 2.30. The music will be supplied by the Lymm Subscription Band. Fancy dances will be given by the children, who will also partici- pate in the inevitable Maypole Dance. There will be every facility for the Public indulging in al fresco dancing during the evening.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>THREE MONTHS' ROW. Wild Scenes in Warburton Street.</head>
        <p>A THREE MONTHS' ROW. Wild Scenes in Warburton Street.</p>
        <p>What the Chief Constable described as "a three months' row in Warburton-street, Win- wick-road," was investigated by the Warrington magistrates yesterday—the Mayor presiding. Amongst the articles produced in evidence were three lots of hair alleged to have been torn from women's heads. Ellen Stokes, 37, Warburton-street, was charged with assaulting Mary Pownall, 9, Bowes- street. Pownall said that Stokes "flew at her and pulled the hair out of her head." Stokes: I was not there, so I could not quarrel with you. Several witnesses were called for the defence, all stating that there was no assault committed by Stokes. One woman added that it was she 1 and not Stcrices who should be summoned for the assault on Pownall. The Chief Constable: We'll oblige you with a summons. (Laughter.) For three months there has been nothing but scrapping amongst the women in this neighbourhood. Edward Stokes was then charged with assault- ing Mary Foley. Defendant denied that he had seized the woman by the throat. At the time he said he was in bed. Witnesses were called to show that he was in bed at the time. The Chief Constable said Mary Foley was no friend of the police, but they were satisfied that she was entitled to protection. She had been trying her best to live quietly, but the other people would not let her. When this man Stokes, said the Chief Con- stable gets a bottle of ginger beer, far less beer, he wants to fight the whole neighbourhood. It is altogether the scenes which are to be seen in this neighbourhood. 1 Both defendants had previous convictions and they were bound over in £5 each for six months and ordered each to pay 10s. costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>ROUMANIAN CONSOLIDATED OILFIELDS.</head>
        <p>ROUMANIAN CONSOLIDATED OILFIELDS.</p>
        <p>Well No. 30 in the Bana-Moreni Field., owned by the Roumanian Consolidated Oilfields, Limited, is more than realising expectations. It will be remembered that this is the first well drilled through the Pontic formation, below which a new fountain source was found at a depth of 2,506 ft. Cable from Ploesti, dated May 12th says " Moreni Well No. 30 flowed for twelve hours with terrific force at rate of 2,000 tons a day before sanding up. In view of vio- lent eruption further precautions necessary. Repairing derrick and fitting flowhead before re-starting well; may take few days."-'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>" Messiah " at Star Kinema.</head>
        <p>" Messiah " at Star Kinema.</p>
        <p>There were two exceptionally large audiences at the Star Binema House last night, when "The Messiah," a great coloured masterpiece, was again shown, and Miss Marie Houghton sang in a delightful fashion "1 know that my Redeemer liveth," "Come unto Me," and "How beautiful , are the feet."</p>
        <p>KIPtINGESE. At the outset, the CITA/RMAN apologised for the absence of Mr. P. M. Oliver, who could not be Present owing to an unfortunate misunderstand- ing. On Monday they would commence a land campaign, with meetings all over the town, which would extend to June 10th. Mr. Cros- field would address the meetings and they would also have other brilliant speakers down. He wanted to refer to one prominent man in the Tory party—another man dug up somehow or other—Mr. Rudyard Kipling, who had been calling the Liberals all the names to which he could lay his tongue. Kipling was a bit of a poet, who practically ran the Boer War by his poetry. He bucked them all up, and they all had to bow down to that "Pay! Pay!! Pay!! !" They all paid whether they liked it or not, and the war was paid for up to the hilt. They had lost thousands of men, and now this man was telling them that they were criminals, murderers, and thieves, and that Mr. Asquith was not fit to be at large. He did not know whether the Tories were tired of Carson and his gun-running, or with Bonar Law sitting tight on the bench, or P. E. Smith not coming forward, but they had got a champion now, who had sprung up like a mushroom. He would read an extract from the "Daily News" about this new champion "The speech of Mr. Rudyard Kipling deserves attention, not merely as a symptom of his state of mind, but as an evidence of the spirit which pervades a large section of the opposition. When an outrage of this kind is possible from a man who has some reputation to lose it ds clear that party malevolence has touched .the limits of in- sanity. That it should be perpetrated'at'a tithe when all the reasonable influences on both sides are hoping for a peaceful issue to the conflict adds significance to the incident It shows the reckless incendiarism which still obstructs the path to a settlement. Mr. Kipling's proposition is that the Government are a Government of criminals, who, like his friend the murderer in India, 'started crooked,' and have passed on from infamy to infamy. The root offence of which they are guilty is that they are for the most part poor men who are dependent on their salaries plus what they make in tips.' It was in order to keep their salaries that they entered upon a career of crime. They first passed the Parliament Act, the purpose of which was to secure them in office for five years so that they might draw salaries amounting to £400,000. The next step was to bribe their followers with £4OO a year. This embezzlement of the public funds' was intended to keep the private mem- bers quiet. There remained the Nationalists, of whom Mr. Kipling speaks with all his accus- tomed hatred, and these were bought off with Home Rule. And so one 'crooked step' led to another, until at last the Government conceived the horrible design of a butchery in Ulster. `lf these Ulster cattle could not be sold on the hoof they should be delivered as carcases.' Only the action of the Army saved the Empire, he says. But even now, it seems, this Government of thieves and potential murderers are still plot- ting bloodshed in order that they may continue to draw their salaries."</p>
        <p>Bunkum—All Bunkum!</p>
        <p>Continuing the Chairman said that Kipling ought to be put in some home. He was beating Harold Smith, who was attempting to do an- other silly thing in finding homes for those who were coming from Ulster. He had never heard of anyone offering Harold Smith apartments for that purpose. He (the speaker) had offered that van, but Mr. Smith had never accepted the offer. (Laughter.) They had had enough of that bunkum. Whether they gun-ran, paraded the streets of Belfast with guns and swords, or F. E. Smith led them on his charger, they could go on with their tom-foolery. Two could play at that game. Without any initiative on the part of the Nationalists, Home Rulers were arming themselves, and were prepared to fight the Ulster Volunteers, and they could do it, too. (Hear, hear.) As soon as a couple of gun-boats were sent over the leaders flocked back to England, because they were all afraid, except those who were being led on—whither, they did not know. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>TORY-ULSTER SCHEME</head>
        <p>TORY-ULSTER SCHEME</p>
        <p>To Fence Round Endangered Monopolies and Privileges.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>PROGRESSIVE LABOUR MEETING.</head>
        <p>PROGRESSIVE LABOUR MEETING.</p>
        <p>Another large audience attended at Bridge Foot on Monday, when the Progressive Labour League held a successful meeting, under the presidency of Mr. E. A. Sanders. The speakers were Mr. FL E. D. Mabbott, Manchester, and Mr. E. Wynne Jones.</p>
        <p>The newest champion of the latter-day Toryism, Mr. Rudyard Kipling, as treated in appro- priate fashion, as also were some other Opposi- tion "stars."</p>
        <p>The speeches touching on the land campaign were full of interest, and the various injustices which are to be wiped out were exposed in all their forms.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>DISEASE AND SLUMS TO GO.</head>
        <p>DISEASE AND SLUMS TO GO.</p>
        <p>Mr. IfAssort covered a wide range in his speech, for he reviewed—although of necessity very briefly—the political situation in the country, extending over the last 80 years. They had, he continued, just started on an- other epoch-making period. It was a glorious page in the history of England that they were about to write. They were going to stamp out rampant diseases and slumdom, do away with ignorance and superstition, and bring hope and liberty into the lives of the people who had made England what she was to-day. Turning to the Ulster question, he said that the Conservatives were whipping up the people in Ulster to turn the Government out. They were not so fond of Ulster as all that; they were fond of that ringed fence round their monopoly and privileges which they could see were going to be broken down. (Hear, hear.) The Ulster agitation was only a diversion to direct their thoughts from the measures forecasted in the latest Budget, which was second only to that of 1909.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>What? and Who?</head>
        <p>What? and Who?</p>
        <p>He would ask Rudyard Kipling to try his own methods, for he wrote once that he had got honest serving men, "Who taught me all I know. I send them abroad on my own affairs and they bring me home the news. Their names are What, When, How, Where, and Who." He (the speaker) now asked "What have they done?" WHAT had the Liberal party done since 1906? and WHO had they done it for? There had been no class legislation since 1906. Every measure that had been passed spelled "working classes" from first to last. HOW had they done it? Let Kipling send out his serving men and let them bring home the news of what the Liberal party had done, and HOW. (Hear, hear.) The Liberal party were going still further ahead. They could not stop the popular demand for land reform. (Hear, hear.) They were quite willing to fight the next electiotenhdauotenatttehhdee, land question, because they knew democracy of the country had been and knew who ought to own the land.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>Warrington Rates' Reduction.</head>
        <p>Warrington Rates' Reduction.</p>
        <p>They were going to see that industry and enterprise were taxed no longer. It was going to mean something to Warrington and that was getting near enough home for the audience. The Budget of 1914 would mean practically a reduc- tion in the Warrington rates of threepence, fourpence, or fivepenee in the .2. Land would be easier of acquisition by municipal author- ities. The Conservatives had got a twist on monopoly and privilege, which was born in the blood, and the ballot box was the only way to throw off the shackles of monopoly and privi- lege. The privilege and monopoly of the House of Lords had also to go. What would their girls I think if they had to " pop the question" three times before it was final? (Laughter and ap- plause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>PROPHECY COMES TRUE. No Civil War. Mr. E. 'WYNNE JONES proposed a vote of thanks to the speaker and at the outset paid a tribute</head>
        <p>PROPHECY COMES TRUE. No Civil War. Mr. E. 'WYNNE JONES proposed a vote of thanks to the speaker and at the outset paid a tribute</p>
        <p>(Continued in Tux! Column.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>PRIZES FOR TICKET SELLERS. May Queen Festival Workers Honourecl,</head>
        <p>PRIZES FOR TICKET SELLERS. May Queen Festival Workers Honourecl,</p>
        <p>An enjoyable social was held on Thursday' evening in the Cairo-street Schools by all who took part in the recent May Festival. Tea, of which about 300 children partook, was provided, and served by the lady workers of the Band of Hope Union, after which an entertainment was, given._</p>
        <p>Mr. Thomas Dale, chairman of the Union Committee, expressed his regret at the un- avoidable absence of Mrs. Dodds, their presi— dent, who should have been with them- that night to distribute the prizes for ticket-selling. He was glad to say that all the comments he, had heard upon the festival had been very fav— ourable ones. (Applause.) Speeches were also made by Messrs. Spruce- and Whitfield. These dealt chiefly with the Mayor's suggestion that they should try and get more boy members.</p>
        <p>- During the evening, Miss Eva Tanner (the queen) sang "Glorious Land of Sliring,". "There's a land," and other songs, and Miss. Ada Hamer (rose queen) gave "Homeland" and' "Maggie" very sweetly. The entertainment concluded with a humorous item from. Mr. J. H. Bibby.</p>
        <p>Prize-winners.</p>
        <p>Mr. F. Gandy (secretary) presented handsome book prizes for ticket-selling to the following : Reginald Grounds and Eva Tanner (over £2 55.), Elsie Whitfield and Vera Davies (over .2T 108.), Doris Eland, Hilda R. Taylor and Ada Hamer (over £1 55.), Cyril Ellison, Edith War- hurst, Ida Whitby, Marion Fellows, Lily Jones, Arthur Hamblett, Charles Sankey, and Amy Littlewood (over ,„t1), Gladys Bonnell, Stanley Woolley, Rena Cunnington, Elsie Clark, Jeannie Wright, Hilda Kinsey, Theresa Babe, and Ada Derbyshire (over 155.), Thomas Williams, Annie Williams, Lily Davies, Elsie Chetter, Lily Green, Mabel Allman, Alice Redfern, Alice Wright, Lilian Himsworth, Thomas Lowe, Hilda Hewitt, Frank Edwards, Martha Ferni- val, Florence Smith, Phyllis Lowe, William Houghton, Maggie Randles, Lily Pye, May. Elliott, Annie Rollings, Annie Edwards, and Elsie Plinston (over 108.).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>TORY-ULSTER SCHEME—continued-</head>
        <p>TORY-ULSTER SCHEME—continued-</p>
        <p>to The Examiner," saying that during the last few weeks they had had no meetings, but our good Liberal paper had kept the flag flying. (A Voice: "And will do.") Yes, and would do.</p>
        <p>During the course of those meetings a good many things had been said. Last summer he said there would be no civil war in Ulster. They stated it was mere bunkum, and the events of the last few days would prove to them that that was correct. Whether the Irish question was settled by consent or not, the Liberals said it must be got out of the way. Before the Liberal party appealed to the country Home Rule, jus- tice for the people of Wales, and the Plural Voting question would be settled, and in the future they must have a chance of passing the land measure and other reforms. Immediately after the Chancellor introduced his Budget the Tories were not talking about an election in June, but deputation after deputation waited upon the Premier to ask him not to inflict an Autumn Session on them. (Laughter.) If the legislation foreshadowed in the Budget were to be carried, they would want to sit practically the whole of next year. The Government had made up their minds to settle the plural voter once and for all. Manhood would be the test for the vote in the future, an 4. not rroperty. (Hear, hear.) The next appeal to the country would be made with the plural voter a thing of the past, and that meant that the democracy of the country was going to have a fairer and more reasonable chance than it had had in the past. (A Voice: "What about Grimsby?")</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>Poor Harold!</head>
        <p>Poor Harold!</p>
        <p>--- • Yes! what about it? continued Mr. Jones. We reduced the majority by 420 votes. Suppose we reduce Harold Smith's majority by that, wherq will he be? (A Voice: "Suppose he gets more?") He only makes that statement because he has been in the sun to-day. ("I scrat him off.") They were getting along now. If a good many people scratched him oft—(" There will be more for him.")</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>God's Great Gift.</head>
        <p>God's Great Gift.</p>
        <p>- --- —• • -• After further illustrating the iniquities of the Plural Voting system, Mr. Jones referred to the land question, and said the Government were going to break down•the monopoly in land. once and for all time. If a person were given the power to monopolise land he had the power to monopolise life itself, and no one should have that power. Land could not be created by man. It was God's great gift to the human race, and it should come readily and easily to those who could put it to the best possible use. mini The Government were going to set UP a ers, and by that they were going Mum rate of wages for the agriculturaelfiltabnlthoousre- who were working in the towns, for rdriven out of ' agricultural labourers were being driven i in the the country districts they w tone,: that the -wasornk the result that the town W towns and competing with tkoeewrn.c, for his labour. Houses insist upon a fair returnnfor the working classes were going to be not workers, with ttho and more healthy. lives happier, 1 and everything beThere was going to be (Hear, hear) tment be done to make their of the present a complete readjustment woboutlieLrt, and land would have to rating system, the future. There was no bear its fair ablrchina plea of urgency could be question for w advanced eater force than to deal dras- tically with the withgreater land problem. The people d independent as a result ' to be free an were going of Liberal legislatio nee —fr to live and thrive in justice, and of truth. the common air of liberty, j</p>
      </div>
