      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0178" n="art0178">
        <head>CHESHIRE CONSUMPTIVES.</head>
        <p>CHESHIRE CONSUMPTIVES.</p>
        <p>Treatment of Tuberculosis in the County.</p>
        <p>-t-.</p>
        <p>The Public Health Committee of the Cheshire Count t Council submitted to the quarterly meeting of that body on Thursday their scheme for treatment of tuberculosis applicable to all classes of persons resident in the administrative county of Chester. Arrangements had been en- tered into between the County Couircil and the County Boroughs of Chester, Birkenhead, Wal- lasey, Stockport and Stoke-on-Trent for the pro- vision of a joint sanatorium containing 150 beds, but with administrative block so con- structed as to allow of possible expansion to a total of 240, the number originally estimated as likely to be required in the future. Of these 150 beds seventy will be reserved for patients from the administrative county area (including twenty-eight specially reserved for the. County Insurance Committee). This insti- .. .</p>
        <p>tution will be devoted to the care of early cases or such as shall be deemed to have a fair chance •- of recovering -working capacity. Pending the opening of this sanatorium for reception of • patients, it may be necessary for the Joint Sana- torium Committee or the County Council, as the case may be, to make arrangements with exist- ing institutions for the accommodation of sixty or seventy patients.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0179" n="art0179">
        <head>Movable Shelters.</head>
        <p>Movable Shelters.</p>
        <p>In addition to the foregoing provision for early cases, a somewhat less number of beds are required for the treatment, isolation, and edu- cation of cases classed as too advanced for cura- tive treatment in a sanatorium. _ _</p>
        <p>Movable shelters of simple construction, and furnished with a bed, mattress, Ste., when neces- sary would require to be loaned out from or through the dispensary. These shelters would primarily be used for curative purposes in suit- able cases accessory to the treatment afforded through the dispensary and augmentative to the sanatorium. Their disposal should, there- fore, be vested in the dispensary staff, acting in conjunction with the local medical officers of health. Of the total sum available from the County 7nsurance Committee for sanatorium benefit (Le., £11,957 a year), .24,783 have been set aside for payment of a capitation fee of 6d. per in- sured person to medical practitioners in respect of domiciliary treatment of.such persons. While it is desirable that the nature of the domiciliary treatment afforded should be largely a matter for determination by the medical practitioner in charge of the case, it is reasonable to assume that the efficiency of such treatment will be considerably enhanced by free inter-communi- cation between the practitioner and the tuber- culosis officer. In many instances it will be found convenient to couple domiciliary treat- ment by the practitioner with contemporary specialised treatment at the dispensary. The one must not in any sense be taken as preclud- ing the other. The scheme was adopted.</p>
      </div>
