      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0188" n="art0188">
        <head>SHAKESPEAREAN PLAYERS</head>
        <p>SHAKESPEAREAN PLAYERS</p>
        <p>Pastoral Comedy to Clear the " Society of Debt.</p>
        <p>BM C. LAW'S SUGGESTION.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Amateur Shakespearean Players are in for a busy time, for at the annual meeting of the society, held in the Junior Con- servative Hall on Tuesday evening, it was de- cided to produce a pastoral comedy, probably within the next two months, to wipe off a deficit of about £l6 on the Commemoration per- formances. "A Midsummer Night's Dream" and "As You Like It" were stated to be " possibles." Dr. Adams presided, and he was supported by Mr. C. Law (honorary OPoducer), Mr. W. Mason (honorary secretary), and Mr. H. M. Ashton (honorary treasurer).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0189" n="art0189">
        <head>FINANCIAL.</head>
        <p>FINANCIAL.</p>
        <p>The TREASURER reported that the total re- ceipts, apart from subscriptions and donationO, received at the beginning of the season, were £195 15s. 6d. The expenditure amounted to £226 16s. 2d., leaving an advewe balance of £3l Os. &amp;I. That was the real deficit on the performances— a very sad state of affairs to report. Forty-five members' subscriptions totalled £ll ss. Od., and donations amounted to £4 14s. 6d. These sums had gone towards wiping off the deficit, leaving a balance of £l6 Is. 2d. still to be raised. That result was much better than was anticipated. and towards the deficit he had already been promised £1 by Mr. W. Brown, 10s. by Mr. Gar- nett, while Mr. Lowe had returned his expenses to the amount of £1 Bs. 6d. (Applause.) He did not think there would be any difficulty in rais- ing the remainder. A matter in which there was room for considerable improvement was the disposal of tickets by members of the society. The value of tickets issued to them was £5B 114 s. 9d., but no fewer than £3B worth were re- turned. He hoped that next year they would 'make a much better effort in that direction. There were eleven packets of tickets returned I unopened, and that was a thing to be regretted. (Hear, hear.) Mr. W. P. ASHTON proposed, and Mr. A. C. HOON seconded, the adoption of the balance sheet. This was carried.</p>
        <p>FAILURE OF THE "POPULAR SIDE." There was considerable discussion as to how best to wipe off the deficit, whist drives or a dance being mentioned. The TREASURER said the deficiency had practi- cally been paid by the committee, but they wanted to get straight as soon as possible. The receipts at the performances last year were £3O in advance of this year's, but the prices were then higher. The reduction of prices was tried as an experiment, as so many complaints were received last year of the raising of the ordinary theatre prices. The committee thought they would induce the general public to support the festival, but unfortunately they did not rise to the occasion. He did not suppose the experi- ment would be repeated. The SECRETARY said the loss was entirely on the popular parts of the house. The circle and stalls produced quite as much as last year, but where it was expected to make money—in the pit and gallery—they lost it. They wanted to start with a clean balance sheet next year. (Hear, hear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0190" n="art0190">
        <head>PRODUCER'S REPORT.</head>
        <p>PRODUCER'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>_ In presenting his report as producer, Mr. C. LAW said that they put two exceedingly good shows on, and it was most unfortunate that a greater number of people did not go to see them. Still, it was the people's loss, and he did not think the players had anything for which to blame themselves. They offered the public of Warrington performances which, as regarded mounting, scenery and dressing, could not have been seen anywhere except in London, and if, the public did not go to see them it was their own fault. They recognised themselves as being a t3wpany of amateurs, who did not profess to have professional attainments, but, still, they knew quite well that they could produce Shakes- peare, at any rate, in a respectable fashion. He thought it was regrettable that in a town such as Warrington, with a population of 72,000 and a large outlying district, they were not better supported.</p>
        <p>_ Pastoral Comedy. He did not think they should be discouraged by the deficit, and he would suggest that in an endeavour to clear it off they should use their own abilities and talents. It would not become their dignity or their art to go round cap in hand and ask for subscriptions or to organise whist drives. The suggestion which he took the liberty of offering to the society was that, in order to raise that very small deficit, they should organise a pastoral comedy and present it very shortly. The expenses would be very small, but the climatic risks were enormous. ,(Laughter.) An astute manager would, however, cover himself in that respect by selling plenty of tickets in advance. (Laughter.) They would probably be able to get some landed proprietor from whom they would be able to obtain the use Of suitable grounds free of charge; the costumes would be obtained as cheaply as possible, and no scenery would be required. It would be a lamentable thing to have whist drives or dances, which were all right for Sunday schools, but not worthy the dignity of a Dramatic Society. going to do Shakespeare. If the society made a bold effort on the lines he had suggested he thought they would have no difficulty in raising the necessary funds. (Applause.) The CHAIRMAN The idea seems an excellent one. .</p>
        <p>Mr. H. M. ASHTON proposed that steps be taken to present a pastoral comedy as suggested, and said he did not think they would have much difficulty in getting ground fo such perform- ances. .</p>
        <p>Mr. 11. G. MAasTow, in seconding, said it was a great pity that the subscriptions had had to go towards the deficit. The performances should, he thought, be self-supporting. Mr. J. E. HANNAN referred to a successful per- formance at Northwich of "A Midsummer Night's Dream." It was decided unanimously to promote a pas- toral comedy performance during the summer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0191" n="art0191">
        <head>OFFICIALS ELECTED.</head>
        <p>OFFICIALS ELECTED.</p>
        <p>The election of officers resulted as follows : President, Sir Gilbert Greenall; vice-presidents, The Mayor (Councillor Peacock), Mr. Harold Smith, M.P., Mr. A. H. Crosfield, Viscount Wolmer, ALP., Rev. Canon Morley Stevenson, Sir Joseph Beecham, Mr. T. Aylmer Frost, Mr. W. Peter Rylands, and Dr. Adams. On the proposition of Mr. MASON, seconded by Mr. H. M. AsErros, Dr. Adams was elected chair- man, in succession to the Rev. Dr. Mellor; Mr. H. M. Ashton was re-elected honorary treasurer, and Mr. C. Law honorary producer. It was proposed and seconded that Mr. W. Mason be re-elected honorary secretary, but Mr. MASON expressed regret that he would be unable to accept the office. The CuAnmax : That is a matter of deep regret to all the members. (Hear, hear.) Mr. C. H. Clarke was elected to be the hon. secretary. The committee was elected as follows :—Dr. Adams, Messrs. C. Law, C. H. Clarke, A. M. Loader, H. L. Warburton, W. Mason, L. R. 11°%vson, H. M. Ashton, L. C. Daintith, A. C. Hodgson, and W. P. Ashton.</p>
        <p>• AFFILIATION DECISION. Alterations of rules having been considered, it was decided that the society should affiliate with the Amateur Dramatic Association, Mr. C. LAW Pointing out that it was a useful association, which would safeguard them in obtaining ama- teur assistance in case of emergency. There would, he said, always be a feeling of security with their performances.</p>
        <p>THANKS. Mr. CLARK proposed a vote of thanks to Mr. Mason for his services, and Mr. 11. M. ASHTON seconded. In reply Isr.llAsox said he undertook the work quite voluntarily, as he was anxious that the society should not be allowed to lapse, and there appeared to be a difficulty at that time in filling the position. The work—had been arduous, but extremely pleasant. The best thanks of the society were also accorded all the officials for their excellent ser- vices, and Dr. Adams was thanked for presiding.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0192" n="art0192">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="2" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0193" n="art0193">
        <p>, SATURDAY, MAY 23, 1914.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0194" n="art0194">
        <head>, I ...... „ :.. , ... _ . , .0. iis f, , RicT pi Eliv3 I:: :, ~ ..</head>
        <p>, I ...... „ :.. , ... _ . , .0. iis f, , RicT pi Eliv3 I:: :, ~ ..</p>
        <p>LAYMM.</p>
        <p>May Queen.—Particulars about the new May Queen and a photograph will be found on an- other page.</p>
        <p>New Tennis Club.—The Misses Warburton and others in Heatley have inaugurated a ten- nis club for the district. Already 20 members have joined.</p>
        <p>• A a t meetingthe oolfi dtale ;s LwYeWrmehmifitxs euTd"rtaaiddseesfmoHlelOniml; Association follow: hit Monday, closed all day; Whit Wednesday, close at-1 e.m.; Whit Thursday, close at 12 o'clock.</p>
        <p>Vicar of Oughtringtony—The Rev. E. P. Wethered, vicar of Oughtrington, who has accepted a living in another part of the coun- try is to receive a presentation from the par- ishioners before his departure.</p>
        <p>Councillor Dixon's Resignation.—Councillor T. Warburton, chairman of the Lymm District Council, informed "The Examiner" represen- tative this week that Councillor Dixon's resig- nation was caused through ill-health and not for business reasons, as Previously stated.</p>
        <p>Death of Mrs. L. Joynson.—Yesterday after- noon the funeral took place at Oughtrington of Mrs. Louisa Joynson, wife of Mr. Ernest Joyn- son, of Canton View, Heatley. The deceased lady, who was 47 years of age, died somewhat suddenly on Monday.</p>
        <p>No Election in Statham Ward.—There was only one nomination yesterday for the vacant seat in the representation of Statham Ward, that of Mr. George Parker, of Brook-lane, and there will be no election. The vacancy was caused by the resignation of Mr. Dixon through</p>
        <p>• To-day's Election.—lt seems probable that the poll in the Heatley Ward, to-day will attract unusual attention. Mr. Ingham and his com- mittee have been working hard during the week, whilst his opponent has not been idle. The result is expected about 9 o'clock to-night.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0195" n="art0195">
        <head>LINES FROM LYMM.</head>
        <p>LINES FROM LYMM.</p>
        <p>"He has not a pair of trousers on his back !" So expressed a witness at Lymm Police Court. Most people wear trousers elsewhere than on the back.</p>
        <p>There is a Lymm dog which loves the police— in a way.</p>
        <p>It held up a gentleman in blue for 15 minutes. Such a type of doggie affection was not appre- ciated,</p>
        <p>And so the owner had to appear in Court, when</p>
        <p>He told the Justices that doggie was IT harm- less</p>
        <p>That the youngsters could pull it .out of its kennel.</p>
        <p>The dog was forgiven this time, but will have to be Very careful not to hold up a policeman again.</p>
        <p>Poor Rate.—The justices sanctioned a poor rate for Lymm of is. 6d. on buildings and 9d. on agricultural land, at Altrincham Sessions on Monday.</p>
        <p>Sleepers-out Brought In.—At an Occasional Court at Lymm on Tuesday, the lion. J. E. Cross and Mr. John Coxon presiding, two tramps were sent to gaol for 14 days each for sleeping out at Statham.</p>
        <p>On the Bench.—At the Lynim Petty Sessions on Wednesday the magistrates were Mr. A. Watkin (in the chair), the Hon. John E. Cross, Mr. John Coxon, Mr. S. Gould, Mr. S. Davies, and Mr. P. Warburton.</p>
        <p>Farm Labourer in Trouble.—James White, a farm labourer, of Arley, was charged at the Lymm Police Court on Tuesday, with being drunk and disorderly, and was fined 10s., includ- ing costs.—Acting-Sergeant Robinson proved the case.</p>
        <p>Anticipation and Surprise.—At the qi,Ymni Petty Sessions on Wednesday a defendant was represented by his father, who produced a letter enclosing a postal order "to pay the fine." The magistrates, however, adjourned the case until the next Petty Sessions, which the defendant most attend,</p>
        <p>An Irishman's Cold.—"l have had a cold and I took a drop of whisky" was how Edward Pally, an Irishman, explained his offence, when he was summoned at the Lymm Petty Sessions on Wednesday for using obscene language.—He said lie was sorry.—The Chairman: You will be fined 55., including costs, but it ought to be more. —Defendant: No, sir, no ! ,(Laughter.)</p>
        <p>Statistics.—Dr. E. M. De Jong, the Lymm Medical Officer of Health, has supplied the Cheshire County Council with the following figures for the year ending 31st December, 1913: Estimated population last August, 5,057 (1911 census, 4,989); area in acres, 4,374; persons to an acre, 1.15; births, 105; birth-rate per 1,000 living, 20.8; deaths, 66; death-rate per 1,000 liv- ing, 13.1; deaths under one year, 11; deaths under one year to 1,000 births, 105.</p>
        <p>Locomotive Without Licence.—At the Lymm Petty Sessions on Wednesday, James Struthers was summoned for driving a locomotive on the highway for haulage purposes without a haulage licence.—Mr. Cooper defended.—Sergeant Davies proved Hie case.—Mr. Lyons, of the Travelling Halls, Ltd., explained that their own traction engine had broken down, and as their lease of the ground was up they had to go. They en- deavoured to get another traction engine with a haulage licence, but could only get the one for which the summons was issued and which only had a threshing licence.—A fine of 55., including costs, was imposed.</p>
        <p>Shop Assistants and Football—A strong en- deavour is being made by the shop assistants of ',Yam to inaugurate a football team for next season, which they intend to enter in the second division of the Manchester Wednesday League. A subscription list has been opened and consid- erable encouragement has been given to the organisers by the ready response. A meeting was held on Tuesday night at the Millstone Hotel, when it was decided to apply for admis- sion to the Manchester Wednesday League. The following officers were appointed : Honorary secretary, Mr. Blount: assistant secretary, Mr. P. Holt; honorary treasurer, Mr. Inglesfield; committee, Messrs. J. Wareham, E. Creasey, J. Pye, Blease, F. Ball, and F. Warburton.</p>
        <p>" One of the Best Irishmen."—An Irishman, named Michael Byrom, a farm labourer, was charged at the Lymm Petty Sessions on Wed- nesday with being drunk in Booth's Hill on the evening. of May 2nd.—P.C. Atherton said Byrom was lying in the road helplessly drunk, and it was necessar Sergeant Robinson before defendant could be Y to obtain the assistance of Acting- rem?ved. When charged at the Court, Byrom replied: "Guilty, all roight. I have never been through a Court door before."—Acting-Sergeant Robinson said defendant was a decelat man— one of the best Irishmen in the district.—When ,discharged, Byrom exclaimed to the Chairman: ,I am very thankful to you. I hope I will never come into a Court again.".—The Chairman: All right; come as a spectator next time.</p>
        <p>Damaging Grass.—At the Lymm Petty Ses- sions on Wednesday Harry Cadman, Edward Gilberts and Charles Ravenscroft, of the New- fields district, were summoned by Philip Moston for doing damage to certain growing grass to the extent of a shilling.—The prosecutor, a dairy- man, of Booth's Hill, said nobody had any right to be on the field in question. He visited the field on April 11th and found about 30 been going on during playing with a full-sized football. This practice had Saturday day on Sunday. He saw the three defendants, amongst the others.—One of the youths said he did not know how three came to be Picked out nights and all of thirty.—The Clerk: You haveldeaded guilty. I do not see what you can say further.—The Chairman: He would rather someone else had been picked. (Laughter.)—Gilberts and Ravens- croft were fined ss. each, and Cadman was fined 10s.</p>
        <p>Without a Ticket—Summoned for travelling on the railway without a ticket, a defendant appeared at the Lymm Petty Sessions on Wed- nesday.—Mr. Arthur Browne, solicitor, Warring- ton, prosecuting on behalf of the London and North-Western Railway Company, said that on April Ist a porter collecting tickets from the 6.40 train asked defendant for his ticket, when he received the reply "It's all right," meaning thereby that he was a ticket-holder. On the following night defendant again replied "It's all right" to the porter, when asked for his ticket. However, the porter was not satisfied, and asked defendant to produce the ticket, whereupon he began to search his pockets and brought out several old ones. As he failed to find it he said it must have been lost.—The defendant, who said he was short of money at the time, was fined £1 10s. in the first case, and 10s. in the second case, and was allowed a month in which to pay.</p>
        <p>Sunday Football Enthusiasts.—" They seem to have no regard whatever for Sunday" was a remark made by Acting-Sergeant Robinson at the Lymm Petty Sessions on Wednesday, in giv- ing evidence against six youths for playing foot- ball on the highway, to the annoyance of pas- sengers. The defendants were Edward Gilberts, Joseph Statham, Harry Cadman, John Conagh- ton, James Taylor and William Rhead, all from the Newfields district. —Sergeant Robinson stated that on Sunday afternoon, May 10th, he was in Booth's Hill, when he saw the defendants play- ing football on a road leading down to New- fields. When they saw him they all ran away. This thing had been going on for some consid- erable time in Newfields. The defendants seemed to have no regard whatever for Sunday. They were playing with a small ball. He had I.peat- edly warned the youths. It was a disgraceful practice.—The Chairman said the case was a' perfectly proper one to be brought into Court, but accused were to be given one more chance. If they went there again they would have to be punished.—The cases were dismissed.</p>
        <p>There are still many people who buy spectacles as our grandfathers bought them fifty years ago. They patronize the door-to-door pedlar, or pick a pair from a tray exposed on some market stall. This is amazing when one thinks of the progress which has been made in the science of spectacle fitting. Our Grandfathers were blameless in this respect. They had no opportunity of doing Rdi.ffvee Your eyesT histceasnted cannot be said of their children. Johnston Watson</p>
        <p>Sight Testing Optician, 84, BRIDGE ST., WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Steel Spectacles from 2/6. Rolled Gold from 8/6.</p>
        <p>Hours of Business 9 to 7-30. Saturday 9to 9-30. Please Note.--Mr. Watson attends personally on Monday and Tuesday from 11 to 4-30; Wednesday 11 to 5-30; Thursday 11 to 1; Friday and Saturday 11 to 5-30.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0196" n="art0196">
        <head>DEATH OF MRS. JANE McCREDE.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF MRS. JANE McCREDE.</p>
        <p>The funeral took place at the Lymm Parish Church on Thursday of Mrs. Jane McCrede, the well-known licensee of the Church Inn. Deceased, who was 74 years of age, was one of the oldest licence holders in the district, and had catered at the Freemasons' annual dinner for 30 years. The deceased, who had been ail- ing for a considerable time, went to Blackpool three weeks ago, where she appeared to have caught a chill. She died on Monday. The Rector officiated at the funeral, and the principal mourners were: Mr. Bill (brother), Mesdames Carter, Wint, GriMiths, Johnson, and Miss A. Bill (nieces), Mrs. Marsland, Mr., Mrs. and Misses Round, Mrs. Cawdren, Mrs. Smith (Warrington), Mrs. Barrow, Miss A. Beddard, Mr. W. Mullard, and Mr. T. S. Steel. A deputation from the Freemasons' lodge, in- cluding the Worshipful Master, Mr. Martin, Altrincham, attended. Wreaths were sent by Mr. Bill, Miss Alice Bill and five other nieces mentioned amongst the mourners, Mr. and Mrs. Round, Mrs. Finchard, and "Ethel and Mary."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0197" n="art0197">
        <head>LIKING FOR POLICEMEN!</head>
        <p>A LIKING FOR POLICEMEN!</p>
        <p>What a Lymm Dog Did.</p>
        <p>Edwin Darbysh ire, of Tanyard Farm, Lymm, the owner of a dog which was stated to have such a liking for a policeman that it would not let one go away for a quarter of an hour, was summoned at the Lymm Petty Sessions on Wednesday for allowing the dog to be at large during prohibited hours. P.C. Atherton stated that he first saw the dog in the roadway 20 yards from the gate of its master's house. The dog kept him at bay for about 15 minutes, and then the defendant got o, and the defendant had told hint to the window and shouted it in. It was a dangerous dg it only went for policemen. (Laughter.) The dog was on defendant's premises when defend- ant called to it.</p>
        <p>Darbyshiie-sqated that the dog was not a dan- gerous one, for every child on the green could pull it out of its kennel. The reason the dog did not like policemen was because at one time a policeman used to tantalise it and strike it with his stick. _</p>
        <p>The Chairman (Mr. Watkin) said that if a deg kept him at bay on the road for fifteen minutes he would consider that dog dangerous. However, the present case would be dismissed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0198" n="art0198">
        <head>MOTHERS' UNION.</head>
        <p>THE MOTHERS' UNION.</p>
        <p>United Gathering at Lymm.</p>
        <p>ton, High • The Mothers' Unions of Lymm, Oughtring- on Wednesday afternoon, when they assembled at the Lymm Drill Hall and proceeded to the Legh, and Bollington, joined forces Parish Church in procession. Amongst the ladies present were Mrs. Ridgway (Lymm), Mrs. Watkin (Lymm), Mrs. Smith (Lymm Hall), and the Hon. Mrs. J. E. Cross (High Legl). The service, which was conducted by the Rev. G. B. Thurston (the rector), was well attended, and the discourse by the Rev. A. C. Bouquet, of St. Margaret's, Altrincham, was most helpful. The offertory, for the Chester Cathedral Restoration Fund, amounted to about .„t1 12s.</p>
        <p>Following the service an enjoyable tea was served in the Drill Hall to about 130 members. The Rector expressed sympathy with Mrs. Johnson (a founder of the Mothers' Union), who had recently lost her husband. This lady had written to Mrs. Watkin thanking the Mothers' Union for the kindness which had been shown to her. In the evening the ladies visited a nufigier of gardens in the neighbourhood, which hailibeen thrown open to them. These included "Dane- bank," Rectory grounds, Lymm Hall, and Mr. Houghton's gardens.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0199" n="art0199">
        <head>FORBIDDEN PERFORMANCES.</head>
        <p>FORBIDDEN PERFORMANCES.</p>
        <p>Lymm Police Take Action.</p>
        <p>At the Police Court on Tuesday, Claude S. Lyons and George Campbell, of-Travelling Halls, Ltd., were charged with being the occupier and licensee of a travelling picture show and allow- ing it to be used in contravention of the condi- tions for which the licence was granted by open- ing and exhibiting films on Good Friday. Mr. Cooper who appeared for the defendants, admitted that they did open on Good Friday. It stated on the licence that they could not open on that day, but as the licence 'as granted in Lancashire they took the conditions on the licence to apply only to the district in which it was granted,. and not to apply to Cheshire. They thought they could open on Good Friday, because when they appeared before the Altrinc- ham magistrates for a music licence for that day they were not told that exhibitions were pro- hibited. There was a misunderstanding.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0200" n="art0200">
        <head>"TAKETHE RISK."</head>
        <p>"TAKETHE RISK."</p>
        <p>Sergeant Davies ssaid he saw Mr. Lyons, the manager of the travelling show, just prior to the afternoon performance, and drew his attention to the clause in the licence prohibiting perform- ances on Sundays, Good Friday, and Christmas Day. In reply the manager said: " I have people in, and I cannot turn them out now." Asked if he purposed opening in the evening, he hesitated before he replied, "Yes; I will take the risk." In consequence of seeing the show advertised the officer visited the manager on the Thursday and asked him if he was sure he could open on Good Friday. The answer was that they could open provided music was not played. There were two performances in the evening, and there would be an attendance of 400 on the occasion the officer was there. It was expected that .{:2O would be taken.</p>
        <p>Acting-Sergeant Robinson, who was in com- pany with the previous witness, corroborated. Superintendent Sutherland said the police were not concerned as to whether it was right or wrong to open on Good Friday, but they had to consider if the licence had been complied with. It was granted on the condition that there were no performances on Good Friday, and the police must see that that condition was complied with.</p>
        <p>The Bench retired, and on their return the Chairman (Mr. Watkin) said they were unani- mously of the opinion that there must be a conviction against the defendants, who would each be fined 40s. and costs.</p>
        <p>Mr. Cooper asked for leave to appeal and this was granted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0201" n="art0201">
        <head>PENEETH.</head>
        <p>PENEETH.</p>
        <p>Band of Hope Trip.—The Penketh Band of Hope visited Southport on Saturday. A most enjoyable afternoon was spent, and the success of the trip must be attributed to the untiring efforts of Mrs. E. Dumbill, Miss Houghton, Miss Appleton, and Miss Jones.</p>
        <p>Death of Mr. J. Routledge.—Mr. joseph Rout. ledge, of The Cafe, Penketh, passed away late on Wednesday night, after a lingering illness. Deceased, who was 54 years of age, was a native of this district. Quietly interested in all matters of an athletic nature, he will be missed in more than one quarter.</p>
        <p>Schoolboy's Success.—Wilfred Hunter, aged 11, eldest son of Mr. Harry Hunter, Daystar Cottage, Station-road, has attained a well. merited honour in gaining a Lancashire County Council scholarship, coming fifth in the Earles- town sectibn. This is, the second scholarship won since the installation of Mr. D. G. Griffiths as headmaster 'two years ago, and speaks well for the personal interest 'he takes with the pupils and the thoroughness a the school cur- riculum.</p>
        <p>The Prize Band.—The Penketh Tannery Brass Band on Saturday went to Spendmore-lane, West Coppull, with strict injunctions from their supporters to return with two first prizes. The fates, however, were in a contrary mood, and willed otherwise, the bandsmen only gaining first in the march and fourth in the selection. A little satisfaction was derived from the know- ledge that the decision was not generally popu- lar. One special feature of the Tannery Band is they are not of the downhearted type, but are ever ready for the fray. With plenty of young blood in their composition and chock full of en- thusiasm, they are bound to make themselves felt wherever they go. Mr. Greenwood was the conductor. Eight bands competed: Waterfoot Prize, Harwich Old, Thornsett, Penketh Tan- nery, Rivington, Barrow Shipyard, Horwich R.M.1., and Parr Temperance. Thornsett was first in the selection.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0202" n="art0202">
        <head>MARTINSCRO FT.</head>
        <p>MARTINSCRO FT.</p>
        <p>District Council By-election.—A by-election to fill the vacancies on the Warrington Rural District Council and the Warrington Board of Guardians caused by the death of Mr. Houghton took place in the Roman Catholic Schools, Martinscroft, on Monday. The candidates were: Mr. Edwin Dennett, Martinscroft, who polled 55 votes; and Mr. John Stubbs, Woolston, who got 34 votes. Mr. Dennett was thus elected by 21 votes. He took his seat at the Guardians' meeting on Wednesday. Mr. J. C. Sutton acted as election agent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0203" n="art0203">
        <head>GRAPPENH ALL.</head>
        <p>GRAPPENH ALL.</p>
        <p>Assessable Value.—The rateable value of Grap- penhall will be increased from X 11,627 to £13,354 under the new county rate basis.</p>
        <p>THEL WALL.</p>
        <p>The New Vicar.—A large congregation gath- ered at Thelwall Church on Sunday to take part in the first service conducted there by their new Vicar, the Rev. Lewis Hermann Tiarks. The Vicar read the Thirty-nine Articles in the morn- ing, and in the evening preached from the text St. Matthew v. 48—" Be ye, therefore, perfect, even as your Father Who is in heaven is per- fect." The preacher said he not only stood before them as one who would have to account for his stewardship to God, but also as one standing in the place of him who for nearly half a century was their friend, shepherd and coun- sellor through cloud and sunshine. He (the preacher) trusted they would regard him not only as their vicar, but as their friend and helper. They could never have, or ought to expect, a sense of finished work. During his life, the Christian would always be stretching forward to something a little way beyond, and they should always keep the ideal embodied in the text before them. If their spiritual life was anything at all it must be to prepare for the life of the world to come, and their daily life, their daily business and daily standard set before them should be a very important part in the life of discipline for the unending life here- after. "Be ye perfect." That was the message which he left with them that night as he com- menced his ministerial life amongst them. How- ever difficult it might be, let them keep that ideal before them: and by the help of their prayers, their-communion, and their daily inter- course with God be enabled to bring their lives before Him, that He might assist them here and perfect them hereafter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0204" n="art0204">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH</p>
        <p>TOWNSHIP TITTLE-TATTLE.</p>
        <p>The first rose of summer! It has duly arrived at the Heath. This week a flower of prize strain Grown in the open has been proudly Displayed by the owner, who says Roses are three weeks earlier this season than last. Roses at Christmas and roses in May Seems a record for Stockton Heath. Are we •:;o have a picture palace?. Rumour has again been busy on that subject. It is said a suitable site has been secured. Where, has not been allowed to leak out. The question is—Could Stockton Heath Support such a place' if it comes? " Heathens" have had a free entertainment this week,</p>
        <p>In watching the P.O. men lay the telephone pipes.</p>
        <p>The energy with -which the lzten tackled th job</p>
        <p>Rather surprised the stolid, steady-going "Heathens." Those who thought the British working man Could not hustle were somewhat surprised. How time speeds along! One would hardly think it was 20-years Since the Ship Canal was opened. It was exactly 20 years on Thursday. Thus we have had two decades experience Of the now much-discussed Swing Bridge. Stockton Heath school children are indignant. Warrington scholars got a holiday for Ascen- sion Day On Thursday and the lads and lasses of the Heath Had to go to school as usual. They feel very sore about the matter. As yet they have not decided to send A deputation to the Cheshire Education Com- mittee. •</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath children who go to school in Warrington Got the holiday, but the Warrington -hi lii ren Who go to Stockton Heath schools were holi- day-less. That aggrieved the latter children vtc y much. The parents sympathised with the youngst•ers.</p>
        <p>Education Rate.—The special education rate to be raised over the parishes of Stockton Heath, Latchford Without and Walton Inferior by the County Council will be 21d. in the</p>
        <p>Rateable Value.—The rateable value of Stock- ton Heath will be increased from £16,625 to .:.t1.7,206 under the new county rate basis. The value of Walton Inferior is to be increased from £7,650 to £8,614.</p>
        <p>Underground Telephones.—The streets of Stockton Heath this week have been the scene of much pipe-laying. A large squad of Post Office workmen have been busy laying the pipes which are to carry the underground telephone wires. After the pipe-laying is .completed the electricians are to begin placing the wires in the conduits.</p>
        <p>Swimming and Boating.—The lake opens for swimming and boating to-day under the aus- pices of the Stockton Heath and District Swim- ming, Angling and Boating Association. Season tickets can be had at reasonable rates.</p>
        <p>• More Honour:. Gained.—Messrs. Hayes Bros., bakers, Stockton Heath, who last year gained many honours at exhibitions with their bread and confectionery, have started the present season well. This week they secured a first prize and two second prizes at the South Essex Master Bakers' Bread and Confectionery Exhi- bition.</p>
        <p>Funeral of Mr. A. H. Dunn.—The remains of the late Mr. Alfred Howard Dunn were quietly interred in the family burial ground at Hill Cliffe on Saturday, in the presence of the family. The Rev. Joseph Howard, of Sankey, performed the last rites. Wreaths were sent by the de- ceased brother, Mrs. Cooper, Mrs. Taylor, Mrs. Mather, Mr. and Mrs. Bow, Mr. and Mrs. Brocklehurst, Mr. and Mrs. W. Isaac, Mr. and Mrs. Deakin (Orford), and others.</p>
        <p>"Is Not Fear't."—The'agenda of the Runcorn Rural District Council meeting at Dutton Work- house on Tuesday contained the item "Mr. Pilling's motion relating to Egerton-street, Stockton Heath." However, it was Councillor Mclntyre who moved it, saying that it was on account of Councillor Pilling's modesty that he proposed "that application be made under the Private Street Works Act for the paving of Egerton-street, Stockton Heath."—A Member: Councillor Pilling is not " fear't." (Laughter.) —Councillor Mclntyre: He is. (Laughter.)— The resolution was carried, Councillor Pilling seconding.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0205" n="art0205">
        <head>HIGH LEGIT.</head>
        <p>HIGH LEGIT.</p>
        <p>Motor Cyclist at Fault.—For not producing his driver's licence when demanded to do so by P.C. Gould, at High Legh on May 2nd, Nathaniel Rothwell, of St. Helens Junction, was fined ss. and costs by the justices at the Lymm Petty Sessions on Wednesday.—The officer stated that lie saw the defendant driving a motor cycle and sidecar in Warrington-road, High Legh. He had occasion to stop the defendant, who, when asked for his licence, produced one which had expired on July 16th last year. The officer pointed out that the licence was an old one and was informed by defendant that he had paid for one a few days previous, but had not then re- ceived it.—ln reply to questions, defendant said that was his first time out this year, and he had been to Knutsford for the May Day revels. He had not been using his car during the winter, for it had broken down and had to be sent to Germany for repairs, "as it was a German bike'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0206" n="art0206">
        <head>QUALITY OF CHESHIRE CHEESE.</head>
        <p>QUALITY OF CHESHIRE CHEESE.</p>
        <p>Cheshire cheese was discussed at the meeting of the Cheshire County Council on Thursday. The County Analyst (Mr. S. E. Melling). men- tioned in a report that three samples of Che- shire cheese did not contain the due proportion of butter-fat to which the genuine article was entitled. Latterly he had given special consideration to this important question, and, following the analysis of many samples, had fixed upon a minimum proportion of fat natural to what was ordinarily regarded as whole-milk Cheshire cheese, viz., 24 per cent. • Mr. W. J. Dutton urged that Us long as they had diverse interests, not merely of the produce of Cheshire cheese, but also of a whole army of traders and retailers who would be placed in a very invidious position if such a standard was established, he submitted that the time was not ripe at present for such statements to go forth to the public. Dr. Hodgson (vice-chairman) said there was no standard fixed in the report, and the Coifn- cil did not fix the minimum of 24 per cent. They should endeavour to maintain the quality of Cheshire cheese, and encourage all the investi- gation which would give them such informa- tion as might eventuate in the fixing of a stan- dard. No resolution was moved, and the report was received.</p>
      </div>
