      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0214" n="art0214">
        <head>TABLE TALK.</head>
        <p>TABLE TALK.</p>
        <p>The World's Tribute of Interesting Items of News. The Local Life of Dr. Philip P. Carpenter, Preacher, Printer and Social Reformer,</p>
        <p>QUAINT AND AMUSING.</p>
        <p>• A well-known analytical chemist, Mr. M. S. Salamon, has uttered an earnest caution about apples that appear to be mildewed near the stalk. He examined some imported apples of excellent quality and flavour that were brought to him. Near the sealk was a green deposit that proved to be not mildew, but copper sulphate (blue vitriol) with some admixture of lime. It had evidently been left behind in spraying the fruit against parasites. Mr. Salamon considers the presence of such a rank poison in fruit largely eaten by children as a grave public danger.—" Science Siftings."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0215" n="art0215">
        <head>Eggs and White Feathers.</head>
        <p>Eggs and White Feathers.</p>
        <p>An egg-laying competition in England has brought to light the intertasting fact that all the champion layers are white hens. A scientific poultry breeder has come to the conclusion, that there is some peculiar virtue in white feathers. He noticed that in a mixed lot of hens the whites went to roost last; they took more exercise and were less affected by bad weather. The English competition suggests that hens capable of laying 250 eggs a year are more likely to come from the white Wyandottes or White Leghorns than , from any other variety, but, what the relation may be no one knows.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0216" n="art0216">
        <head>Chickens to Kill Scorpions.</head>
        <p>Chickens to Kill Scorpions.</p>
        <p>A correspondent in Tunis wrote to a French scientific newspaper asking for advice as to the best way ,of ridding his farm of scorpions. 'Answers poured in'from all over the world, and, extraordinary as it may seem, almost all gave the same remedy—chickens! It seems that com- mon barnyard fowls are never attacked by scor- pions, but kill them with one peck and love to Pick the flesh out of their shells. Another com- monlY 'Suggested remedy was cats. ' These animals hunt scorpions with as much avidity 'as they hunt mice, but kill the poisonous creatures with one blow of their paw.</p>
        <p>Human " Gifts of the Sea Gods." The fate of many of the hundreds of explorers who have gone out to the Pacific Islands since the sixteenth century and never been heard of since was a terrible one. In a lecture before the Royal Geographical Society on Wednesday Mr. Basil Thomson, the recently appointed Assistant Commissioner of the Metropolitan Police, said that many of them, who were often deserters or mutineers, were Tairly certain of dying a natural death, i.e., "they ,were killed anti:eaten by the natives. In Fiji," said Mr. Thomson, "the man who came ashore with salt water in his eyes was killed and eaten, and he was regarded as a gift of the sea gods."</p>
        <p>What An Inch of Rain Is.</p>
        <p>An inch of rain strikes one as very little. But it is really a big rainfall. When an inch of rain has fallen it means, literally, that the amount of water descending from the sky in that par- ticular shower would cover the surrounding territory to a depth of one inch, providing, of course, that it did not run away or soak into the ground. It means that on one acre water enough to fill more than 600 barrels of 45 gallons each has fallen. This quantity of water weighs more than 110 tons. If the rain storm covered a territory comprising a thousand acres, which would be a very small shower, 113,000 tons of water would fall from the clouds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0217" n="art0217">
        <head>To_ Replace Searchlights.</head>
        <p>To_ Replace Searchlights.</p>
        <p>Some remarkable experiments with a-new method of illumination for the purposes of naval work at night time have been carried out by a German battleship off one of the North Sea naval bases. The object was to find a substitute, in various contingencies, for the present search- light, which has the drawback of disclosing its source of emission. Tubes filled with calcium carbide were fired from a special gun into the sea. On rising to the surface the carbide came into contact with water, and was transformed into acetylene gas. This was automatically lit,' and gave a tremendous flare of several thousand candle-power. A few of these projectiles, thrown around an enemy, would place him at a great disadvantage for a considerable time.</p>
        <p>Longevity of Fleas. Socrates asked how far a flea could jump, but (says a writer in "Knowledge") it is even more important to know how long it can live. It is important, because fleas are bearers and dissem- inators of various parasites (amgrig bacteria, protozoa, and even Ilelminths); thus the rat. flea of India (Xenopsylla cheopsis) is the bearer of the plague microbe. The British rat-flea (Ceratophyllus fasciatus) usually passes through its developmental cycle in two or three weeks; in ten days in warm, damp weather. Gautier and Raybaud kept one alive on human blood for ninety days. Another lived for forty-one days in an ice-chamber without feeding. Dr. William Nicoll finds that a rat-flea can live on an average about a week apart from its host.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0218" n="art0218">
        <head>Mexico an Illiterate Nation.</head>
        <p>Mexico an Illiterate Nation.</p>
        <p>...--.--.. -. —.- Mexico is still, in spite of recent educational efforts, an illiterate nation, says the "Chronicle." How, then, does the news of war travebdewn to the classes now threatening violence to American citizens? It is due in this way. While the peon is making merry in his cafe there will suddenly enter a well-dressed, well-mannered man, with the courtly salutation, "Senores, with your per- mission„ I propose to read something for your entertainment." A glad cry goes up. The reader mounts a barrel, selects a good story or two, and having finished goes round with the hat, and proceeds to the next saloon to entertain a fresh audience. These readers are extremely popular, and for thousands of Mexicans form the only link with the stirring events of the world.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0219" n="art0219">
        <head>How to Fly Across the Atlantic.</head>
        <p>How to Fly Across the Atlantic.</p>
        <p>An aviator and aeroplane constructor of world-wide renown has expressed astonishment (says Mr. T. F. Farman, the aviation expert) that the £lO,OOO prize has not yet tempted any- one to run the risk of flying across the Atlantic Ocean on an existing aeroplane. Ingold's non- stop flight of 1,055 miles, to say nothing of other aviators' previous performances, proves that, favoured with propitious weather and with absence of motor trouble, it is possible to cross the Atlantic in three stages. The distance separating respectively Labrador, or even New- foundland from Greenland, Greenland from Ice- land, and Iceland from the Hebrides, is less than SOO miles, or 250 miles less than Ingold's record. undoubtedly the risks the pilot would run in those northern regions would be very great. But after accomplishing each of the 800-mile flights he could rest, verify his machine, and, above all, his motor, and await a favourable moment for starting on the next stage.</p>
        <p>Several people were seen waiting outside the Customs House at Brindisi, where £60,000 worth of bullion was stored, on Saturday night, but the guards fired their revolvers and they dis- appeared.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0220" n="art0220">
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST —continued.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0221" n="art0221">
        <p>his position. He is liable to be moved at any time without any fault or lailing of his own." 'Altogether, none need be surprised that " curates are not so easy to get as blackberries in Septembe4," and that the worthy local vicar whose experience that has been has decided to get alOng as best he may without such assist- ance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0222" n="art0222">
        <head>MORAL AND PHYSICAL BENEFITS OF SWIMMING.</head>
        <p>MORAL AND PHYSICAL BENEFITS OF SWIMMING.</p>
        <p>A dozen years of crowded life were the gift of Dr. Philip 'Carpenter to the town of Warrington. The results of those years linger yet, though more than fifty years have passed since his ministry closed. To a few he lives yet in happy memory; in a more real sense he lives in the larger life of thettown,s which he sweet- ened by his presence and quickened by his aspirations and :ideals. Philip Pearsall Carpenter came to Warrington at the invitation of the congregation of Cairo- street Chapel. lie was a bachelor in his 27th year, who had already, during' five years spent at Stand, shown himself an original and zealous preacher. He was born in Bristol, November 4th, 1819. His father, a schoolmaster of a private school and minister of Lennis Mead Chapel, had migrated there from Exeter, and Philip grew up among the well-to-do pupils who were taught in the home. First a pupil of his father and then a scholar at Bristol College, afterwards a scholar at large, hunting fossils in the quarries at Dundry and arranging them in cabinets, he soon became in miniature what he was to become and to remain through life—a "born teacher, a naturalist by chance," with predominating tastes for music and shells.</p>
        <p>Many things happened to make him a thought- ful youth. When twelve years old .he witnessed the Bristol riots, in which the Mansion House, Gaols, Custom House and Bishop's Palace were burned. His 'father gave evidence at the trial of the Mayor of the city, and Philip copied out his</p>
        <p>have been of essential service. We are now beginning a night school, and in three weeks got a hundred scholars, two-thirds of them men. I have stirred up their minds with lectures on the Christianity of War- rington, the drinkidg customs of W., the sanitary condition of W., and various out- door temperance lectures. All have excited great attention. Wt 3 have a Working Men's Sanitary Association; they go visiting two and two, and make reports of the town; we have nearly concluded a quarter of it. I am secretary. We have got a Juvenile Temperance Society in our school, and have meetings on Sunday evenings, and a Juvenile Peace Society. All this time I have excellent health. We bathe every morning, wet and fine—have done for three months—set out at half-past five; and I am learning to swim. You can't think what an intense delight it is to me, and how much good it has done me, phy- sically and morally. We make this long extract from a letter of Philip Carpenter (the date is August 19th, 1847) because it reveals so admirably the writer, and lyhis the conditions he was called to face during his first year in Warrington. Philip had made his home in -Academy Place (afterwards re- moving to Buttermarket-street) with his sister ravages housekeeper and right bravely tisl sources other facts may be le va of disease and famine. e From Susan faced as other gath- ered. Always practical, he preached a sermon on "Waste of Fragments," in which he showed t could imultde b made of Indian corn as a cliiiee es of distress. In another Part of the sermon he trounced the brewers for wasting barley that would have fed the starving millions. The Sanitarrpse food for f y Association was Made up of working men out of employment, who made a house-to-house visitation, reporting upon the condition of dwellings. Persons re- ceiving relief were set to clean and scour out back streets and yards. When Lord Morpeth introduced a Health of Towns Bill, Philip pre- pared a petition in its favour, signed by 4,319 persons, which contained the names of almost the entire adult working population of War- rington. He promoted eleven petitions in all, which were duly presented to Parliament by" Lord Winrnarleigh. Many of the passages in his sermons did not, as his sister had to remind him, sound like the "Sunday Reading" they had been accustomed ,to in their youth, but none could quarrel with the preacher, who became desperately practical in the presence of desperate conditions. While doing his best to feed the poor, he preached to them out of doors, and on Sunday evenings spoke "behind the Bridewell" and at Town's End after the services at Cairo-street were over.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0223" n="art0223">
        <head>SWIMMING ACADEMY..</head>
        <p>THE SWIMMING ACADEMY..</p>
        <p>••- - - - Through the stress of this terrible period, and during the years that followed, Philip Carpenter found refreshment of body, and his chief recrea- tion in bathing. A young man whom he had befriended and who had joined his school for those out of work was discovered to be a good swimmer. Philip had never trusted himself to deep water, but yielded to an invitation to learn to swim. He took to the water with the zest of a schoolboy, and proposed a " Swimming Academy," to be held at five-thirty in the morn- ing for the benefit of those who desired to be- come swimmers. The chief seat of learning of this new Academy was Buttermilk Bridge, though the mill ponds at Walton and Acker's offered fine diversion to the scholars and gave the "Doctors of Swimming" better opportuni- ties to reveal their skill. In a very delightful letter to W. H. Herford, an old College friend, Philip described the " College" : I never was better in my life. I walk upright (upon his arrival in Warrington he had been described as the parson ',rho walked with his head first), am fuller in the face, and my chest is grown much , larger; stronger altogether, and in a healthy state of mind. This is to be attributed to my going to bed at ten and getting up at five, and spending all the before-breakfast time in air, water and exercise. The event which has worked this radical change in my habits is learning to swird—an accomplish- ment I never expected to acquire; but thanks to intense perseverance, wet and fine, Sundays not excepted. I have so far overcome my natural awkwardness that I can swim about twenty or thirty yards at a time, can float a bit, and altogether have moderate confidence in "a tidy depth of water." Our bathing party has met at half. past five every morning; and we teach one another somewhat in the German fashion, by stringing up at a turn bridge. 01 course, they have fallen away since the stormy weather began; but I have never been entirely without company, except on one morning. We have established a regu- lar college, and give degrees according to proficiency. To take a Doctor, it is neces- sary to have saved someone from drowning, and taught someone to swim. To take a Bachelor, they must swim across the broad part of the canal, turn and come back with- out stopping, some forty yards. All grown- up persons who can't swim belong to the awkward squad. of whom I am President. Lads that are learning are simply under- graduates. On the first of this month we christened rails, fivae new et nturnine bridgewhich was a decent plunge for a squad! Our favourite inches. by diving off place is a mill stream which runs down a steep channel into a pond. We go in with the stream, swim across the deep Dart and land in the shallow. There has lately been a flood and such a stream! We jumped in from the wall, and shot off like wildfire. One of our doctors dived in from the top of the water wheel. On Sunday week. four persons were killed through drinking in Warrington : one hung himself; the others were out in a boat; three drunken men and two lads. They reeled about, capsized the boat. and the two lads and one man were drowned; the other two would have been also, but that my swim- ming doctor plunged in and saved them. I preached about it last Sunday; chapel crowded; many went away.</p>
        <p>TO WIN MEN TO HIGHER THINGS. Yet another description of the Swimming School may be copied. It might be inferred from his own playful words that the morning excursions were times of riotous fun and little else, but a memorial pamphlet written by one of his pupils shows that the friendly heart of the preacher had discovered in the "Academy" an opportunity for winning men to higher things than the art of swimming,</p>
        <p>He wrote He was indefatigable in teaching all who cared to learn, and be often spent from an hour to an hour and a half in a morning teaching swimming by means of a rope and belt at Buttermilk Bridge. On Sunday mornings, when the weather was fine and favourable, there would be hundreds bath- ing in the canal, and this gave him excellent opportunities of coming into contact with men whom he never would have reached in any other way. He had a kind and encour- aging word for all, but was especially mind- ful of the younger ones, whom he was very fond of calling his young cubs. He must have encouraged thousands to acquire the art of swimming; and never had a case of drowning. . . . I have no doubt that the genial and truly English way of doing this won for him the genuine esteem of the working classes of Warrington, for they Oiliest alone were his attendants, especially on Sunday mornings. Lessons of perseverance could be easily based upon lessons in swimming, and a letter which has been preserved shows that he took oppor- tunity to apply one to the moral efforts of his " Swimming Doctor," or, in other language, the young man who had taught him to swim. • "Don't be disheartened; just think how many scores of times I went down in learning to swim —or rather SHOULD have done, if you had not held me up. So now I must hold your head up; and 'When at first you don't succeed, Try, Try, TRY again!" . .</p>
        <p>(To be continued.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0224" n="art0224">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON WORTHIES.</p>
        <p>correspondence with Earl Grey and Lord Hol- land on the subject. His father published much, and Philip became proof-reader and book- binder from his boyhootl. More important, he lived through the fervour of the Reform Acts agitation, and imbibed from his father a hatred of slavery, which was not concealed because some of the leading members of his congregation were slave owners. _ _ .</p>
        <p>A brief period spent in London in the service of an uncle, who was an optician, gave him a glimpse of a wider world, but his heart was set upon teaching, an,cl he went back to Bristol Col- lege, and thence to "Manchester College," at York. At Welburn, near York, he preached his first sermon, and after a visit to Stand, six miles from Manchester, was invited to become The minister there. Though Philip to the end of his days would have rejected the title Unitarian minister, he entered the Unitarian pulpit and found usefulness in it through his best years. When the Cairo-street congregation invited him he wrote: "1 perceive that your congregation calls itself by the name 'Unitarian Christian.' When I was invited tO become the minister of the Stand congregation they called themselves 'Presbyterians,' and my principles are entirely those of the English Presbyterian Dissenters; although I had rather that all distinctive names were given up, and that we were content to be known simply as the disciples of Christ. • • • if you allow me full liberty to teach the religion of Christ as a spiritual influence, irrespective of sectarian distinction, I shall feel pleasure in becoming your minister. But without that liberty I could neither be faithful nor useful among you." FIRST WINTER IN WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Cairo-street Chapel, in 1846, was not such a sanctuary as the preacher would have designed. It had a three-decker pulpit, which he after- wards had the satisfaction of reducing to a tlto-. decker, and it retained the square family pews that had done duty for a full century. But there was a schoolroom, built not long before, which gave him great satisfaction. To provide a harmonium for the school he gave a series of lantern lectures and raised about .86. The scene of the lectures was the theatre, and as lantern lectures were then a novelty he had audiences which promised well for such effort in the future, though he had to admit that his gallery audience would take a full season to "lick into shape." A very characteristic effort of this first winter was a meeting of the Anti-Slavery League. The immediate object of the agitation was to induce the Free Church of Scotland to send back money which had been given by American slave- holders. Philip Carpenter was not a speaker on this occasion, but, as he wrote, devoted him- ' self "to keeping the gallery quiet, and to the Christian work of keeping the window open; to do which I was obliged to let the flap down ' and sit on it."</p>
        <p>FAMINE YEAR—"A MASS OF MISERY." The year 1847 was Famine Year, and one of almost unparalleled misery in Warrington. A description written by the young preacher sad- dens us, though it is two generations old : I never knew such a winter and spring and summer, and I trust I never may again. Most of the mills stopped; one since Novem- ber, another since January, others for two • or three months, and the rest half time. Only three Mills are going and those but partially. Fustian cutting, not one-twelfth work; pin-making, ditto. Inundated with thousands of starving Irish of the worst class, determined not to work; food terribly high; fever much worse than the cholera. The Union surgeon has died of typhus fever, and four other officers are down with it. We have set up a starving schoolmaster in a Ragged School, and must try to raise him 7 or 8 shillings a week. We have had more than twice the usual number of deaths; large wooden sheds erected for the sick, and have now got so accustomed to see people with starving faces that one hardly thinks of it. You may trace them gradually getting thinner. and . thinner, and more and more sickly; things gradually pawned; credit gradually• used up; hard-hearted relieving officer, and alto- gether a mass of misery. We have had a soup kitchen with regular visitations, divid- ing ,the town into districts. For a fortnight I did not sit down in my study.. The rich people, for once, found the • wretched ones out in their courts and hovels, and I cannot describe to you the stenches we meet. To go into the bedroom of an Irish lodging-house, with one or two ill of fever, and no windows open. walls and floor and everywhere reeking with filth! I have gone everywhere that duty called hfe, fearlessly and safely, thanks to our Father's Protection. I have worked hard, and, hav- ing saved • un a bit ot money for times of pressure at Stand, have been able to do some good. Susan has been more than a helper— a leader in the town. She has freely spent her money, time, incessant labour—cooking, doctoring, visiting, comforting, teaching— doing everything. I think it is a great mercy that we were sent here when we were; for we have not only been useful ourselves, but have been able to stir up others. We have had, a day school for the unemployed boys and. men, and an industrial school for females, and both (especially the latter)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0225" n="art0225">
        <head>23, 1914.</head>
        <p>23, 1914.</p>
        <p>Literary Gleanings.</p>
        <p>INDIA'S FIRST LADY.</p>
        <p>Among all the rulers of the Native States of India, there is no one else who stands out in commanding personality as does the Nawab Sultan Sahan Begam of Bhopal. She has dis- __ _</p>
        <p>tinctions unique in many directions. No other woman at this moment fills;•er appears likely to fill an Indian throne. She alone of her-sex, with the exception of the Queen Empress, wears the mantle and insignia of the grand Cross of , the Order of the Star of India, and is quite alone in the corresponding exalted rank of the Order of the Indian Empire. To these she adds the Crown of India in unique combination. No other woman up and down that great land re- ceives a salute of nineteen guns on all occasions of State ceremony. She is a brilliant English and Persian scholar, she has travelled widely, she enjoys equally the confidence of the Home and Indian Governments, and hosts of native and English friends.—" The Woman's Maga- zine."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0226" n="art0226">
        <head>CHARLES KEENE'S LODGINGS.</head>
        <p>CHARLES KEENE'S LODGINGS.</p>
        <p>When the term of his apprenticeships over he set up for himself in a studio, finding con- genial quarters on the attic floor of an old hduse in the Strand. Here, amid dust and cobwebs, old costumes and properties hung upon a clothes-line drawn across the room, he worked hard for his living. He would have no char- woman about his Premises. Any dusting and cleaning required he did himself. Also he was his own cook. This habit he kept up to the end. When in the course of years he reached his studio in the King's-road, Chelsea,' he took with him a gas stove, with whose dubious aid he prepared his midday meal. Some men would , have supplied themselves with a better equipped apparatus, The expenditure upon gas was quite enough for Carlos without running into extras in the way of plant. A cheap gaslight set on a stool and connected with the chandelier by a flexible tube supplied the heat, whilst for cook- ing contrivance he ingeniously adapted the coiled spring taken from an old Gibus hat. This, combined with a jam gallipot, served him just as well as the elaborate kitchen contrivance at the Reform pleased Soyer when he was the • club's chef. •</p>
        <p>Keene had, to tell the truth, close sympathy with one of the Scotchmen he immortalised, he who, coming to London "had not been there two hours when bang went saxpence." In re- ceipt of a handsome income from various sources, he carefully guarded the expenditure of every penny. .In a rare access of garrulity lie told me that walking home late one, night from his studio, after a hard day's work, he was haunted by apprehension that he had left the gas burning in his cooking stove. That meant an appreciable expenditure utterly wasted. The night was far advanced. lie was worn out with fatigue, and the studio was dis- tant. But he could not sleep in his bed with the idea of the reckless gas flaunting aimlessly through the long night. So he walked. all the way back, and never decided whether he was pleased or vexed to find his dereliction of duty purely fanciful. The gas had been properly turned out.</p>
        <p>The result of these frugal habits was that when he lay dead in his plainly furnished room at Hammersmith, it was found he had left be- hind him nearly £4o,ooo.—From "-Sixty Years in the Wilderness," by Sir Henry Lucy, in the " Cornhill Magazine" for May.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0227" n="art0227">
        <head>AN UNKNOWN SON OF NAPOLEON.</head>
        <p>AN UNKNOWN SON OF NAPOLEON.</p>
        <p>When Napoleon was finally overthrown and sent off to be, and that in a very limited sense, monarch of all he surveyed in the rocky islet of St. Helena, he left behind him in Europe, not only the infant King of Rome, but also his two elder sons, the Comte Walewski and the Comte Leon, of whom History, looking askance at the bend sinister, has taken less note. It is of the eldest of this trio of Napoleon's sons, the one to whom he gave a portion of his name, that Al. Hector Fleischmann tells in this volume. It was as recently as 1881 that the Comte Leon died, and it is difficult to avoid wondering what would have been the story of France during the nineteenth century had he been the eldest son of the Enaperor legally as well as naturally! It is true that the biographer admits that Napo- leon himself, when in exile, expressed doubts as to his paternity of Leon, but he shows that such doubts, if they may have been justified by assumptions, were not justified by facts, and Points out with emphasis how in his old age the Comte Leon resembled his father. The like- ness, it is admitted, was limited to physical characteristics. In piecing together the story of this strange man's life, M. Fleischfrtann has been able to bring together much varied in- formation, not only about him, but also about his mother, the charming young woman, who, the daughter of an adventuress, attracted an adventurer. It is a pretty story of diamond cut diamond, but the lady had the best of it, for having attracted the attention of the Em- peror, she became a person of importance, while her husband was placed conveniently out of the way in gaol. As to Count Leon, his story is by no means an edifying one. Many claimants to be descendants of " The Corsican" have at different times cropped up in various parts of the world. We follow the record of Comte Leon through the most varied experiences, with a consequent trial for gambling, duelling— manslaughter, and so on, up to the bitter end in aged poverty.</p>
        <p>THE WAR OFFICE, PAST AND PRESENT. The publishers of this book proclaim it as the first comprehensive history of the War Office, and at the same time express their surprise that so promising a subject should have waited so long for an adequate historian. IJnder the cir- cumstances, Captain Owen Wheeler must be said to have made the very best .of a difficult opportunity. With a certain touch of quiet humour our author traces back the origin of the Comman- der-in-Chief's duties to the activities of Cas- sivelannus, who in the year 54 B.C. appointed himself leader of the British troops against the invasion of Julius Ccesar. The precedent which he then set survived for generations, for the ' military command always went hand in land with the dignity of kingship. For years and years the King led his men into _battle, and it was not until the Tudor times that a Treasurer at War took on the daties of feeding and cloth- ing the troops. Here was the first germ of a permanent War Office, and in 1620 a regular bureau devoted to military interests actually ' came into existence. Its early years, however, are "shrouded in a doubt," and despite Crom- well's military activities, it was reserved for the period of the Restoration to see the history Of the modern British Army and of the War Office itself started upon a definite and pro- gressive career. In 1660 Monk, afterwards Duke of Albemarle, became Commander-in-Chief, and set a fashion of noncialance which many of his followers accepted with equanimity. With Queen Anne, however, there was insti- tuted a new regime—Marlborough was ap- pointed head of the Army, and became at once the most important man in the kingdom. But in the meanwhile there had arisen a scandal over accounts, ' and 'two Paymaster-Generals were now established, one for home affairs and one for foreign. With St. John, afterwards Lord Bolingbroke, at hand to render Marlbor- ough loyal service, the whole question of cloth- ing the Army was put upon a systematic basis, and the position of the common soldier was encfrmously improved. There followed a period of princely autocracy, until the outbreak of the great scandal of 1809, when the-pelticoat gov- ernment of a whole generation was exposed to the ridicule of the satirists. There had been for years a systematic traffic in commissions, in the course of which one Mary Ann Clarke, mis- tress to the Duke of York, had enriched herself freely at the expease of age and competence. There was a House of Commons inquiry, the stables of scandal were scoured, and the Duke of York had to resign his command. Nevertheless, despite the blow thus dealt to the autocracy of birth, the abuses of military</p>
        <p>service continued for more than another half- century, and it was not until Edward Cardwell joined 'Gladstone's Ministry in 1868, as Secre- tary of State for War, that the age-old abuses of privileges were swept away. Cardwell, in fact, revolutionised the War Office, which re- mains to-day in all essential features what he made it by -his energy and integrity.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0228" n="art0228">
        <head>Coidinued in next. column,.</head>
        <p>Coidinued in next. column,.</p>
        <p>0 . CHILDREN'S CORNER. y Conducted by "UNCLE JACK. i11''')42,0.24,50-ooozilwaooo 0 eoac</p>
        <p>My Dear Children,— I suppose you are all so busy playing out during the present fine weather that you do not desire a long letter from me, so I shall not let you have one of more than a few lines. I hope to be able to announce a competition next week, so you keep your eyes on this column and tell your friends to do likewise. Your loving UNCLE ':IACK.</p>
        <p>THAT-TICKET' MAN. Guarding the gate, a noble figure he— Uniform-clad, for all 'the world to see. The crowd pours by, and every face he'll scan, Unbending, ominous, this ticket man!</p>
        <p>A brave man he who would this'stripling baulk, With eye as piercing as a bunter's hawk. I'll wager hundreds if he falsely can Stoutly say Season"" to this ticket man!</p>
        <p>A FAREWELL. Elsie Mary Skeet. Farewell, relentless winter! farewell to frost</p>
        <p>and snow! And fare thee well, 0 cililblain mine, for thou must also go. In pleasure and in toil my true companion thou hast been, But we must part, 0 chilblain, for the hedges now are green. Was ever friend so faithful, so changeless and sincere?- But now the trees begin to bud; thou may'st not</p>
        <p>• linger here. The breath of Spring awakes again the primrose</p>
        <p>in the dell, So, faithful little chilblain mine, we twain must say farewell.</p>
        <p>Throsirji all the dreary winter days to leave me you refused— In vain were all the ointments and the lotions that I used. How noble of you—and my soul with admiration burns— To come to me when nought is left, and go when all returns!</p>
        <p>The flowers of Spring are here again; the bird sings on the bough; So think not that I need thee—these are my companions now. The sun is shining brightly; to linger would be</p>
        <p>vain— And so farewell, 0 chilblain mine, till winter comes again.</p>
        <p>THE' LOST ALPHABET.</p>
        <p>The Board of Education insist that scholars in the first and second year's course in higher elementary schools shall spend at least four hours per week in the study of science, half of which time must be devoted to practical work. The ages of these scholars will vary between ten and twelve. Half of them are girls. Touch- ing this a Halifax correspondent, who signs himself W.D., sends the following:— Seated one day at my lessons, I was wearily trying to cram A problem in hydrostatics— Something about a gramme. I know not if I was dreaming, I fancy I wanted my tea; But I heard a melodious murmur Like the sound of A, B, C. It flooded the dreary classroom Like an echo from long ago, It filled my soul with yearnings And my eyes with 1120. I couldn't think where I had heard it, My memory seemed to halt; It brought back the days when children Called sodium chloride 'salt." It made me forget for a moment The smells and the formulte, And it trembled away into silence With a sound which I thought was D. I have sought, but I seek it vainly, That A, B, C divine; I heard it in my childhood, But now I am nearly nine. I learn about nitric acid, I learn about NH3; Perhaps when I go to the Techitical School I shall learn my A, B, C.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0229" n="art0229">
        <head>FABLE-BUT NOT FALSE.</head>
        <p>A FABLE-BUT NOT FALSE.</p>
        <p>The desire of liberty sometimes develops in imprisoned animals new instincts which result in their employment of remarkable clever stratagems. An unusual story is told by a French scientific authority of the odd adven- tures of a mouse. Some time ago a dwarf mouse was confined in a glass jar by means of a piece of canvas tied over the top. The little creature was well fed and happy until one day someone through a hole in the canvas let down a bit of twine as far as the straws. This animal, wlich is endowed with great agility, instantly climbed up the straw, caught hold of the string, pulled herself up to the can- vas, and immediately began with her sharp little teeth to chew a hole in the cloth. The jar was then shaken so that she fell to the bottom and the string was withdrawn. After this, however, the mouse was no longer at ease, but was constantly climbing up the straws as if looking for the string. Some heads of wheat were now put in the jar in order to amuse her, and she gave herself up to gormandising until these were consumed. Then she was observed to be carrying the long- est straw and fixing it upright against the side of the jar. In this way she made a ladder, and one morning she was found to have escaped and to be running about the house. With some difficulty she was captured and put back, where- upon she hid herself as if in shame in the bunch of straw.</p>
        <p>Who knows but what,, like King/ Bruce's spider, she may merely have been meditating how to "Try again."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0230" n="art0230">
        <head>LANCASHIRE JUNIOR EXHIBITIONS.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE JUNIOR EXHIBITIONS.</p>
        <p>The following children from public elementary schools have, among others, gained Junior Ex- hibitions under the Lancashire Education Com- mittee Herbert L. Peattie, Henry Campbell, Hilda Hammersley, George Bouldin, Herbert Hughes, William Boylan, John Coxhill, William J. Cushnahan, Eric Cooper, Jack R. Hartland, Ernest Hartles, Clarence W. Cartwright, Wil- liam S. Richardson, and Edward Martley, of Widnes Sim's Cross Council School; Elizabeth Sparks and Thomas B. Jones, of Widnes West Bank Council School; William E. Davies, of Widnes Waterloo-road C.E. School; Florence J. Williams, Edward Wolfe and Margaret M. Grif- fiths, of Earlestown Central Council School; Alfred Bailey, Thomas It Fairclough, Ethel Dickenson, and Ellen Sephton, of Earlestown District School; Wilfred Hunter, of Penketh Council School; Sidney A. Walker, of Newton- in-Makerfield, Wargrave C.E. School. Elsie Greenhalgh, of Golborne Parochial School; Sarah J. Bell and Gwendoline Hayes, of Gol- borne Central Council School. George F. K. Ormsby, of Wigan Grammar School, and Marion Hutchen, of St. Helens Cowley Girls' School, gained Open Junior Exshibitions.</p>
      </div>
