      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0233" n="art0233">
        <head>AMATEUR PHOTOGRAPHY.</head>
        <p>AMATEUR PHOTOGRAPHY.</p>
        <p>Work in the Dim Light of the</p>
        <p>Dark Room.</p>
        <p>AN ALL-ROUND DEVELOPER.</p>
        <p>It was quite an unnecessary act for Messrs. Burroughes, Welcome and Co. to send me a sample case of tabloids of their developer "Rytol," so that I might try .it and report. I have used no other developer, hardly, than "Rytol " for at least three years and intend to keep on with it. For any amateur li-ho works with an enlarger it is an ideal developer. It makes negatives of wOntleitul softness and a rich greyish black colour, and the silver deposit is so very transparent that printing either in the enlarging lantern or in the, contact frame is far quicker than when the negative has been developed with metol-hydroquinone or with pyro-soda. Its action is so even, both as regards the quality of deposit, and during .the develop- ment, as to leave nothing to wish for on that score. It has never stained a single negative of mine, out of hundreds; it does not stain nor otherwise injure the hands, and with care it is quite as cheap as most other developers. And lastly, it is always at hand. One tabloid from each tube is dissolved in 4 ounces of water, and your developer for negatives or bromide papers is ready. In the course of a good many years' dabbling in photography I have tried a great assortment of developers, but this is the most reliable all-round preparation I have yet•found.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0234" n="art0234">
        <head>DARK ROOM BLINDNESS.'</head>
        <p>DARK ROOM BLINDNESS.'</p>
        <p>Many workers experience great difficulty in getting used to the dim light of the dark room, especially when dark-red or dark-green safe- lights are in use. When using a dark-green light something like ten minutes is often neces- sary before we can see anything at all, but then the trouble vanishes suddenly, and we can see quite easily; in fact, far better than we can with a brighter red light The period of pre- liminary blindness can be very materially shortened by various dodges, the efficacy of which seems to vary with different people. One method is this: After a minute or so in the in- visible red light turn up the white light and then turn it out again. This often has an in- stantaneous effect, though sometimes one or two flashes of white light are required. Another method that generally works still better with ourselves is to go back for a moment or so into daylight, and then re-enter the dark room. Though at the first entry the room seems quite dark, at the eecond it often appears quite light. As a rule it is quite useless starting work in a blind condition, and any dodge that will dis- pel this condition as soon as possible is valu- able.—" British Journal of Photography."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0235" n="art0235">
        <head>WELLINGTON " B.B." PAPER.</head>
        <p>WELLINGTON " B.B." PAPER.</p>
        <p>Messrs. Wellington and Ward have just issued a new developing formula for use with their "B.B." (brown black) .bromide paper, the revised formula yielding a tone of considerably greater warmth. The developer is made up as follows: Metol, 2 grs.; hydroquinone, 200 grs.; soda sulphite cryst., 1,000 grs. ; soda carbonate cryst., 1,000 grs.; potass bromide, 10 grs. ; water to 80 ozs. At a temperature of from 60 to 65 deg. F. development is complete in from 1+ to 2 minutes, and as the print gains depth steadily from the start there is plenty of opportunity for the printer to control the character of the final result. The extremely pleasing extra warm tone which this developer gives should further popularise the Wellington "B.B." paper, which as regards the richness and grada- tion of the prints apart from colour is a beauti- ful product, and one which is admirably suited for the most handsome effects in photographic portraits. t"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0236" n="art0236">
        <head>SNAPSHOTS.</head>
        <p>SNAPSHOTS.</p>
        <p>Holiday Photographers Beware !—While in the act of taking views of Southsea Castle a picture postcard photographer was arrested and detained for three hours, during which period the negatives taken were developed. The authorities decided to destroy the plates and to release the photographer.</p>
        <p>Carbon Printing—Why So Called.—lt is sixty years since photographers found out that on coating glass plates or paper with an adhesive or sticky substance, as gelatine, and then dust- ing on lamp black or powdered blacklead (two forms of finely divided carbon), the coating could be made sensitive to light by immersing in certain, chemicals. Messrs. Gamier and Salmon first used ferric citrate, but were not long bcfore they discovered the advantages of bichromate of potash as a sensitiser. Thus their name, " Proced4 au Charbon" has stuck to this method of printing, although for years most of the pigments have not had the remotest relation to carbon in their composition.</p>
