      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0246" n="art0246">
        <head>=ace</head>
        <p>=ace</p>
        <p>is such a welcome change to the old-fashioned kind of sauces.</p>
        <p>Those who previously never used sauces now enjoy H.r every meal every day</p>
        <p>FEWER LANCASHIRE TEACHERS.</p>
        <p>At the Lancashire Education Committee, Pres- ton, on Monday, Sir Henry Hibbert, who pre- sided, explained the new regulations passed by the Bostrd of Education with regard to the pre- liminury education of teachers, and said that the additional grants had been made in the hope that a larger number of young people might he' induced to come forward and be educated for the profession. The following proposals were adopted by the committee : (1) That bursars be selected by examination from pupils in secondary schools at 15 years of age instead of 16 years of age, and that they be recognised as such for two years instead of one; that during the first year of recognition they be paid a maintenance allowance of 1:10, and in the second year a maintenance allow- an of £l5; that they be granted free tuition, I-4th travelling expenses, if any, and an annual u:lowancu of £1 for books, and that in cases of</p>
        <p>children from rural areas in attendance at sec-</p>
        <p>ondary schools a sustenance allowance of 10s. Gd. per week be paid to students intending to became teachers, when selected to become bursars, be granted, in addition to the allowances above- mentioned, if the students are unable to travel to and from their homes each day. .</p>
        <p>- (2) That children between thirteen and four- teen years of age, from elementary schools, selected by examination to become teachers, be granted free tuition for at least three years, with travelling expenses and an annual allowance of £1 for books, and a maintenance allowance of £5, £lO, and £l5 respectively in each year of attendance; and that pupils from rural areas be granted in addition an allowance of 10s. 6d. per week in lieu of travelling expenses in those cases where they are unable to travel to and from their houses each day.</p>
        <p>The Metropolitan Asylums Board proposes to purchase at a cost of £lOO a steam potato-peeling machine. It was stated that the work of peeling potatoes at present occupied practically the whole time of several maids.</p>
        <p>I</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0247" n="art0247">
        <head>WORK-A-DAY WONDERS. VI.—TELLS OF THE TREASURE HIDDEN IN THE Borram DRAWER OF AN OLD BUREAU.</head>
        <p>WORK-A-DAY WONDERS. VI.—TELLS OF THE TREASURE HIDDEN IN THE Borram DRAWER OF AN OLD BUREAU.</p>
        <p>" Well," said Patty, "of all the pleasant take. ins I ever had there never was one that quite came up to what I got on that Tuesday after- noon that I Diamond-dyed Mrs. King's parlour curtains. "When I got to the house I found the dear old soul muddling about with the curtains, try- ing her hardest to get them ready for me, and her poor old hands were all of a tremble over them. So I just made her let me bring her armchair out into the washhouse, where she could sit and watch me—and I did EVERYTHING; talking to her all the while and telling her how much more satisfactory Diamond Dyes ace than the one-packet-for-everything dyes, and explain- ing all about the Diamond Dye Book of Direc- tions which the chemist gives away with each packet. "Old Mrs. King's curtains were a beautiful quality pinky damask, most dreadfully faded. I worked away with them till I had got them to a lovely deep crimson, and the dear old soul cried with delight over them. "And then came the surprise. She opened the bottom drawer of her bureau and took out an armful of peach-blossom poplin—the rich lovely quality they used to make sixty years ago. ''Twas my wedding dress, dearie,' she said; 'eight full widths in the skirt; and I've had it picked to pieces for years thinking of get- ting it dyed black. But now it's YOURS, and I'll LOVE you to have it. The only thing I ask is that you Diamond-dye it to a light purple and let me come to your house and see you do it.' (to be continued.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0248" n="art0248">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="2" unit="words">
