      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0249" n="art0249">
        <head>WOMAN'S WORLD.</head>
        <p>WOMAN'S WORLD.</p>
        <p>CHAT and GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>By MARJORIE.</p>
        <p>Foreign Visitors to London. Numbers of Indian gentlemen have already arrived in London, and their lithe, slim figures, in Eastern dress, with white flowing headgear, make them conspicuous in the streets. Ameri- cans have come in thousands, and London store- keepers, milliners, and tailors are hoping that the Argentine millionaires, who spend their money so freely in Paris, will cross the Channel ; and favour them with their custom. The Park is very gay with equestrians in the morning, and the afternoon drive in barouche, landau, or Victoria is to be revived, for the horse is coming into favour again. Motors do not show off pretty toilettes, as the old-fashioned horse carriage did. The motor car has, in fact, greatly levelled down the distinction which used to be enjoyed by carriage folk. Tradespeople and others who never thought of keeping a car- riage, now drive about in style. All sorts and conditions of people may be seen using motor cars, and there is consequently a disposition in fashionable quarters to return to more exclu- sive equipages for the afternoon drive.</p>
        <p>French Freak Fashions. The disappearance of that good taste and ele- gance which have for centuries distinguished French fashions, is a matter for sincere regret, and strenuous efforts are being made to put a stop to the introduction of freakish, eccentric, and extravagant modes. Smart women of the highest social position started the protest, which has now the support of artists, painters and the best actresses. The deterioration in taste is said to be due to the influence of foreign firms and foreign visitors. Berlin styles have been favoured by Parisiennes for some time, and the greater number of the leading houses have found it to their financial advantage to cater for the wealthy birds of passage, rather than for the dwellers in the capital. The latter de- mand refinement and simple elegance, but the former being vulgarly ostentatious, require a lavish display of costly materials, and outre.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0250" n="art0250">
        <head>MEMS. FOR MOTHERS. Useful Laundry Hints.</head>
        <p>MEMS. FOR MOTHERS. Useful Laundry Hints.</p>
        <p>To make white linen a good colour, add a squeeze or two of blue to the water in which it is boiled.</p>
        <p>To set colours, dissolve one tablespoonful of common salt in each quart of rinsing water. To revive colours, add one tablespoonful of ' vinegar to each quart of rinsing water. To render articles non-inflammable dissolve two ounces of alum in one gallon of cold water, and use for rinsing. To wash lace, squeeze in hot, soapy water, then in cold. Dip in milk to stiffen it. Press on the wrong side with a fairly hot iron. To make cream lace look fresh and new, add a few drops of Condy's fluid to the rinsing water. This makes it a lovely colour, light or dark, according to the quantity used. Boiling Clothes. When boiling soiled clothes, try putting a slice of lemon in the pot and boiling it along with the clothes. This removes all the dirt and stdins, and makes the clothes nice and white. Mildew stains can be quite easily removed from linen in the following way :—Rub a little soap over the stained parts, then on top of this a little chalk and lemon juice. Put the garments out in the sun for a few hours, and then wash in the usual way. You will find that the stains will have quite disappeared. Cold Water Starch.</p>
        <p>After using cold water starch, don't throw what remains of it away. Put it on one side, and when the starch has settled pour off the clear water. Stand the bowl in the oven for a few minutes, and you will find that the starch has formed into a hard cake, which can be used another time. Just a very little soap added to starch prevents the iron from sticking, and gives the linen a beautiful gloss.</p>
        <p>Solomon. A child in a Sunday school was asked what he know about Solomon, and said: "He was very fond of animals." Being asked why he thought so, he replied: "Because he had three hundred porcupines."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0251" n="art0251">
        <p>styles, and for these they are quite willing to pay extravagantly.</p>
        <p>White Muslin Tunics for Ascot. Dresses for Ascot are being prepared in Lon- don and Paris. This function is always re- garded as important from a sartorial point of view, since new fashions and styles are on their trial until that event takes place. The modes at Ascot are, as a rule, stamped with the ap- proval of leaders of fashion, and constitute the prevailing styles for the rest of the season. The muslins to be used are in the loveliest de- signs, and the embroideries are unsurpassingly beautiful. White muslin tunics to be warn over coloured taffetas skirts are exquisite specimens of the skill of the embroidress. The taffetas cape with gorgeous linings, and much be-ruched, will be greatly in evidence. Lace flounces, most artistically and cunningly arranged, will also be to the fore. Many of the taffetas capes pre- pared in Paris are in black or blue, and lined with the new flowered muslins, whilst others are of this muslin lined with taffetas of the softest thinnest make.</p>
        <p>The Importance of Collars. Plain white muslin is to be used for blouses as well as for tunics this summer. Shirts, too, are made of it, and these, when of fine white linen, have collars and cuffs of pigne. A new make of uncrushable muslin is chiefly used for these garments. Collars are, in fact, a very im- portant feature of this season's fashions, and are in numerous styles, patterns and designs. A. pretty new model for a shirt had a sailor collar, not very deep, at the back, and revers cut from it, which gradually widened as they bordered the V front. A narrow edging of lace had French knots for a beading. Fine lawn, baptiste, net and muslin are all used for collars, and crochet buttons are seen on many plain shirts. Some of the latter look remark- ably like coats, only they lack the basques now considered indispensable. A breast pocket, set very low on either side, adds to the coat-like appearance, especially when the shirt is doubled breasted.</p>
        <p>Frilly and Pleated Skirts. Some skirts of the new taffetas gowns are a mass of frills, the effect of which is like the petals of a rose. The upper part of the sleeves of these dresses are plain, and of net, or some transparent fabric, but from the elbow to the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0252" n="art0252">
        <head>COOKERY CORNER.</head>
        <p>COOKERY CORNER.</p>
        <p>Some Simple Recipes.</p>
        <p>A good haricot bean soup can be made thus: For two quarts of soup wash and then soak over night one pint of haricot beans. In the morning drain and put to cook in cold water, adding one-third cup of well-washed rice if desired, boil slowly for two hours. When done rub through a colander, thin with boiling water, and season with a little nutter or butter. Salt to taste.</p>
        <p>Bran Stock. For every quart of stock desired boil a cup of good wheat bran in three pints of water for two or three hours, or until reduced one-third. This stock may be made the base of a variety of palatable and nutritious soups by flavouring with different vegetables, and seasoning with a little cream, butter, or oil. The stock may be flavoured with celery or by the addition of a quantity of strained, stewed tomatoes. To one quart of bran stock may also be added lentil pulp to make it the consistency desired.</p>
        <p>Potato Soup. For each quart of soup required, take three medium-sized potatoes, pare, cut in slices, and cook rapidly in sufficient water to cover them. When tender, rub through a colander. Then return to the fire and add three cups of water, or milk if possible, a little butter or oil, add salt to taste. Let the soup come to a boil, add a tablespoonful of brown flour rubbed smooth in a little water, boil a few minutes and serve. A slice of onion or a stalk of celery may be sim- mered in the soup to flavour it and then re- moved.</p>
        <p>Tinned Food. The danger in tinned food is due to the pre- sence of air in the tin, the result of defective packing. A simple test is to press the bottom of the tin with the thumb; if it makes a noise similar to the suction noise in a sewing-machine oil-can it is not free from air, and its contents are probably not fit for use.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0253" n="art0253">
        <head>task Nom .iscaa vas, Nam Num vsia Nam Norbi. NI" 'GM Valk VON NOW Valk VIIIN Wok VOW vilik Volk Nagsk *llk VOW NW" Vs" Wok Wilk 'ask</head>
        <p>task Nom .iscaa vas, Nam Num vsia Nam Norbi. NI" 'GM Valk VON NOW Valk VIIIN Wok VOW vilik Volk Nagsk *llk VOW NW" Vs" Wok Wilk 'ask</p>
        <p>COMPLETE EXAMINER STORY.</p>
        <p>WILY MISS WILTON.</p>
        <p>W. E. NORRIS (Author of " The Square Peg," "Not Gullty," etc.).</p>
        <p>"We will not," said Mr. Hickson, stand- ing with his back to the fire and addressing his son, " waste more words upon an issue go clearly defined. You are determined, you tell me, to marry a woman who skips about the boards of a London theatre under the name of Letty Maltravers. You point out that you are of age, assert that you are of sound mind, and claim the right to marry anybody you like. I, on my side, have mere- IT to repeat the answer that I gave you some time ago. Marry, since you are bent upon it, a woman whom neither your mother nor I can receive; only understand that on the day when you take that step, the handsome al- lowance which you have hitherto had from me will be reduced to a sum of £3OO per an- num and that you will get nothing more than a corresponding amount of capital on my death. There is no occasion for us to quarrel; but we both now know how we stand, and I believe you are pretty well aware that when I say a thing, I mean it." " Same here," quietly observed the tall, dark-haired young man with the slight, up- turned moustache who sat facing the speak- er.. A. looL:r.on would probably have judoi.xl that the appearance of the two men Ls such as to lend support to their common boast. The elder—square:shouldered, giley- bearded, keen-eyed, architect of his own very large fortune, proprietor of the many broad acres Which surrounded Dropfield Hall, and member of Parliament for the neighbouring borough of Netherhampton— had the air of one born to command; the i younger—subaltern n a smart cavalry regi- ment which he would assuredly have to quit, should he persist in his matrimonial craze— had inherited the paternal mouth and chin, and possessed, in addition, a certain aspect of sleepy serenity and obstinacy which w h all his own. Neither showed the _slithtea:t I sign of anger, while each (so a ear-on might have guessed) secretly anticited p that the other would end by giving in. lolow- ever, there was no looker-on, except poor old Mrs. Hickson, who scarcely counted as such, for her vision was blurred by tears. To Mrs. Hickson it was all very dreadful indeed. Dreadful that Hugh should have been. leastray by this wicked, designing actress; ress'al- most more dreadful that he should threat- ened by pecuniary ruin; most dreadful of all that she should be severed for the rest of her days from the son whom she adored. Be- cause, although John might choose to talk about not quarrelling, it was only too obvi- ous that nothiwr short of surrender be one of</p>
        <p>rue disputants could possibly avert a quar- rel. So Mrs. Hickson, conscious of personal impotence, could but twist her jewelled fin- gers together miserably- and weep. She sheltered herself hastily behind a newspaper when the butler came in and handed a card to Mr. Hickson, who, after glancing at it, grunted out: "Oh, all right ! Show the lady into the library." The card exhibited the name of Miss Vio- let Wilton, beneath which was scribbled in pencil " from. The Universe.' " Mr. Hickson had promised to grant an interview to a re- presentaive of that enterprising journal, and, although he was not best pleased at finding that he was to be catechised by a fe- male emissary, he recognised the neces- sity of receiving her. He was, in one way and another, a prominent personage, and a General Election was imminent, and to give offence to "The Universe " would be to make a rather bad start. Moreover, long ex- perience had taught him how to deal with interviewers—even with the voluble, discur- sive feminine variety of the genus—so he told himself that he would be rid of this one in twenty minutes, at the outside. If at the end of half an hour he had not yet got rid of her and was in no great hurry to do so, it was not only because Miss Wil- ton, so far from being voluble or discursive, proved to be singularly quick and practical in her methods, much less because she was quite young, nice-looking and becomingly dressed. What appealed to Mr. Hickson, as a man of affairs, was the girl's grasp of the business that she had in hand. Her highly eulogistic sketch, which she read aloud to him, was already complete, save for the per- sonal touches which she added to it while she talked; the few queries that she put were pertinent, not impertinent; her synop- stnioos.te, oibsfetosiohlec, rt aisanshaude jrmte ceaqupturp e smi r eupdru bnl ioc ue laureene i ga id titles hered chin ushe tchl ohseedr h pen- cilcr reflectively; " I think I've got it all now, except the local colouring. I should like, if I may, just to have a look round the house and grounds before I go. Oh, and, by the way, Mr. Hickson, there's this affair of your SO n and Lett y Maltravers. What am I. to. say, about it ?'' a; You will say nothing at all about it," swered Mr. Hickson, turning a displeased countenance upon his bland interrogator. Miss Wilton met his gaze with unruffled composure. " Just as you think best," she returned; "only there has been a great deal yoiii yfoo uutraol talk own,ruythoculensksstniostiewienrciYat yn d. p ellofphl leea rr might tt a e eevveeunnts to advise, I would point out that a very good effect would be produced by your intimating that you don't really intend to cut the offen- der adrift." "My dear young lady," answered Mr. Hickson, "I cannot allow my. domestic con- cerns to be dragged into a political cOntest."</p>
        <p>"Oh, very well," said Miss Wilton, good- humouredly. "After the kind way in Which you have received me, I must of course re- spect your decision, though I am afraid it may cost you votes. lam simply bound to make some allusion to the matter in my arti- cle; but I'll let you down as lightly as possi- ble. I shall say, Upon this delicate topic Mr. Hickson is evidently disinclined to en- large. He smilingly puts it by and proposes a visit to his famous conservatories and hot- houses.' "</p>
        <p>Mr. Hickson had not been going to pro- pose any such thing; yet he presently found himself exhibiting the orchids of which he was justifiably proud to an appreciative, in- telligent and sympathetic visitor. Curious. ly appreciative and intelligent, this newspa- per woman; more and more sympathetic, too, while she chatted easily about this, that and the other. Mr. Hickson, who entertained old-fashioned ideas respecting the legitimate sphere of feminine activity, was not disposed to view lady journalists over favourably; still he had a hearty esteem for all fellow - mortals, male or female, who know how to earn an independent livelihood somehow or other. She was an orphan, she told him, in re- ply to a in which he felt sufficiently in- terested n her to ask; her father, a country parson, had left her but a few hundred pounds; so, having fortunately discovered that she had a knack for her present craft, she had adopted it. "I suppose," she remarked, "you would Aot approve. You would think governessing much safer and more respectable. Well, per- haps. Not nearly as amusing though, and so long as one has sense enough to take care of one's self, what does one's trade matter ? Personally, if I could dance like Letty Mal- travers, I'd apply to-morrow for the part that she's throwing up." "Then," said Mr. Hickton sternly, "Y' would do a very wrong and foolish thing. Yours is a perfectly honourable avocation. That it is not exempt from dangers for a lady who is very young and—er—attractive I don't deny; but no doubt, as you say, you can protect yourself from annoyance and in- suit. Ballet-girls couidn't do that if they would, and I am afraid not many of tilers wou:d if they could." " I daresay you know all about them," Miss Wilton obsnved demurely. " I have been told that they are neither better nor worse than their neighbours; but one can't believe all one hears. Anyhow, I quite un- derstand your not wanting your son to marry one of them. What I don't understand is your threatening to disinherit him. That sort of thing is so out of date ! And it's al- ways a nitv for a public man to be out of date. /should have ftought a much better plan would be just quietly to cure him of his disease. Apply a counter-irritant, I mean. He's only three or four and twenty, isn't he ? "</p>
        <p>"Not twenty-four yet; but—' "My dear sir, one need never despair of them at that age. Stave the crisis off and get him to fall in love with somebody else. Any moderately good-looking girl will do." Dar. Hickson was a good deal amused at the notion of directing destiny in this off-hand style. "You don't know my soil Hugh," said he. with a grim smile.</p>
      </div>
