        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY 23, 1914.</p>
        <p>wrist, frills, puffings, or rachings are as thick as they can be placed. Lace collars of the standa-outhetybpaecka.ceTwilPanY these dresses, but They make a pretty frame to the head and face. kilted skirts in the new cotton and summer fabrics are charming under the long tunic, and when worn with a basqued blouse make ideal tennis dresses. They com- bine grace with comfort, not a very usual com- bination. For tennis, white or cream wincey is to be preferred to cotton voile, or crepe, as it is more durable and almost as light.</p>
        <p>Earrings and Necklaces. Ears, or at any rate, the lobes of these organs, are now permitted to show beneath the hair dressed high on the head, and as a consequence the earring has returned to favour. Long drops of pearls, crystal studs and pendants, that are reminiscent of the old-fashioned lustre, add a softening effect or a brilliance to an evening toilette. Some girls wear strings of pearls, or brightly coloured crystal beads round the neck, with a decollete blouse. Truly lovely are the colours of some of these crystals, such as hyacinth blue, amber, aquamarine, rose and jade. Transparent hats for wear with the high coiffure are shown in many of the best houses. In tulle, with brim-edged Greek lace, a Breton cap is very dainty. These hats .when of the Niniche or Dolly Varden type, are laden with fruit, with which flowers are indiscriminately mixed. One hat, for instance, had most natural red currants on one side and black on the other. Between the two was a strap of tiny rosebuds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0256" n="art0256">
        <head>Return of the Fan.</head>
        <p>The Return of the Fan.</p>
        <p>That fans .are to be used again this summer will be welcome news to many women who pos- sess beautiful specimens of this daillty and charming accessory to an elegant toilette. Queen , Victoria favoured the fan, and possessed several of rare beauty, painted during the reign of the unfortunate Louis XVI., which, with the pre- ceding reign, might be termed the golden age of the fan. Queen Mary has a fine collection , also, containing a few that are matchless for the beauty of their design, and the skill of the painter. Lace and feather fans will be first favourites this year. Tortiseshell, which has such a vogue just now, and mother-of-pearl are used for sticks. Painted fans in Watteau de- signs are also favoured, and many of painted lace are shown.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0257" n="art0257">
        <head>HINTS ON HEALTH.</head>
        <p>HINTS ON HEALTH.</p>
        <p>Little Things to Remember.</p>
        <p>Rheumatism in one joint, such as ankle or toe, can arise in a predisposed subject by too much exertion. For instance in the case men- tioned too much standing would set it up. The cure consists in rest to the joint, covering it with cotton wool and a well-applied flannel bandage, and taking a mixture containing Salleylate of Soda. Sometimes painting with lodine is most valuable.</p>
        <p>Chalky Soil and Rheumatism. It is a fact that rheumatic ailments are more apt to develop on a chalky soil than on a gravel one. But it is an easy matter to obviate this, for it is because the water is drunk that the disease appears. Anyone subject to rheumatism and compelled to live on such a soil should instal a domestic still, such as the "Gem," on the kitchen hob, and thus rid the water of any impurity. In this way not only rheumatism, but many other ailments may be cured.</p>
        <p>Superfluous Hair. Electrolysis for the removal of superfluous hairs on the face or elsewhere is quite successful. But it is very disappointing unless the patient's pocket is well lined and her time is at her own disposal. For lam convinced, from long experi- ence, that it is not possible to stop when you have once commenced until every apparent or even potential hair follicle is destroyed. From thirty to fifty can be removed at one sitting, and even the most soft-hearted operator must charge for this half a guinea, while better known men may charge as much as two guineas. But what are thirty hairs among so many, for the upper- lip and chin are covered with an immense num- ber of downy hairs of no account until the larger ones are removed, when the energy thus set free soon causes them to develop, when they in their turn must be removed. In this way from 20 to 200 sittings may be necessary. for the complete eradication of hair upon chin or lip, and this means both money and time. That is why most ladies are deluded by false advertisements, or else prefer depilatories, or, in some cases, even , the razor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0258" n="art0258">
        <head>`mak Isalk 'Wok lk lOW %lOW VIM Wok</head>
        <p>`mak Isalk 'Wok lk lOW %lOW VIM Wok</p>
        <p>ttowever, brie was soon to make good that deficiency. The inspection of the hothouse's had occupied sonic time, and it was now so nearly the luncheon hour that Mr. Hickson felt constrained to offer a hospitality which was promptly accepted. Thus it was that surprising dings came to It was not, to be sure so very surprising that the strang- er, should at once win her way to the good graces of simple, kind-hearted Mrs. Hickson; pass. but to convert the bored indifference with which Hugh sat down to table into the inter- ested animation which he displayed before he rose was really no small feat, and this Miss Wilton accomplished without any visi- ble effort. Her host, watching her and list- ening to her, could not but alma that she was an uncommonly clever girl, and li,, opini- on of her rose still higher when, as he was es- corting her to the front door, (for she had to leave immediately after luncheon) he over- heard his son murmuring something. to her about meeting in London. He also heard her laughing reply of— " I'll say goodbye now, please. I want one last word with Mr. Hickson before I go." This last word, which doorstep, was not less frank than her previ- ous utterances. was spoken on the " Well," said she, " there you are ! I thought I'd just show you. It's as easy as that, you see.,, " I see what is easy to you," was Mr. Hick- son's slightly beyirildered rePI,Y• " Whether it would question." be easy to anybody else is another He paused; then resumed, with that swift decision whereby so many of his commercial triun4phs had been won:— "It has been E. great pleasure to make your acquaintance, Miss Wilton, and I should be sorry to think that we were not to foregather again. In about a fortnight as you know, the election is to take place. Possibly the spectacle might have some value for you from a professional point of view. If so, and if you cared to run down to us as our guest for the last few days, we should be very glad indeed to see you. While he made this somewhat audacious proposition, his clear eyes stared straight at hers, which were dancing with merriment. Of course she realised—was, in truth, meant to realise—why she was asked; but she took no umbrage. Oh, tnanks awfully," she reVed. " Yes' I'll come. It will be rather fun, think." And, as if this were not a sufficiently plain intimation that she accepted the part as- signed to her— " Chain up your son in the meantime," she went on. " Make out that he's wanted here for shooting parties or something. The essential is to prevent him from returning to London and his Letty." * * * • * * * Upon the morning of the day appointed for the taking of the poll at Netherhampton Mr. Hickson passed the events of the previ- ous fortnight in mental review and asked himself whether he was satisfied or not. On the whole, he felt able to reply in the affirma- tive. His return was virtually assured, and although at some of the meetings there had been rude interruptions relating to his fami- ly affairs, it had undoubtedly told in his fav- our that his son had almost always been at his side on the olatform. He wait entitled.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0259" n="art0259">
        <head>BORSTAL TREATMENT.</head>
        <p>BORSTAL TREATMENT.</p>
        <p>System That Saves Youthful Offenders from Life of Crime.</p>
        <p>SHORT STUDY IN CRIMINOLOGY.</p>
        <p>What is the Borstal system? That is a ques- tion one frequently hears when the magistrates have dealt with a boy or girl by sending the young offender to a Borstal Institution. Briefly put, it means that the delinquent is sentenced to reformative detention rather than actual punishment in the hope that the end of the term may find hire (or her) worthy of re- storation to honest citizenship. Under the Borstal plan, every boy is taught a trade well calculated to equip him for an indus- trial career, such as baking, building, black- smithing, bricklaying, cooking, carpentry, farm- ing, gardening, and shoemaking. Here is the day's procedure :-5.30 a.m.: A drink of milk and a biscuit. 7.0: Breakfast (bread, porridge, and margarine, with tea or coffee for special grade, i.e., good conduct boys). 7.30: Work in the workshops, or in the open, or in cleaning the premises. 12.0: Dinner (bread, potatoes and meat, and soup or suet pudding). 1.10 to 5.30: General work. 5.30: Work ceases and a parade of the boys takes place. This is fol- lowed quickly by supper, consisting of bread, cocoa, margarine or cheese. 6.15: Chapel, ad- dressed by the officers or by visitors, when occa- sion is taken to read aloud letters from former inmates who have done well since discharge and who write by way of encouragement to the boys still undergoing the treatment. Boys who have had the good sense to behave properly and so have attained the special grade are allowed an hour's recreation in the evening, with games, chess, draughts, &amp;c., and newspapers and maga- zines. Those who have not earned this promo- tion may read in their cells till eight. A system of evening classes similar to those held in this district during the winter months has lately been prepared. The lads, moreover, have access to a fine, well-stocked library, a gymnasium (with shower baths and other conveniences), and the conditions of life are really ideal. In each boy's cell there is a spring bed, with a strip of carpet on the floor, and when the boy has ob- tained a certain total of good conduct marks he is allowed the privilege of having photographs of his friends and other pictures placed on the walls. In "The Modern Prison Curriculum" Dr. R. F. Quinton naturally has something to say respecting the benefits of this system, as well as the satisfactory results which are ex- pected in due course to ensue upon the newly- inaugurated regime of preventive detention. As a matter of fact, in their last report the Prison Commissioners state that in 20 years convicted male offenders between the ages of 16 and 21 have fallen from 18,000 to 8,000, or more than 50 per cent., while female offenders have de- creased from 4,000 to less than 1,000, or 75 per cent. The credit for this is given to the Borstal system, along with the Probation and Children Acts; and there is to be further legislation along these lines.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0260" n="art0260">
        <head>HUMANE-BUT NO "CODDLING."</head>
        <p>HUMANE-BUT NO "CODDLING."</p>
        <p>As to convict treatment generally, readers of "The Examiner" who are at all interested in the subject will remember that just three years ago a new system was inaugurated whereby dis- charged prisoners are officially provided with the opportunity of retrieving the past in a way that was not possible previously. It had been hoped to do away with the active police super- vision generally exercised in the case of every ticket-of-leave man. Its entire supersession was not practicable, but in many cases discharged prisoners come directly under the influence of societies interested in their welfare, and are free of police control so long as they prove worthy of that freedom. This has been brought about by the establishment of a central agency whose representatives are admitted to the prisons for private conversation with the con- victs in their cells, in order that prompt and souk</p>
        <p>'Welk Vim, 'Wok VBllk %Mk *Pk NoMak VOW. 'NSW VW</p>
        <p>he thought, to take credit for some strate- gic skill in that he had contrived to detain his son at Dropfield the whole time, and it was gratifying that not once during the course of it had the young man alluded to Miss Letty Maltravers. On the other hand, what was to be thought about the astonish- ing Miss Wilton, who for several days past had formed one of the rather large party as- sembled under his roof ? There was really only one deduction that could be drawn from her behaviour: she meant to marry Hugh, and what was more, she was going to do it. Of course the unspoken past had in- volved a danger—if danger it ought to be called—which so shrewd a man as Mr. Hick- so'n was not likely to ignore; only he had not regarded it as serious or imminent, because he had good reason to believe that Hugh, whatever else he might be, was not a wea- thercock. But who can explain or account for the whirling tergiversations 0:1 youth P What was beyond question was the complete- ness of Miss Wilton's conquest. Hugh and she had become inseparable; the .young man was her undisguised; abject slave; everybody was laughing about it. Mr. Hickson himself would have laughed, had he not felt it a lit- tle irritating and a little humiliating to be the father of so unstable a fellow. For the rest, let him marry the girl, by all means, if he wanted to marry her. He might, it was true, have done better for himself; but then he had nearly done so very much worse ! Miss Wilton, when all was said, was a gentle- women by birth and manners. However, the time seemed to have arrived for speaking plainly to llugh, who was accordingly drawn aside after breakfast and brought to book with— Now, look here: you've been carrying on for some days in a style which I oughtn't to allow in my house, unless it means what I presume it does. It's my duty to ask you what it means, because I don't forget— though perhaps you do—that scarcely a fort- night ago you were proclaiming your unalter- able determination to marry a dancing girl called Letty Maltravers." "That's all right," answered Hugh com- posedly. "I haven't changed my mind." "The deuce you haven't ! Then all I can say is that you have given Miss Wilton, not to mention myself, grave cause for com- plaint."</p>
        <p>"I don't see it, sir," drawled the young man, as he lighted a cigarette. "You can't fairly complain of me for taking a hand in your transparent game. You ought to have known that I'm a man of my word, like your- self. And, after all, I don't suppose you would consent to my leading Miss Wilton to the altar, would you ? " "Consent !" cried the disconcerted schei- mer; "of course I should consent ! Only too thankful to see yctu married to a girl who is worth a dozen of you ! " "Really? I thought perhaps you and she were only pulling my leg. Sorry to disap- point you; but, you see, I'm pledged to Let- ty, and I don't break my pledges." " Very well," said Mr. Hickson curtly; "go your own way, then. You are aware of what the consequences will be." He strode off. deeulv chagrined. Not only</p>
        <p>adequate arrangements may be made for them when they leave. One welcomes the assurance that convicts are not " coddled " under this scheme. I certainly agree that a "penalty which does not punish" is a grave mistake. The vaunted Elmira (U.S.) treatment does not seem to act as a deterrent, but rather the contrary. Italy and France abolished capital punishment in cases of mur- der, but the latter soon found itself obliged again to enforce the death penalty, and in the former country homicide is much more preva- lent than in our own. All the same, especially in Switzerland and the United States, the fad- dists who make pets of habitual criminals are having a good deal of their own way. Not long ago I read an article showing in detail how in some Swiss prisons the convicts are so gener- ously treated that many ask to be allowed to remain after they have completed their sen- tences. Two men who had been in the prison at Thorberg, near Wassen, told a hardly credible story of the good time they had in gaol. Visitors to gaols in the United States have declared that they had the utmost difficulty in realising that they were among people who had been guilty of breaking the law. Everything possible is done to make the convicts forget their crimes. An official of the lowa State Peni- tentiary has stated that he thought the pris- oners ought to be dressed in tailor-made suits, white shirts, and polished boots, and be allowed plenty of time out of doors. He also advocated the very best cooking as an aid to reform evil- doers!</p>
        <p>We have travelled a certain distance in that direction, but I trust and believe that in this country these experiments will never be car- ried to any such absurd lengths. "Give him a fair trial and acquit him, or put him on proba- tion if you may; but punish if you ought, re- form if you can, and restrain him if you must." That is a statement of the creed adopted by an author whom I have already quoted. Dr. Quin- ton thinks that the indeterminate sentence is the only real remedy for the habitual criminal, and he is inclined to condemn the English senti- ment that views with such timidity any ill-de- fined restraint upon personal liberty. As a re- viewer observed some years ago, in this system, while leniency would be extended to him who, during his confinement, showed no sign of habitual perversity, the irreclaimable criminal could be effectively controlled.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0261" n="art0261">
        <head>CRIMINAL TENDENCIES.</head>
        <p>CRIMINAL TENDENCIES.</p>
        <p>The suggestion of another student of crimin- ology once interviewed by a journalistic friend of my own was that some measure of dealing with criminal tendencies should be formulated which would be neither too harsh nor too lenient, but would obey the legal axiom of tem- pering justice with mercy. A prison (as he very reasonably and sensibly urged) should be a place to inspire a wholesome fear—a place at once punitive and reformatory. It cannot be denied that many old hands have nowadays something like an affection for prison life, and certainly no such feeling existed in the days of the "hulks." Now, as remarked above, a con- vict finds rescue agencies waiting to take him in hand, secure him employment, and render him other assistance which too often is lacking in the case of a poor unfortunate fellow who has remained honest. This is indeed a defect. It is scarcely too much to say that it places a premium on dishonesty; at all events, it pre- sents a problem which the community should seriously endeavour to solve without delay. There are among us odd individuals who advo- cate " reforms " which would unmistakably lead to the coddling of prisoners. Of course, I have no sympathy whatever with their extreme views; on the contrary, I fully share the con- viction that if the extraordinary proposals in question were ever made operative men who had never thought seriously of committing crime would be induced to indulge in it that they might enjoy the comforts of the new prison system, and long sentences would be welcomed by criminals instead of acting as a deterrent. The incorrigible "old lag" should be kept under lock and key and subjected to stern discipline; but of course all possible effort should be made to preserve young offenders from a criminal career—hence I commend the Borstal Institution and the committal thereto of such suitable cases as the one recently sent from Warrington for the special treatment. WINE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0262" n="art0262">
        <head>thstitutes.</head>
        <p>thstitutes.</p>
        <p>• Vige N3224' .46135' '1255k Viglik .1112D1/4 ‘21:15k Nglik Niglik 'oak NIS% Nei teak V11.0401101101k</p>
        <p>was it an anominanie nuisance to no con- fronted once more with consequences which promised to be at least as unpleasant for him as for his son, but there was the mortifica- tion of having been so promptly and con- temptuously detected in what, to be sure, had been a transparent plot enough. He was per- haps scarcely fair in associating Miss Wilton with the uncomplimentary epithets which he heaped upon his own head, still it did look as if Miss Wilton had been rather a goose. He was not sorry that the full programme of the concluding day left him no leisure for private converse with her.</p>
        <p>Whether Miss Wilton was conscious of fail- ure or sanguine of success, she certainly seemed to enjoy that concluding day, during which she drove about the constituency with the rest of. the house-party, looking very bright and pretty, and closely attended by the perfidious Hugh. When, late at night, it was proclaimed that Mr. Hickson headed the poll with a substantial majority, she was among the first to shake the re-elected mem- ber by the hand and congratulate him upon " a splendid and well-deserved victory." " Thanks. Wish I could return the com- pliment," was the somewhat ungracious re- joinder. This was in an upper chamber at the Town Hall, where a large concourse had assembled, and before Miss Wilton, whose countenance had assumed an expression of innocent inter- rogation, could open her lips, Colonel Bat- tershv, the rosy, good-humoured High Sher- iff, tbrust his way towards her. It was to her, not (as might have been expected) to Mr. Hickson, that he addressed himself, and what he salt] was: " Well, Miss Letty, this is making ac- quaintance with the county under happy au- spices—what ? Give you joy with all my heart !--and you too, Hickson. You've done the right thing in the right way, sir. I've no doubt you were as well aware as anybody that there were stupid and malicious rum- ours about your having forbidden your son's marriage and that they weren't doing you any good with the electors; but you're not the man to offer explanations to people who had no right to ask you for them. .17ou pre- ferred to let facts speak for themselves. As for me, I must say that I never gave a mo- ment's credence to that silly talk. I've known Miss Maltravers, on the stage and off it, long enough to know that anybody might be proud to welcome her as a daughter-in-law, and I know you, Hickson, a great deal too well to mistake you for an obstinate, preju- diced jackass." It is not certain that Mr. Hickson alto- gether merited the above encomium; but what may be said for him is that under no circumstances did he lose command of his wits. Enlightened on the instant by Colonel Battersby's harangue and Miss Wilton's sub- dued smile, he replied, without hesitation: "Much obliged to you, Battersby. As you say, I'm in the habit of conducting my own affairs after ray own fashion, and I don't know that I've ever had reason to regret that rule." Not for another quarter of an hour was it possible for him to breathe a word, without being overheard, to the young lady who re- mained at his elbow. Only as they were</p>
        <p>quitting tne rimming 'ctia ne manage t° per in her ear: " A pretty trick you've played We'. two ! I'm sure I don't know what yo- pect to get for your duplicity—" " Oh, but I think you do !" the girl boo interrupted. "It was your fault, n°,.61 remember that a trick had to be reser;oo: And I took care to be as truthful as 'P bly could. My name really is Wiltern my father really was a clergyman, etl,'o g ally did get the " Universe ' people ploy me as an interviewer." Mr. Hickson burst into a short cp,j " Oh, you're clever," he said; " a cleverer than I thought TOU were tai9oo ing, I may tell you. Well, of course the game. If it comes to that, thole don't exactly lose either; for I told ij i should on'!3* be too thankful to see blnoil ried to Miss Wilton, and when I say I mean it."</p>
      </div>
