      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0266" n="art0266">
        <head>COUNTY CHAMPIONSHIP.</head>
        <p>COUNTY CHAMPIONSHIP.</p>
        <p>Cemetery Hotel Teams' Success.</p>
        <p>The Cemetery Hotel men look like bringing county honours home, for on Saturday they knocked out Liverpool Sandon by 63 points, and although there are several "dark horses" still in the competition, one feels almost certain that, with such a set of .skilful and experienced trundlers, the Cemetery Hotel men should pre- vail. In the fourth round, to-day, they meet Leigh Railway, at Flixton, and although it will be a very tight game the chances are that the local players will "pull it off." The Warring- ton team have dropped only 30 points in three games, while their opponents have totalled but 476 out of a possible 750. These figures stand as a sign of consistent brilliant bowling. On Sat- urday they were always the victors, T. Bolton setting a fine example by disposing of J. W. Lawson to the tune of 21-5. The wins by T. Ellison and T. Quarrie were also big ones, and, indeed, every player in the team played with such brilliance as to strike terror in the hearts of opponents. Roebuck are fancied as possible opponents to the Cemetery Hotel in the final, and their performances are such as to give large rays of hope to their backers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0267" n="art0267">
        <head>ASSOCIATION COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>In the second round Bull's Head• and Junior Conservatives were both successful, although Earlestown gave the former a good run, they only being vanquished by a solitary, point. One was sorry to see J. W. Jolley beaten, but J. Spencer played so well that he defeated him by 21-16. Each side suffered unexpected reverses, and although the last set of players on the Bull's Head side played their hardest, two of them had to go under with scores well under 20. The Junior Conservatives were by far the better team against the Bird-in-Hand contin- gent, and they feel quite " cocky " after their 152 points victory. To-day they meet Cheetbam ot Stretford, and Bull's Head have as their opponents Swinton Liberals, at Ermston. Both teams should win, although they will have nothing to throw away.</p>
        <p>SECOND TEAMS. In the second teams' championship, second round, Cemetery Hotel beat Roebuck by four points, after many exciting games had been played. The " locals" won eight of the games, but their majorities were never so great. Bull's Head accounted for Griffin Hotel, Broughton, by 22 points. In the third round Bull's Head meet Whitefield, and the Cemetery Hotel men meet the Lord Nelson men at Pomona Hotel, Reddish.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0268" n="art0268">
        <head>Co ©toe</head>
        <p>Co ©toe</p>
        <p>WORKS ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>PrOMiSillff Teams Win.</p>
        <p>Although it is rather early in the competition to give any definite prediction, one cannot help but notice on form that there are two or three teams who will go a long way farther than the third round, and amongst these will certainly be a team from Rylands'. Cheshire Lines and Thewlis and Griffith?' men were both well han- dicapped, but the railway men were by far the superior set, and won by 65 points, not a losing card being returned on their count. If ever a man should have won 'his game it was J. Lloyd, who started with a handicap of seven against W. Dutton's five, and he led 10-7 at the fourth end. He had everything to inspire his confi- dence, but he lost the jack and Dutton went to 13-10 without any trouble. Lloyd then re- newed his efforts, and when lie had got to 13.7-.14 one thought his chances were "rosy." Dutton, however, came better than was expected ,with one wood and drew away by two points clear. J. Lockett ,and G. Gleave played a strenuous game, but the railway man was always 'pretty near the jack and eventually won by three points. NAYLOR'S TEAM LOSE.</p>
        <p>Rylands' (Wet Shop) had by no i-means an easy thing on when they met the men from Naylor's' and they had to play exceedingly well to be the ultimate victors by such a narrow margin as four points. Naylor's had an advantage in the handicap, but, apart from this, their men never let any chances slip by, and even when they lost individual games the majority was never great. T. Taylor (4) played a consistent game against E. Lewis, who had a start of seven. Although he led 11-6 at the fifth end, Lewis could not maintain his form, and Taylor peeled at the eighth end, only to run to game with a majority of two points. A. Smart (7) and S. Garnett (6) put up a stiff fight for honours, the scores being equal, with each man only wanting one for game. Smart was the fortunate one, i for he held the jack and followed it up n a skilful fashion. F. Cooper (5) and J. Bluck (6) met in a similar game, and although he led all the way up to the fourteenth end Bluck had to retire one point behind. A similar majority separated R. Wicks (4), Naylor's skipper, and F. Philips (5), the latter returning a 15-14 card on the fifteenth length.. E. Lunt (6) led 14-9 against R. Colley (6), but it was not until five more lengths had been played that he won by the odd chalk. Other games of equal interest were those in which A. Stretford and J. Dunn won by 15-13 and 15-14 respectively.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0269" n="art0269">
        <head>LONGFORD VANQUISHED._</head>
        <p>LONGFORD VANQUISHED._</p>
        <p>P. and K. (Bewsey) were handicapped too well for the Longford (No. 1) men, who lost by 17 points. Eight of the P. and K. men followed the example of their skipper, J. Spencer, and returned winning cards. W. Savage (5) and J. Bartley (4) caused some excitement, for the Longford man was put so much about after hav- ing led 12-7 that be was beaten by the odd point in 15 lengths. J. Feeney (6) played a very nice game of howls against A. Scott ‘1), and despite the fact that his opponent was the better exponent he kept at it, and was rewarded by a two-point victory. The last set of players on the P. and K. side made a decided impression on the scores, and, indeed, it was chiefly owing to their wins that the game was concluded with such a majority. EXIT ASYLUM (No. 1).</p>
        <p>Although they played a better game than the Mersey White Lead team, the Asylum players were not fortunate enough to win. W. Rudge (3), the Winwick captain, returned an encourag- ing card against E. Poole, who only added six points to his handicap of seven. Most of the best wins were in favour of the Winwick men, and, indeed, only five of their opponents won their games.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0270" n="art0270">
        <head>TWO POINTS' WIN.</head>
        <p>TWO POINTS' WIN.</p>
        <p>Union Tanneries' team deserved their two- point victory over Crosfield's (Fitting Shop). J. Hill returned a card worth seeing against W. Dickinson, for after having been in arrears practically all through the game, he won by one point. W. Bowers accounted for the Tanneries' captain in a commendable manner. Both were handicapped at five, and the scores were equal at 11, 12, 13, and 14, Bowers just pulling it off by a single. F. Bennett and W. Shaw are also to be complimented on the excellent bowling they dis- played in winning their games.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0271" n="art0271">
        <head>CONTRAST.</head>
        <p>A CONTRAST.</p>
        <p>Between Rylands' (No. 3) win by 27 points over Fletcher's Moulders and Orford's success</p>
        <p>444 L. 914. -) • 8.5. A., Rudge and Sunbeam Motor Cycle.</p>
        <p>r PORD CARS 4111 Agent : W•okKtordnlGHHTeLth Garage.</p>
        <p>th .0 %to ' age for 16 Cars. Inspection Pits. Niro ONLY when Driven by Members of our own Staff.</p>
        <p>_</p>
        <p>by seven points against Carpenters and Joiners there was a decided contrast, although it is singular to note that the former match was the better of the two. The Fletcher's men literally groaned under the heavy weight of handicap points they had to wipe out, but, nevertheless, they put up a splendid show, and one can readily sympathise with them. The last game of the afternoon provided little in the Way of eicitement, large majorities characterising the major portion of the winning cards returned on each side. Orford deserved their victory, for they won seven of the individual games in e clever manner. The last matches in the first round of this competition will be decided this afternoon, after which the draw for the second round will be made.</p>
        <p>e COCO</p>
        <p>PITHY PARS.</p>
        <p>The entry for the "Harold Smith" Cup Com- petition is a record one, and the first round will be played to-day. The opening main of the Parish Church Club took place at the Rectory on Saturday. Some excellent games were witnessed, and the form of Mr. John Craik landed him in the final, only to be defeated by Mr. Antwis, who had also been very consistent in all his games. The result was as follows R. W. Antwis; 2, John Craik; 3, Walter Dennett ; 4, Arthur l3rimelow.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON LIBERAL ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>"Peter Peacock" Challenge Cup.</p>
        <p>The name of the member of the winning team is printed first in each case</p>
        <p>FIRST ROUND—contihued.</p>
        <p>- Whitecross L v. Whitecross K. Whitecross L won by 7 points. William Buckley (3), 12; H. Holt (2), 13. W. Lawton (2), 13; H. Deakin (1), 6. T. Welsby (2), 13; G. T. Walker (5), 12. P. Hardy (3), 11; W. Locker, capt. (3), 13. W. Jones, capt. (scr.), 13; C. Holt (2), 5. W. Savage (scr.), 13; J. Rigby (3), 10. . H. Hardy (3), 5; T. Tweedale (2), 13. G. Wilcock (1), 12; H. Jones (2), 13. Total—Whitecross L, 92; Whitecross K, 85.</p>
        <p>Whitecross H v. Howley A. Whitecross won by 46 points. Peter Wright, capt. (scr.), 13; B. Lloyd (6), 6. W. Farrington (4), 13; C. Derbyshire (3), 3. H. Howard (4), 13; B. Forster (2), 2. J. Barton, sen. (scr.), 12; J. Rigby, jun. 43), 13. A. Lowe (6), 13; E. Pownall (3), 3. W. Head (5), 13; F. Brown, jun. (6), 6. J. Lawless (3), 3; F. Brown, sen. (6), 13. J. Morgan, jun. (3), 13; George Shaw (1), 1. Total—Whitecross H, 93; Howley A, 47.</p>
        <p>Latchford B v. St. John's D. Latchfoid B won by 3 points. T. Millman (4), 13; J. Holt (3), 6. J. Hill (5), 10; W. Newns (3), 13. N. Cook (2), 13; T. Cunningham (4), 8. P. Broadhurst (4), 7; J. Corlett (3), 13. T. Leigh (4), 13; P. McHugh (5), 10. A. E. Richards (3), 12; J. H. Senior, (3), 13. John 'Davies (1), 13; J. Bailey, capt. (5), 10. R. Hitchmougli, capt. (3), 8; I. Holcroft (4), 13. Total—Latchford B, 89; St. John's D, 86.</p>
        <p>St. John's G V. Bewsey C. St. John's G won by 31 points. G. Clive (3), 13; R. Barnett (3), 12.. W. Alcock, capt. (3), 13; J. Lennon (4), 4. A. Molyneux (2), 13; Massey, 9. W. Molyneux, sen. (2), 13; J. Coyne (3), 7. W. Molyneux, jun. (4), 12; Baguley, 13. E. Hardman (6), 13; W. Bouchier (5), 10. W. Dutton, jun. (3), 13; B. Matthews (3), 11. J. Lehrle (4), 13; J. Egan (6), 6. Total—St. John's G, 103; Bewsey C, 72.</p>
        <p>Whitecross M v. St. Austin's F. Whitecross M won by 9 points. , T. Lowe (4), 13; I. Elwell (4), 9. 1 T. Nock (4), 9; J. Oakley (5), 13. W. Unsworth (1), 13; S. Millington NJ 10. R. W. Gray (4), 7; S. Hassall (6), 13. G. Melia (2), 13; J. Graham (2), 8. D. Ingham (2), 11; P. Gleave (scr.), 13. B. Green, capt. (1), 13; P. Walker (1), 7. F. Davenport (4), 13; J. Wilkes, capt. (3), 10. Total—Whitecross M, 92.; St. Austin's F, 83.</p>
        <p>Orford B v. St. John's H. Orford B won by 23 points. Jas. Langley (2), 13; J. Lingard (2), 10. F. Budge (3), 13; H. Leather (3), 4. W. Hatton (6), 9; J. Oakes (3), 13. R. Savory, capt.(scr.), 13; J. W. Pritchard (4), 4. T. Harrington (5), 13; W. H. Stringer (3), 8. T. Bond (3), 3; H. Langley (2), 13. A. Hoyle (6), 12; A. P. Davies (3), 13. Budge (5), 13; J. W. Afeachin, capt. (1), 1. Total—Orford B, 89; St. John's H, 68.</p>
        <p>Whitecross I v. Howley C. Whitecross I won by 27 points. J. Gorst (3), 9; W. Connor (3), 13. W. Boyle (6), 11; J. W. Rigby (1), 13. T. Pownall (5), 6; W. Morgan (3), 13. J. Legge (3), 13; W. B. Lester (3), 3. A. Hands, capt. (1), 13; James Smith (3), 3. R. Sudlow, sen. (4), 13; S. Hatton (3), 8. 0. Wade (3), 13; F. Mather, 6. W. Arnold (5), 13; J. Wrench, capt. (1), 5. Total—Whitecross I, 91; Howley C, 64.</p>
