      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0275" n="art0275">
        <head>CRICKET.</head>
        <p>CRICKET.</p>
        <p>NOTICE.</p>
        <p>We shall be pleased to receive from Secretaries scores and details of bowling in matches played in this locality. Out of the way feats with bat or ball, or in the field, will be Specially welcomed, and will be used as NOTES. Particulars should reach "The Examiner" Office as early in the week as Possible.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0276" n="art0276">
        <head>‘VARRINGTON WIN AGAIN. Fine Victory Over Stockport.</head>
        <p>‘VARRINGTON WIN AGAIN. Fine Victory Over Stockport.</p>
        <p>Warrington cricketers added another victory to their rapidly growing list. Their victims were Stockport, a team of no mean ability, and these the Warringtonians defeated by 74 runs. The game was played at Arpley, on a pitch which was more in favour of the batsmen than the bowlers, and the Warrington trundlers did well in getting Stockport out for the cost of 68 runs. The first Stockport wicket was not , long in falling, Duckworth bringing off a fine catch from a ball delivered by Partridge. This batsman had only two runs to his credit. Hannam's bowling was responsible for six of the subsequent wickets. Bennett was also found. in good form with the ball and took three wickets. The top score for Stockport was the 16 made by Parkinson. The Warrington innings was not long in pro- gress before it was seen that Partridge was to make a good stand. He batted safely and never gave a chance. When, however, he had placed 38 to his credit he was stumped. It was rather hard luck, as he seemed set for the half-century at least. B. Silcock was, another man who wielded the willow with much purpose for Warrington. If he batted a bit slowly lie was sure and placed the ball judiciously. At 24 from his own bat he was clean bowled. Hannam again proved him- self as versatile with the bat as with the ball, and had knocked up 36 before being bowled:</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON .RESERVES DEFEATED. Warrington Reserves journeyed to Stockport to return home defeated. The home side had first lease of the wicket, and found the War- rington bowling not so deadly as it had been in the earlier matches. Stockport were not dis- posed of until they had knocked up 100 runt. Warrington were only able to reply with 69 runs. Bell was the best bowler on the War- rington side, and took five of the wickets. T. Dixon was top of the Warrington batting list with 25, Partridge Coming next with 15 runs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0277" n="art0277">
        <head>TOTAL OF, THREE RUNS.</head>
        <p>A TOTAL OF, THREE RUNS.</p>
        <p>_ There was a remarkable conclusion to the match played at Arpley between- Walker's Brewery and Sankey. The latter side batted first and compiled the respectable score of 75, to which Berry contributed a nice 27. Forster Made 24. When Walker'a went in the disaster was complete. They were soon all out for three runs. Of the trio of runs, J. Gregsdn made one and Savage two. The Sankey bowling was re- markably good. Millington took •six wickets for one run and Forster took the other four at the expense of two runs. Savage, for Walker's,' placed seven wickets to his credit for 11 runs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0278" n="art0278">
        <head>EMMANUEL'S FINE WIN.</head>
        <p>EMMANUEL'S FINE WIN.</p>
        <p>Winwick Asylum C.C. met with-t.h.e-ir first de- feat on Saturday, on their own ground. Em- manuel Church, Warrington, inflicted a defeat by 11 runs. The visitors batted first, and were dismissed for 65. Winwick went in to what ap- peared a good win, but the batting was poor, only two men reaching double figures—Walker 22, and Cunningham 10. The Emmanuel team are to be complimented upon beating such formidable opponents. W. Knight knocked up • a splendid 23 before being unfortunately run out, and J. Bibby also batted well for his 18. Albert Hughes was at the top of his form with the ball, taking 5 wickets for 8 runs. Sam Hughes took 4 wickets for 23 run. The Emmanuel team have a very clever set of fielders, and to this alone they must attri- bute their fine win over the Asylum team.</p>
        <p>PADGATE WESLEYANS' DEFEAT. Cairo-street secured an easy win over Padgate Wesleyans in the Junior Cricket League. For Cairo-street Ellesmore and Mather were the successful bowlers. The former gave a good all- round exhibition, making the top score of 15 and securing five wickets for 4 runs, whilst the latter secured .four for 10. The Wesleyans did very badly in batting. but Rowlands bowled very well, obtaining five wickets for 9. runs.</p>
        <p>ELMWOOD AVENUE CONGREGATIONAL V. BOLD-STREET iNESLEYANS. Played dn Saturday, on the ground of the former at Manchester-road, and resulted in a win for Bold-street Wesleyans by 31 runs. The Wesleyani; batted first, and, after a bad start. reached a total of 41, L. W. Weaver being top scorer with 12. Elmwood Avenue, however, could do nothing against the bowling of the Wesleyans, and between them only just reached double figures (10), W. Stringer taking three wickets for four runs, and L. W. Weaver six wickets for five runs.</p>
        <p>_</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0279" n="art0279">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH WIN.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH WIN.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath were successful on Saturday in gaining the first win they have had, against Gossage's Recs. 2nd, at Appleton Park. The home team batted first, coming out for 41. Fred Moors carried his bat right through the innings, and although his score was not a large one (13), lie gave an exhibition of good batsmanship. Gossage's were successful in obtaining 36 runs, Stockton Heath thus winning by the narrow margin of 5 runs. Egerton again improved on his previous averages, taking 5 wickets for 21 runs. Knight also bowled well, having 5 wickets to his credit for 11 runs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0280" n="art0280">
        <head>FRIENDLY MATCHES. The details were : .</head>
