      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-23_WidnesExaminer_art0312" n="art0312">
        <head>Yesterday's Doings in Warring Schools.</head>
        <p>Yesterday's Doings in Warring Schools.</p>
        <p>FAIRFIELD GIRLS'.</p>
        <p>Empire Day was celebrated yesterdaY Technical Institute and Fairfield Schools. other schools will hold their festivities on day.</p>
        <p>At Fairfield Schools a concert was held, was attended by Alderman Bennett, Willis, Rev. J. M. Buckmaster, and other number of bright-faced girls dressed in cost characteristic of our wide-spreading dote represented the principal colonies which greeting to Britannia. Marie Bolswortil personated Britannia, whose immediate reil was formed by representatives of the ' countries, and each colony and depend' Songs and recitations told the story of , land's greatness and how she attained it. the lighter side of English history wail; duced by the advent of an old English 0, th' Green. This quaint personage intro' us to a typical English May day of three dred years ago. Britannia gave him CO" of the revels, and at his behest one coniP', merry maidens after another gave dances in old English costumes. In the vals between the dances old English song choruses whiled away the merry moment in conclusion the whole company salute.' flag. The entertainment had been well orga by Mrs. Stansfield, head mistress.</p>
        <p>TECHNICAL a nd EmpireSCHO OLSa.y; Alderman THE Bennett The proceedings in connection with the nical Institute were held in the Borough nasium, Mr. E. S. A. Robson, head master: siding. Many patriotic songs were sung 13! scholars, who had been trained by Mr. Baxter, one of the masters. In the course of an interesting addres° the "Uses of Empire Day." Alderman Bej said it was always a very great pleasur' him to go to the schools on Empire Day', which they could look in two or three waj made them feel proud of being English Englishwomen; but they ought rather gard it as a source of very grave respong° and set an example•of what Empire Dal„c to be. He sometimes thought that the V) Empire would fall liko other great emPirel, done; but its future depended to a large e' Upon the boys and girls. They were no vi through a very critical time; the older, would not be there for ever—and he solve, thought they had made a very great mess ' He would not decry any good English gara,e he thought the nation spent far too Taile`" on sport, and thus lost their seriousness., them make every possible sacrifice in ora build up the Empire still more. (API3II Mr. E. S. A. Robson thanked Alderniao,, nett for his speech, and alluded to methoa; ployed in other countries, to impress. 1° country upon those of other nationalities., During the afternoon recitations were by Muriel Critchley, Lily Weston and .45' Morralli Stanley Whitfield gave a solo style.</p>
      </div>
