        <p>LOCAL INTELLIGENCE.</p>
        <p>LYMM MAY QUEEN. Great Preparations Now in Progress. A GRAMMAR SCHOOL QUEEN. Great preparations are being made for the crowning of Lymm's May Queen—a festival which was so nearly lost. The queen-elect is Miss Dorothy Pearson, a pupil at the Grammar School, and the 16-year-old daughter of Mr. and Mrs. John Pearson, Higher-lane, Lymrn. The new committee, with all the strength and spirit of youth on its side, was at first faced with the most perplexing problem of how to "speed up" and perform three months work in the space of less than three weeks. Difficult as this problem was, their enthusiasm has paved the way to overcoming the obstacles. CHILDREN KEEN ON DANCING. The children who are to take part in the dances think of nothing else but "steps," and it is surprising what progress they have made</p>
        <p>, LIVE POLITICS. i Great Land Campaign to Begin. on Mcnday. #. BURDENS ON TRADESMEN. .. t: Preparing for Lloyd George's 1915 Budget. i The Liberals of Warrington will be especially busy the next two weeks. As will be seen from an advertisement on the front page, a great land campaign will begin next Monday evening at Bridge Foot, and will be continued each evening until June 10th. Home Rule, Welsh Disestablishreent, and the Plural Voting Bills will be placed on the Statute Book before the next General Election. Those questions are settled, and Liberals, leaving the final touches to be applied by the Premier and his colleagues, will concentrate upon the ques- tion of land reform, a question vitally affecting their rights. Some capital speakers are coming down thor- oughly to 'dace the whale question before the people of the borough.' We feel sure that both Tories and Laserals will, before the campaign has finished, agree that the settlement of the land question' has been too long delayed. Particularly does this question appeal to the tradesman whose security of tenure, compensation for improvements and goodwill, relief from restrictive conditions, fair rents, rents not increased on goodwill, or fix- provements, &amp;c., will be fully explained. There is no doubt that the Budget of Mr. Lloyd George of 1915 will be of a far-reaching charac- ter, as it will embody drastic proposals for clear- ing with the land question. One interesting feature of the campaign Ivill be a special meeting for ladies, to be held in the- Co-operative Hall on Thursday afternoon, June. 4th, at three o'clock. All are invited.</p>
